/* dives-glossary.js — the layered explanations behind the mini dives (dives.js).
   Each term walks in from the shore, one step at a time:
     s = SHORE     one short, plain sentence: the gist, for anyone.
     f = TOES      the next useful bit: how it shows up in real life, or a tiny example.
     w = SHALLOWS  what's inside the idea (grouping terms list their parts), how it
                   links to the rest of the program, and any caution the site gives.
     x = WAIST DEEP  a longer layer for the scrolling part of the wade-in: 3 to 5 short
                   paragraphs, separated by "\n" (the renderer splits on it). It goes one
                   step past the shallows without repeating them: what the term includes,
                   one gentle everyday example, and a last paragraph that starts "Try: ".
     u + l = DEEP  the page (and anchor) to dive all the way in, and the link's words.
   Other fields: t = title, m = phrases to spot in page text (the first one found on a
   page is marked), cs = match case exactly (used for acronyms). */
window.TOL_DIVES = {

  /* ---------- The book: Preface and Chapters I to V ---------- */

  unbilled: { t: 'Unbilled Debt', m: ['Unbilled Debt'],
    s: 'The everyday work of running a shared life that nobody sees or thanks.',
    f: 'Remembering the dentist, noticing the milk is low, smoothing a mood before it turns. It builds up like a debt only one person can see.',
    w: 'The other person usually isn’t ignoring it; they just don’t know it’s owed. The program’s answer isn’t keeping score. It’s making the invisible column visible to both of you, in the same units, at the same time. WP-01, the Lemonade Stand and the Deficit Audit are where that happens.',
    x: 'The Preface calls it a debt because it builds up quietly over months and years. The gap isn’t between two people. It’s between work that was done and work that was ever noticed.\nThis work is invisible by nature. When it’s done well, there’s nothing to see: the mood got smoothed before it turned. And people only say thank you for work they can see, so this kind never gets thanked.\nA small scene: Sunday, 9pm. One of you notices the laundry still in the washer, the forms due Tuesday, and the birthday card that never got mailed. They also notice nobody else has noticed. The house looks fine. That’s the problem, and it isn’t anyone’s fault.\nThe fix the book offers isn’t a bill for the past. It’s a shared page for the future: both columns written in the same units, read at the same time.\nTry: tonight, each of you write down three small things you noticed and handled today that the other might not have seen. Read them out, no replies needed.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#unbilled-debt', l: 'Read the Preface' },

  invisible: { t: 'Invisible work', m: ['invisible work', 'unseen work', 'the work nobody sees', 'invisible labor', 'emotional labor', 'mental load'],
    s: 'The noticing, remembering and planning that keep daily life running, mostly out of sight.',
    f: 'It never shows up on a calendar, so it rarely gets a thank-you. The person doing it often feels unseen; the other person often has no idea it exists.',
    w: 'This is what the Preface calls unbilled debt. It becomes visible when you write down a real week (WP-01), put hours side by side (the Lemonade Stand), and thank the specific thing someone did (Turning Toward). Naming it is about seeing the whole picture together, not winning.',
    x: 'Invisible work has three quiet parts. Noticing: the milk is low, the room has gone tense. Remembering: the dentist is in three weeks. Planning: what has to happen, in what order, before Friday.\nNone of it takes long on its own, which is why it’s so easy to miss. It adds up because it never stops. The person carrying it can feel tired without being able to point to one big job.\nAn everyday example: one person has already thought about lunches, the permission slip and a gift for Saturday before the other has finished their coffee. Nobody is being lazy. One head is simply holding the list, and the other can’t see it.\nWriting it down changes the conversation. “You never help” becomes “this list lives in my head, and I’d like to share it.” That’s a request the other person can actually say yes to.\nTry: for one day, jot every small “I noticed…” moment in your phone. At the end of the day, pick one and ask if the other person would like to own it.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#unbilled-debt', l: 'Read about unbilled debt' },

  ledger: { t: 'A shared ledger', m: ['shared ledger', 'working ledger', 'a set of books'],
    s: 'A written record, open to both of you, of what it takes to run your shared life.',
    f: 'It isn’t for winning arguments. It lets a hard talk start from shared facts instead of two different memories.',
    w: 'The book borrows words from accounting because accounting describes a system without blaming anyone. A ledger that’s out of balance isn’t evil; it’s information. The goal isn’t a perfect 50/50 split, but a home where both people can see the whole picture.',
    x: 'In the book, a ledger is simply a record both people can open. It says what happened, in plain terms, before anyone decides what it means. That’s an auditor’s habit, turned gently toward home.\nDouble-entry bookkeeping has a quiet idea behind it: every entry has two sides. A book that only balances from one direction isn’t balanced at all. So each person fills in their own column, then you read both together.\nAn everyday example: two people disagree about how much time the school run takes. Instead of arguing from memory, they each note it for a week. On Sunday the numbers are just there, and the talk starts from the same page.\nThe ledger in this program is made of a few simple pages: the weekly “who did what” log (WP-01), the Lemonade Stand totals, and the RACI Treaty (WP-03) that names who owns each regular job.\nTry: pick one shared job this week and both write down when it happened and who did it. Look at it together on the weekend, with curiosity rather than a verdict.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#not-scorekeeping', l: 'Why it isn’t scorekeeping' },

  scorekeeping: { t: 'Not keeping score', m: ['grudge with better formatting', 'scorekeeping', 'keeping score'],
    s: 'Writing things down here is the opposite of keeping score to win.',
    f: 'The Preface puts it plainly: a ledger only one person gets to read isn’t a ledger, it’s a grudge with better formatting.',
    w: 'Every tool is filled in by each person about themselves, then read together. If a number is ever used to end a conversation instead of start one, it’s being misused. When you notice yourself wanting to win, the site suggests stepping back to the Calm-Down Kit.',
    x: 'Keeping score is private and one-sided. It collects evidence for the next argument. A ledger is the reverse: it’s shared, both people add to it, and it’s read out loud to start a calmer talk.\nA simple test helps. If a number or note is being used to close a conversation, like “well, the page says…”, it has slipped into scorekeeping. If it’s being used to open one, like “what do you make of this?”, it’s doing its job.\nA small scene: one person has quietly kept a list of every time the trash was missed. They could bring it out as proof. Instead they say, “I think the trash might not have a clear owner. Can we look at that?” Same facts, very different evening.\nThe book also says the goal isn’t a perfect 50/50 split to the decimal. It’s a home where nobody is carrying something the other can’t see.\nTry: next time you feel the urge to list what you’ve done, pause and swap it for one question about how the week felt for the other person.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#not-scorekeeping', l: 'Read the Preface' },

  radio: { t: 'The Radio Frequency Paradigm', m: ['Radio Frequency Paradigm', 'Radio Frequency'],
    s: 'Two kind people can clash like two radios slightly out of tune.',
    f: 'Neither radio is broken, but together they make a squeal neither was making alone. Pace, tone and urgency are what knock you out of tune.',
    w: 'It’s a picture, not a measurement: nothing records or scores anyone’s voice. It gives you a shared way to say “we’re out of sync” without anyone carrying the whole blame. The fix is retuning: name it, check your state, slow down, then go back to the topic. WP-09 and the Carrier Wave Decoder put it into practice.',
    x: 'The picture comes from physics. Tune two radios close to each other, but not quite the same, and you don’t get silence. You get a squeal: a third sound neither radio was sending.\nChapter I uses this for a familiar moment. Two people each say something reasonable, in a tone shaped by their own tiredness, urgency or stress, and a fight appears that neither meant to start. The words weren’t the problem. The mismatch was.\nWhat sets each person’s “frequency” is mostly pace, register (the tone under the words) and urgency. How stressed or calm your body is shifts all three, often without you noticing.\nAn everyday example: “I said I’d do it.” “I know, I’m just asking when.” Four sentences later, nobody is talking about the trash. Neither person is broken; they’re just out of tune.\nTry: agree on a short, shared phrase like “different frequencies?” that either of you can say, kindly, when a talk starts to turn sharp.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#frequency', l: 'Read Chapter I' },

  squeal: { t: 'The squeal', m: ['heterodyne squeal', 'heterodyne', 'squeal'],
    s: 'The ugly noise two nearly matched signals make together, like a talk that suddenly turns sharp.',
    f: 'Four sentences in, nobody is talking about the trash anymore, and neither of you can point to the moment it turned.',
    w: 'Engineers call it a heterodyne: a third sound neither radio was sending. The book uses it for fights nobody meant to start. The words weren’t the problem; the mismatch was. The answer isn’t deciding who’s at fault, it’s retuning.',
    x: 'Engineers call it a heterodyne, or beat frequency: the sound two nearly-matched signals make when they overlap just enough to interfere. It’s loud, it’s unpleasant, and it doesn’t belong to either radio.\nThat’s why the squeal is so confusing in a conversation. Each person can replay what they said and find it reasonable. The sharpness came from the gap between you, not from one mouth.\nA small scene: a Tuesday evening. One of you is tired and brief. The other is anxious and asks again, a little faster. The brief answer sounds cold; the second question sounds like nagging. By the third line, it’s about respect, not the trash.\nChapter I is clear that arguing about who is “really” at fault doesn’t clear the interference. Retuning does: name it, check your state, slow the pace, then go back to the topic.\nTry: the next time you can’t remember when a talk turned, treat that as a sign of a squeal. Say so out loud, and take a slow pause before the next sentence.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#squeal', l: 'Read about the squeal' },

  static: { t: 'Static', m: ['relational static', 'static between', 'heavy static'],
    s: 'The noise that happens in the gap between two people, not inside either one.',
    f: 'You say “in a minute,” meaning when I finish this. They hear “sixty seconds.” Nobody lied, and now you’re arguing about tone instead of the dishes.',
    w: 'Nobody is static-free on their own. What you can do is know your own signal well enough to say it out loud: your pace, what your silence means, what you need when you’re low. Wired Differently, the Wiring Card and the Signal Translator all help you find static before it becomes a fight.',
    x: 'Static is a “between” word. It doesn’t describe either person. It describes the space where one person’s message leaves on one frequency and lands on another.\nThat’s why no one can fix static alone by trying to be nicer. What each person can do is know their own signal: how fast they move, what their silence usually means, what they need when they’re running low. Said out loud, those things stop being invisible.\nAn everyday example: you go quiet after work. To you, that means “I’m recharging.” To the other person, it can look like “I’m upset with you.” One sentence, like “quiet after work is just me resetting”, can remove a whole week of worry.\nThe program’s self-discovery pages exist for this. Today’s Weather, Wired Differently, the Wiring Card and the Signal Translator each help you describe your side of the gap.\nTry: finish this sentence and share it: “When I go quiet, it usually means…”. Then ask the other person to finish it for themselves.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#static', l: 'Where static comes from' },

  channels: { t: 'Pace, register and urgency', m: ['pace, register and urgency', 'pace, register, urgency', 'pace, register or urgency', 'pace, tone and urgency'],
    s: 'The three things that quietly shift how a message is sent and heard.',
    f: 'One person’s “when you get a chance” means tonight; another’s means sometime this week. Same words, different window.',
    w: 'Pace is speed and timing: a stressed person talks faster and reads pauses as stonewalling. Register is the tone underneath: a status update can land as an accusation. Urgency is how soon something matters. Each wiring is more or less sensitive on each channel, and most mismatches happen on one of them.',
    x: 'Pace is speed and timing. When your body is revved up, you talk faster, interrupt sooner, and a normal pause can feel like being ignored.\nRegister is the tone underneath the words. “The sink’s full again” can be sent as a simple update and still arrive as an accusation, depending on the tone and the day.\nUrgency is how soon something matters. Two people can use the exact same phrase, like “when you get a chance”, and mean two very different windows of time.\nAn everyday example: one person asks, “Did you call the plumber?” They meant it as a gentle check. Said quickly, on a tired evening, with “today” in their head, it lands as pressure. Neither person did anything wrong; the channels just didn’t line up. Chapter I is clear that none of this needs anyone to be wrong. Naming which channel slipped makes the mismatch smaller and easier to fix.\nTry: when a request feels loaded, ask one channel question, like “by when do you need this?”, before you answer anything else.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#frequency', l: 'Read Chapter I' },

  retune: { t: 'Retuning', m: ['retuning', 'retune'],
    s: 'Getting back in sync when a conversation starts to squeal, before you carry on.',
    f: 'Say it without blame: “I think we’re on different frequencies right now.” Then slow everything down.',
    w: 'The four steps: name the mismatch out loud; check your own state on the ladder (you can’t retune while revved up or shut down); slow the pace with shorter turns, more pauses and lower volume; only then go back to the actual topic. WP-09 is the written version, and CALC-01 counts how often it happens.',
    x: 'Retuning comes before content. The idea is simple: you can’t solve the actual problem while the talk is still squealing, so you clear the interference first.\nChecking your own state matters because a revved-up or shut-down body can’t tune accurately. The Nervous System Ladder gives you three words for it: safe and social, mobilized, or shut down.\nSlowing down is physical, not just a promise. Shorter turns. Longer pauses. A lower voice. It can feel odd at first, and that’s fine.\nA small scene: a question about the weekend starts to turn tense. One person says, “I think we’re on different frequencies right now.” Both take a breath, sit down, and the next few sentences are shorter. Five minutes later, the weekend gets planned. The Tone Filter (WP-09) is the written version of these steps. How often you retune also counts, as a small, honest tally in the apex score.\nTry: practice saying the naming line once on a calm day, so it’s ready when you need it.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#retuning', l: 'Read about retuning' },

  verdict: { t: 'The Epistemic Verdict Engine', m: ['Epistemic Verdict Engine', 'Verdict Engine'],
    s: 'A calm question: judging by what you both wrote down, can the way you share the work last?',
    f: 'Most “is this working?” questions get answered by last night’s dishes. This one looks at the month’s written record instead.',
    w: 'It reads three things you already fill in: hours from the Lemonade Stand, tasks with a named owner from the RACI Treaty, and each person’s own stress reading. It scores the arrangement, never a person. “Epistemic” just means it cares about what you actually know, not what you remember in the heat of the moment.',
    x: 'The word “verdict” sounds stern, but the question is narrow and calm. It doesn’t ask whether the relationship is working. It asks whether the current way of sharing the work looks like it can last, on paper.\nChapter II compares it to a small business cash-flow snapshot. That tells you about this month’s operations, not whether the owners are good people. The same is true here.\nIt uses only three things you both fill in: hours from the Lemonade Stand, jobs with a named owner from the RACI Treaty (WP-03), and each person’s own stress reading from WP-02. It never uses tone of voice, private thoughts or how anyone talks.\nAn everyday example: after a rough week, one person feels “nothing ever changes.” The written month shows ownership got clearer and hours evened out. The feeling is real, and so is the progress. Both can be said.\nTry: next time you ask “is this working?”, add “over the last month, on paper?” and see if the answer changes.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'Read Chapter II' },

  solvency: { t: 'Can the load last? (CALC-01)', m: ['P(Solvency)', 'Solvency Read', 'solvency score', 'Can the load last?', 'CALC-01'],
    s: 'A number from 0 to 1 on whether your current split of work looks like it can last.',
    f: 'Above 0.7 is holding, 0.4 to 0.7 is drifting, and below 0.4 asks for a kind rethink of the arrangement.',
    w: 'It weighs three inputs: workload balance (0.40), ownership clarity (0.35) and how much stress you’re already carrying, turned upside down (0.25). It reads the setup, never a person, and it isn’t a compatibility score. The word “probability” in its name is only a label, not a prediction. If it’s used to end a talk, it’s being misused.',
    x: 'CALC-01 builds each input from raw entries rather than a guess. You enter rough hours, list jobs with their owners, and each fill in your own five stress factors. The page does the sums, because a hand-picked score is just a mood with a decimal point.\nIt runs in your browser, and nothing on the page is saved or sent. Closing the tab clears it, so print or screenshot a read if you want to keep it. A few special cases are handled on purpose. No hours logged gives no reading, not a perfect score. If only one person has filled in their stress column, no read is made at all.\nAn everyday example: a couple gets a 0.55. Instead of worrying about the number, they look at the shortfall bars. The biggest gap is ownership clarity, so their next step is simple: name owners for three jobs that nobody holds.\nIt’s a rule-of-thumb worksheet, not a proven statistical model or a compatibility test, and it can’t say whether a relationship is worth continuing.\nTry: after a read, look at the largest shortfall bar rather than the total, and pick one small change for that input.',
    u: '/workpapers/calculators/calc01-solvency.html', l: 'Open the calculator' },

  inputs: { t: 'The three inputs', m: ['three ledger inputs', 'three inputs'],
    s: 'The three written-down things the solvency read is built from.',
    f: 'Each one comes from a worksheet you’ve already filled in, never from a mood in the moment.',
    w: 'The three: workload balance (how evenly visible hours are shared, from WP-01 and the Lemonade Stand), ownership clarity (how many regular jobs have a clear owner, from WP-03), and autonomic saturation (how much stress each of you is already carrying, from WP-02). When a read is drifting, ownership clarity is usually the first thing to slip.',
    x: 'Each input is scored from 0 to 1, from the worksheets rather than from a feeling in the moment. That keeps the read steady, even on a hard night.\nIt includes: workload balance (how evenly the visible hours were shared, from WP-01 and the Lemonade Stand); ownership clarity (how many regular jobs have both a named Responsible and Accountable person, from WP-03); and autonomic saturation (how much stress each of you is already carrying, from WP-02).\nBalance counts most, clarity next, and stress least, because stress swings most from week to week. The site says these weights are judgment calls, not numbers worked out from data.\nAn everyday example: a week feels awful. The inputs show hours were fairly even and owners were clear, but both stress readings were high. That points to a hard season, not a broken arrangement.\nTry: before your next weekly closing, gather the three pages in one place so the read takes minutes instead of an evening.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#inputs', l: 'Read about the inputs' },

  balance: { t: 'Workload balance', m: ['workload balance'],
    s: 'How evenly the visible hours are shared between you.',
    f: 'An even split scores 1.00; a fully one-sided week scores 0.00. It doesn’t matter which person carries more.',
    w: 'It comes from your week of logs (WP-01) or the Lemonade Stand totals, and it’s the most heavily weighted of the three inputs to CALC-01, because an unshared load keeps building whether or not anyone notices. An empty log isn’t a perfect week; it simply gives no reading.',
    x: 'The sum is simple: take the difference between each person’s share of the hours, and subtract it from one. A 50/50 week scores 1.00. A 100/0 week scores 0.00. It doesn’t care which person carries more.\nRough hours are fine. CALC-01 says counting to the minute would only look precise; the split is what matters.\nThe weight is highest because an unshared load keeps building whether or not anyone notices, and it’s the easiest of the three to see and check.\nAn everyday example: one person feels the week was even. The log shows twelve hours to four. Nobody was lying. From inside each person’s own week, it did feel even. The page just lets you see both weeks at once. Balance doesn’t mean every job is split in half. It means the total load is shared in a way you both agreed to.\nTry: this week, each of you log rough minutes for household jobs in WP-01, then add them into the Lemonade Stand together on Sunday.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#inputs', l: 'Read about the inputs' },

  clarity: { t: 'Ownership clarity', m: ['Ownership Clarity Score', 'ownership clarity'],
    s: 'How many of your regular jobs have a clear, agreed owner.',
    f: 'Count the jobs with both names filled in, then divide by all the jobs listed.',
    w: 'It comes from the RACI Treaty (WP-03) and feeds CALC-01. It measures how clear ownership is, not how fair it is. Don’t shorten the list to raise it: a real job nobody owns belongs on the list, because that’s the row the score is there to show.',
    x: 'Responsible means the person who does the job. Accountable means the person who notices if it didn’t happen and follows up. They can be the same person. A row only counts when both names are filled in.\nThe score is simple: count the rows with both names, then divide by all the rows listed. It feeds CALC-01 with the second-largest weight, because unclear ownership turns a fixable imbalance into a recurring one.\nIt measures clarity, not fairness. A table can be fully clear and still feel lopsided; that’s a separate talk, and the treaty’s amendment process is where it happens.\nAn everyday example: a couple lists ten jobs and finds three with no Accountable name, including “birthday cards.” They add those names. Their score rises, and so does the chance the cards get mailed.\nTry: add one job to your treaty that currently lives in nobody’s column. Leave it blank if you must. Seeing the gap is the first step.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#clarity-score', l: 'How it’s worked out' },

  apex: { t: 'The apex score', m: ['apex score'],
    s: 'A second read that also asks whether you’re repairing after friction, not just sharing the work.',
    f: 'A home can share work evenly and still never repair after a sharp moment. The apex score can show that when solvency can’t.',
    w: 'It adds a fourth input, retuning frequency, to the three solvency inputs. When the two scores disagree, that’s the useful part: solvency high and apex low means friction is being swallowed, not talked through (WP-09 is the lever). Solvency low and apex higher means good talking is holding up an arrangement that needs fixing (the treaty is the lever).',
    x: 'The apex score uses the same three inputs as solvency, plus retuning frequency. The weights shift a little to make room: balance 0.35, clarity 0.30, stress 0.20, and retuning 0.15.\nSolvency asks one narrow question: can this week’s workload last as it’s set up? The apex score asks a wider one: is the whole system working, including repair after friction?\nWhen both are high, keep going. When both are low, CALC-01 says a single conversation probably won’t be enough, and it may be worth considering outside help rather than another worksheet.\nAn everyday example: a couple shares jobs well and scores high on solvency, but sharp moments keep getting swallowed and never talked about. The apex score dips. That dip isn’t a failure. It’s a gentle pointer toward the Tone Filter (WP-09).\nTry: when your two scores differ, read the gap first. It usually tells you which lever to reach for this week.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#read', l: 'See both scores' },

  rf: { t: 'Retuning frequency', m: ['Retuning Frequency'],
    s: 'Out of this week’s rough moments, how many you paused and retuned before answering.',
    f: 'Count the moments something landed badly, then how many you ran through WP-09 first.',
    w: 'It’s the one input that measures what you did about friction, not how much there was. No friction events means no reading at all, not a zero: nothing to repair isn’t the same as failing to repair. It only feeds the apex score, with a small weight, because it’s the easiest tally to inflate.',
    x: 'A friction event is any moment this week where something landed badly enough to notice. A retune is when you paused and ran it through the Tone Filter (WP-09) before answering. The tally is retunes divided by friction events.\nIf you somehow count more retunes than friction events, CALC-01 caps it at 1.00, because more repairs than problems means the tally is counting something else.\nThe weight is small on purpose. It’s a tally rather than a measurement, and it’s the input most easily inflated by the person filling it in. Honest counting matters more than a good number.\nAn everyday example: five moments stung this week. In two, one of you said “give me a second” and used the fact, feeling, ask shape. That’s two out of five, and it’s a real start.\nTry: keep a tiny tally on your phone this week: one mark for each moment that stung, and a circle around it when you paused first.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#rf', l: 'Read about the tally' },

  bands: { t: 'Reading the bands', m: ['Interpretation bands'],
    s: 'The three ranges a solvency read can fall into. Treat them as rough zones, not strict lines.',
    f: '0.70 and up: change nothing structural. 0.40 to 0.69: look at the biggest shortfall. Under 0.40: rethink the arrangement, not the person.',
    w: 'The boundaries are round numbers chosen to be easy to read. A 0.69 and a 0.70 are the same week, so don’t let a decimal point carry a decision. Arrangements get renegotiated all the time; a low band is a statement about the setup, not anyone’s love or worth.',
    x: 'Each band comes with a first move, so the number points somewhere useful rather than just sitting there.\nIt includes: 0.70 and up (the arrangement is carrying its own weight; keep the same rhythm of check-ins); 0.40 to 0.69 (something is drifting; go to the shortfall bar with the largest gap, which is often ownership clarity, but read it rather than assume); under 0.40 (not sustainable as set up; a full treaty renegotiation, not another quick patch).\nAn everyday example: a couple sees 0.68 one week and 0.71 the next. They almost celebrate, then remember that the bands are rough zones, not strict lines. Nothing big changed. They keep doing what they were doing.\nThe bands describe one week’s workload setup. They say nothing about whether a relationship is good or worth continuing.\nTry: when you read a score, say the band’s first move out loud, and skip talking about the decimal.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#spec', l: 'See the bands' },

  driftrule: { t: 'The drift rule', m: ['drift rule', 'treaty review'],
    s: 'Three weekly drops in a row is a cue for a calm treaty review, not an emergency.',
    f: 'Specifically: three consecutive drops of more than 0.10 in the apex score.',
    w: 'A single big drop is usually just a week, not a pattern. The trend log in CALC-01 and REPORT-01 watch for this and say so when it happens. The response is to look at the RACI Treaty together, not to hold a crisis talk.',
    x: 'The rule looks for a pattern, not a bad moment: three weekly drops in a row, each bigger than 0.10, in the apex score. One sharp drop is usually just a week.\nThe trend log on the CALC-01 page and REPORT-01 watch for it. Remember that CALC-01 saves nothing, so keep a printed or screenshotted log if you want to see a trend.\nWhen the rule is met, the response is calm and practical: look at the RACI Treaty together, see which jobs have shifted, and write any changes into the table.\nAn everyday example: a new job starts, and over three weeks the scores slide. Nobody is failing. The school pick-up quietly moved to one person and was never written down. A short treaty review fixes the table, not the people.\nTry: set a regular spot, like the first Sunday of the month, to glance at your trend together, so a drift gets noticed early and gently.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#trend', l: 'See the trend log' },

  saturation: { t: 'Autonomic Saturation', m: ['Autonomic Saturation', 'autonomic load'],
    s: 'How much of your stress right now was already there before this moment.',
    f: 'When your tank is already full, a small question can get a big reaction. That isn’t a flaw; it’s a full tank.',
    w: 'It separates “I’m upset about the dishes” from “I’m exhausted and the dishes are what I noticed.” A high reading isn’t an excuse or a diagnosis; it’s a note that whatever gets decided now should probably be revisited later. WP-02 turns it into a number, and it’s one of the three CALC-01 inputs.',
    x: 'The short version is the Nervous System Ladder: safe and social, mobilized, or shut down. The fuller version in WP-02 breaks it into a few self-rated factors, like sleep, workload elsewhere, recent conflict and how your body feels.\nIn CALC-01 it enters the sum turned upside down, as one minus the reading. High stress lowers the read, because a workload that only works when both people are rested isn’t a workload that works.\nA small scene: Thursday, 5:15pm. A pleasant question about the dentist gets a reaction three sizes too big, and you can hear it happening. Nothing about the question changed. The tank was already full.\nChapter III says a high reading means that anything decided in the next ten minutes should probably be revisited later, when things are calmer.\nTry: before a talk about something that matters, each of you say one word for your own state. If either word is high, agree to pick a better time.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#saturation', l: 'Read Chapter III' },

  baseline: { t: 'Baseline stress', m: ['baseline stress'],
    s: 'The stress you carry in from the rest of life, before the household even comes up.',
    f: 'Work, sleep, money or health can raise it. Then a normal chore talk feels much heavier than it is.',
    w: 'The program keeps it separate from workload so one doesn’t get blamed for the other. It enters CALC-01 through WP-02, with the lowest weight, because it swings the most week to week and a single bad-sleep week shouldn’t dominate the read.',
    x: 'Baseline stress is what you bring through the door: a hard day at work, a poor night’s sleep, money worries, a health scare. The household didn’t cause it, but the household is where it often shows up.\nKeeping it separate protects both people. Without that line, “I’m exhausted” can sound like “you’re too much,” and a heavy week at work can look like a problem with the chores.\nAn everyday example: a parent is caring for their own sick mother. Their chore hours dip and their fuse gets shorter. Seen as baseline stress, that’s a season to plan around together, not a sign they care less.\nThe How It Works page names life’s big transitions: a new baby, a job change, a move, an illness, caring for a parent, or grief. They change how much you can hold, often before you notice.\nTry: at your weekly closing, each of you name one thing from outside the home that weighed on you this week. Just name it. Nothing needs fixing.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#inputs', l: 'Read about the inputs' },

  selfreport: { t: 'Self-report', m: ['self-report', 'self-rated', 'gut-check'],
    s: 'You fill it in about yourself, and only yourself.',
    f: 'Nobody rates the other person’s stress, tiredness or mood. You say what’s true for you.',
    w: 'That rule keeps a reflection tool from turning into a surveillance tool. No self-report is perfectly neutral, since your mood comes along with it. What makes it trustworthy enough to act on is repetition: the same questions, week after week, with earlier answers beside today’s.',
    x: 'Self-report is the backbone of the whole program. Every stress reading, battery score and state check is filled in by the person it describes, and no one else.\nThe site is honest that this isn’t perfectly neutral. You fill it in, so your mood comes along with it. That’s why the program leans on repetition: one entry is a mood; twelve entries are a pattern that memory alone can’t show you. It also means the other person’s answer is theirs to give. You can ask, and you can listen, but you don’t get to correct it.\nAn everyday example: one person rates their battery low for three weeks running. The other had assumed they were fine. Nobody guessed and nobody accused. The page simply let the tiredness be said.\nThis is a practical rule of thumb, not a clinical or diagnostic tool.\nTry: answer the same short check, like Today’s Weather, at the same time of day for a week, and look at the entries side by side.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'Read the honest note' },

  surveillance: { t: 'Reflection, not surveillance', m: ['surveillance tool'],
    s: 'These tools are for looking at yourself, never for watching someone else.',
    f: 'Guessing someone else’s stress score on their behalf turns a reflection tool into a surveillance tool.',
    w: 'So each person fills in their own column. CALC-01 refuses to give a read from one person’s inputs alone, and nothing on the site takes tone of voice, recordings, message sentiment or reply times as an input.',
    x: 'Chapter III draws a firm line. Both tools in that chapter are self-report, filled in by the person they describe. Filling one in about someone else turns reflection into watching.\nCALC-01 backs that up in practice. It won’t make a read from one person’s stress column alone. It keeps nothing after the tab is closed, because a saved record of one person’s stress scores on a shared computer is no longer just a reflection tool.\nThe site also lists what it will never take as an input: tone of voice, recordings, message sentiment, reply times, or anything about a person that they didn’t enter themselves.\nAn everyday example: one person is tempted to fill in the other’s tiredness score “to save time.” Instead they hand over the page and say, “Only you know this bit.”\nTry: if you ever catch yourself guessing how the other person “really” feels, turn it into a question you ask them, and accept the answer they give.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#saturation', l: 'Read Chapter III' },

  vectors: { t: 'The 7 Ocular Vectors', m: ['7 Ocular Vectors', 'Ocular Vectors', 'seven angles', 'seven vantage points', 'vantage point'],
    s: 'Seven different angles from which people can see the same moment.',
    f: 'Most arguments are two people looking from different angles, each assuming the other sees what they see.',
    w: 'The seven: physical fatigue, time scarcity, historical pattern, family script, fairness perception, communication style and external stressors. Saying yours before a hard talk stops you both guessing. Saturation asks how much charge is in the room; the vectors ask what each person thinks the room is about.',
    x: '“Ocular” is used loosely here to mean vantage point: the angle you’re looking from. Two people can both be right about what they see and still be looking at different sides of the same event.\nIt includes: physical fatigue (how rested you really are); time scarcity (whose calendar is fuller this week); historical pattern (whether this has come up before); family script (what normal looked like growing up); fairness perception (whether the split feels fair, apart from the hours); communication style (direct or indirect); and external stressors (money, work, health).\nAn everyday example: one person sees an unwashed pan as a small thing on a busy night. The other sees the fourth time this month. Both are true. One is looking through time scarcity, the other through historical pattern.\nChapter III suggests using them before a talk rather than during one, so you say your angle instead of both guessing.\nTry: before your next chore talk, each pick the one vector you’re looking through most right now and say it in a sentence.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#vectors', l: 'See all seven' },

  familyscript: { t: 'Family script', m: ['Family script'],
    s: 'What “normal” looked like in the home you grew up in.',
    f: 'If dishes were done straight after dinner growing up, a sink left overnight can feel wrong before you’ve thought about it.',
    w: 'It’s one of the seven ocular vectors. It isn’t right or wrong, just an angle. Naming it out loud (“in my house, this was always done by nine”) turns a clash about character back into a clash about habits.',
    x: 'Everyone brings a picture of “how homes work” from the one they grew up in. It covers timing, tidiness, who cooks, how money is talked about, and how loud a disagreement is allowed to get.\nMost of it runs below thinking. That’s why a family script can make something feel wrong before you’ve had a chance to decide whether it is wrong.\nAn everyday example: in one home, the tree came down on New Year’s Day. In the other, it stayed up until mid-January. In early January, one person feels the house is untidy, and the other feels rushed. Neither is being difficult; two scripts are meeting.\nOnce each script is said out loud, you can choose together which parts to keep, which to mix, and which to let go. That’s a conversation about habits, not about who is a good person.\nTry: each finish this line and share it: “In my house growing up, it was normal to…”. Listen for one thing that explains a small, repeated friction.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#vectors', l: 'See all seven' },

  parity: { t: 'Deontological Parity', m: ['Deontological Parity', 'two kinds of fair'],
    s: 'Once a job has a named owner, you ask whether the owner did it.',
    f: 'Not whether it got done anyway because someone else quietly covered for them.',
    w: 'It protects the person who keeps filling the gaps, so their covering isn’t used as proof the gap doesn’t matter. It needs a named owner first, which is what the RACI Treaty (WP-03) gives you. “Deontological” just means judging by duties rather than by outcomes.',
    x: 'Chapter IV notes that most chore arguments are fairness arguments “wearing a dish towel.” Parity is a simple agreement about which kind of fair you use for owned jobs.\nUnder this agreement, a job with a named owner in the RACI Treaty is judged on one thing: did the owner do their part? If someone else quietly covered it, that’s noticed and appreciated, but it doesn’t count as the owner’s job being done.\nThe About page adds a second meaning: each person’s needs carry equal weight as a starting point, not something earned by doing more.\nAn everyday example: the recycling belongs to one person. For three weeks, the other took it out so it wouldn’t pile up. Under parity, the talk isn’t “it got done, so it’s fine.” It’s “this one keeps sliding; do we need to change the owner or the timing?”\nTry: pick one job where someone often covers for the owner, and talk about whether the owner, the timing or the job itself needs to change.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#parity', l: 'Read Chapter IV' },

  twofair: { t: 'Outcome-based or duty-based fair', m: ['Outcome-based', 'Duty-based', 'consequentialist'],
    s: 'Two honest ways to judge fairness: did it get done, or did each person do their part?',
    f: '“It all got done, who cares who did it” is outcome-based. “You said you’d handle the trash” is duty-based.',
    w: 'Both are reasonable. Friction shows up when each person uses a different one without saying so. A household needs to agree which it’s using, the way a contract names its governing law. The book’s choice for owned jobs is duty-based: deontological parity.',
    x: 'Outcome-based fair asks whether the right things got done, by whoever had time or energy, however the week worked out. It’s flexible, and it suits busy, changing weeks.\nDuty-based fair asks whether each person did the things they agreed to own, however the week went. It’s steady, and it protects the person who keeps filling gaps.\nChapter IV compares the choice to a contract naming its governing law. Neither is wrong, but mixing them without saying so is where the friction comes from.\nA small scene: the same argument, twice a month. One person counts hours. The other counts who noticed the job needed doing. Both are keeping score honestly, in two different currencies, and then arguing about the exchange rate. Many homes use both: duty-based for owned jobs, outcome-based for the rest.\nTry: ask each other, “When a job gets done by the wrong person, does that feel fine to you, or does it bother you?” The answers show which kind of fair each of you uses.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#parity', l: 'Read Chapter IV' },

  gating: { t: 'Sensory Gating', m: ['Sensory Gating'],
    s: 'Letting a comment land before you react to it.',
    f: 'Say “give me a second,” let the spike pass, then decide if it needs an answer now, later, or not at all.',
    w: 'The three moves: notice it landed harder than its content deserved; name that out loud; decide once you’ve settled. It’s about timing, not bottling things up. Paired with parity, it separates “this job wasn’t done” (a fact) from “this landed as an attack” (a reaction). The site also treats light, noise and scent as part of it: choosing the room is part of the method.',
    x: 'The term is borrowed loosely from neuroscience, where it describes the brain filtering out repeated or low-priority input so attention isn’t swamped. Here it’s a practical habit, not a clinical claim: not every comment has to be fully processed the moment it’s said.\nIt matters most when you’re mobilized or shutting down. In those states, a neutral comment can arrive at full volume and seem to demand an answer right now.\nA small scene: “Did you move my keys?” lands like an accusation after a long day. Instead of snapping back, one person says, “I heard that as bigger than it probably was. Give me a second.” A minute later, the keys are found, and nothing needed defending.\nThe room counts too. The site treats light, noise and scent as real inputs that change how much either person can take in. Choosing where and when to talk is part of the method.\nTry: pick your own version of “give me a second” and use it once this week, even on something small.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#gating', l: 'Read about gating' },

  deficit: { t: 'The Deficit Audit (WP-04)', m: ['Unbilled Deficit Audit', 'Deficit Audit', 'monthly look-back', 'monthly reconciliation'],
    s: 'A monthly look back to find the jobs that keep slipping.',
    f: 'A job missed once is an event. The same job missed four weeks running is a pattern, and patterns hide at the weekly scale.',
    w: 'Three parts: pull the month’s logs; sort each repeat into a structural gap, a capacity issue or a one-off; then close the month by fixing the structure. It isn’t a performance review and it doesn’t bill backward for past hours. A falling count of structural gaps is the clearest sign things are working.',
    x: 'Part A pulls every job from the four weekly logs that was noticed but not done, or done only after being raised more than once. A job flagged three or four times in a month is a real deficit, not a fluke.\nPart B sorts each job flagged twice or more into one of three kinds: a structural gap (no clear owner, or the owner isn’t the one doing it); a capacity issue (the owner keeps being unable to keep up); and a one-off (a clear temporary cause, so no action).\nPart C closes the month. Update the treaty for each gap, schedule a talk for each capacity issue, and write down how many gaps you found.\nAn everyday example: “the trash again,” for the fourth Tuesday running. Instead of a fifth row about Tuesday, the audit asks what it is about Tuesdays.\nTry: at the end of this month, circle any job that showed up in your logs more than twice, and bring just those to the audit.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'Dive into the Deficit Audit' },

  structgap: { t: 'A structural gap', m: ['structural gap'],
    s: 'A job that keeps slipping because nobody clearly owns it.',
    f: 'Or the named owner isn’t the person actually doing it in practice. “The trash has quietly become no one’s job.”',
    w: 'It’s one of the three kinds of repeat problem in the Deficit Audit. The fix is to update the RACI Treaty that same session, not to try harder. Gaps left open cost the most over time, because the same job gets argued over from scratch every time it comes back.',
    x: 'A structural gap has two common shapes. Either the job has no clear owner in the treaty, or it has one on paper while someone else actually does it. Both mean the table and real life have drifted apart.\nTrying harder doesn’t fix it, because the problem isn’t effort. The fix is to write a real owner into the RACI Treaty during the same audit, so the job stops getting renegotiated from scratch.\nAn everyday example: the treaty says one person handles the car, but the other always ends up booking the service because they notice the warning light first. The gap isn’t a character flaw. It’s a row that needs updating to match reality, or a clearer handover.\nWP-04 says a falling count of structural gaps month over month is the clearest sign the framework is working. A rising count may mean something bigger changed and needs its own conversation.\nTry: look at one job you both do “sometimes” and write one clear owner beside it tonight.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'Read the three kinds' },

  capacityissue: { t: 'A capacity issue', m: ['capacity issue', 'capacity conversation'],
    s: 'A job has an owner, but that person keeps being unable to keep up with it.',
    f: 'The fix is a direct, kind conversation, not a private promise to just try harder.',
    w: 'It’s one of the three kinds of repeat problem in the Deficit Audit. The audit only brings it to light; the talk still has to happen. Use a neutral refusal or a reassignment (WP-01, WP-09), and hold the talk as a check-in.',
    x: 'A capacity issue isn’t about ownership being unclear. The owner is named and agreed. They just can’t keep up with it, again and again, for reasons that are real.\nThe worst fix is a silent promise to try harder, because willpower collapses exactly when you’re most depleted. The audit points out the issue; the talk is what solves it.\nThat talk can use a neutral refusal from WP-01: acknowledge the job is real, say your capacity honestly, and offer something concrete instead, like a different time, a swap, or a smaller version.\nAn everyday example: one person owns cooking, but a new job means they get home late four nights a week. Instead of apologizing every night, they say, “I can’t keep weeknight dinners right now. Can I take weekends and the Sunday shop instead?”\nTry: if one of your jobs keeps slipping, write the three-part neutral refusal for it before the next check-in, so the talk starts calm.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'Read the three kinds' },

  oneoff: { t: 'A one-off', m: ['One-off, no action', 'one-off misses'],
    s: 'A miss with a clear temporary cause, like illness, travel or a busy work month.',
    f: 'Note it and move on.',
    w: 'It’s the third kind of repeat problem in the Deficit Audit. The caution: don’t let a temporary cause justify a permanent change to the structure. If the “one-off” keeps coming back, it was probably a structural gap all along.',
    x: 'A one-off has a cause you can name, and that cause is temporary: illness, travel, a busy work month, a family event. The miss is real, but it doesn’t say anything about the structure.\nThe audit’s rule is simple. Note it and move on. No treaty change, no big talk.\nThe caution runs the other way too. A temporary cause shouldn’t be used to make a permanent change. If one person was ill for two weeks, that isn’t a reason to hand their jobs away for good.\nAn everyday example: the laundry slipped three times in March while one person was traveling for work. In April, it’s back to normal. The audit marks March as a one-off. If April and May slip too, it’s time to call it a structural gap.\nTry: when you mark something a one-off, write the cause next to it. It makes next month’s check much easier.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'Read the three kinds' },

  anchortax: { t: 'The Anchor Tax', m: ['Anchor Tax'],
    s: 'The hidden cost of arguing about the same unowned job again and again.',
    f: 'It doesn’t come from one missed task. It comes from arguing over that task from scratch every time it comes back.',
    w: 'Chapter V says it grows fastest wherever a structural gap is left open. The cure is to give the job a name that sticks, in the RACI Treaty, so it stops being argued over.',
    x: 'The Anchor Tax is a cost of friction, not of time. Each time an unowned job comes back, you both have to argue it from the start: who noticed, who did it last, whose turn it is. That costs energy and goodwill as well as minutes.\nChapter V says it grows fastest where a structural gap is left open. A single missed task is noise. The same task reopening month after month is the tax.\nAn everyday example: every few weeks, the question of who books the kids’ haircuts comes up. It takes ten minutes each time and always ends a bit sour. Writing one name into the treaty ends the renegotiation, and the ten minutes go back into the week.\nThis connects to the Deficit Audit, which exists to find exactly these repeat gaps, and to the RACI Treaty, where the fix is written down so it sticks.\nTry: name one job you’ve argued about more than twice this month. Give it an owner and a date to review how it’s going.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#deficit', l: 'Read Chapter V' },

  retrobill: { t: 'Not a retroactive bill', m: ['retroactive bill'],
    s: 'The audit never adds up past hours and charges them to someone.',
    f: 'Those hours are gone. Billing for them backward doesn’t fix anything; it turns an audit into a grievance.',
    w: 'The Deficit Audit only looks back to find the pattern, and only acts forward on the structure. A job with no owner is a gap in the treaty, not a verdict on the person who kept covering it.',
    x: 'It’s tempting, after a hard month, to want the past counted. The audit doesn’t do that, on purpose. It asks only one question about last month: where did the structure fail to hold?\nPast hours matter, and the person who carried them deserves to be seen. But the program’s way of honoring that is to fix the structure going forward and to thank specific things, not to send an invoice.\nAn everyday example: during the audit, one person starts to list every weekend they spent on the garden. The other listens, then says, “You’ve carried that for months. Let’s make it shared from now.” The hurt is heard, and the page moves forward.\nWP-04 is also clear that it isn’t a performance review. A job with no owner is a gap in the treaty, not a judgment on the person who kept covering it.\nTry: if old hours come up in the audit, write each one as a forward change: “From now on, this job belongs to…”.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#not-for', l: 'What it isn’t for' },

  rhythms: { t: 'The program’s rhythms', m: ['two rhythms'],
    s: 'Small checks every day, a closing every week, and a look back every month.',
    f: 'Each catches something the others miss.',
    w: 'The daily 90-second loop (WP-13) keeps small things small. The weekly closing tallies the Lemonade Stand and glances at the treaty. The monthly Deficit Audit (WP-04) spots patterns that only show up over four weeks.',
    x: 'Each rhythm has a different job, and they work best together.\nIt includes: the daily 90-second loop (WP-13), kept tiny on purpose so it’s too small to skip; the weekly closing, where you tally the Lemonade Stand and take a quick look at the treaty; and the monthly Deficit Audit (WP-04), which steps back to find what keeps reopening.\nSmall, frequent checks keep small things small. But they have a blind spot: a job missed once looks like a fluke. The monthly look is what catches the pattern. And the monthly audit only works if the weekly rhythm keeps feeding it real data.\nAn everyday example: a couple does the daily loop over breakfast, their weekly closing on Sunday evening with tea, and the audit on the first Saturday of the month. None of them takes long.\nTry: pick a fixed, easy moment for each rhythm, tied to something you already do, and write the three times on your fridge.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'Read Chapter V' },

  maintmargin: { t: 'Maintenance margin', m: ['maintenance margin'],
    s: 'Leaving some room to spare, so an ordinary bad week doesn’t tip everything over.',
    f: 'A household running at ninety-five percent of its capacity has no reserve, and the ordinary bad week always comes.',
    w: 'It’s a principle borrowed from economics that runs through the program: noticing, remembering and planning draw from a real, limited account. It’s part of why high stress readings mean “later” and why CALC-01 counts stress at all.',
    x: 'Maintenance margin is spare room: time, energy and attention you leave unused on purpose, so you have something to draw on when a week goes wrong.\nThe About page traces it to economics, the study of limited capacity and what it costs to share it out. Noticing, remembering and planning are real costs from a real account, whether or not anyone records them.\nAn everyday example: a household plans every evening and weekend to the hour. It works, until a child gets a fever on Tuesday. With no margin, one sick day knocks over the week, and tempers go with it. With a little margin, it’s just a hard day.\nIt’s also why high stress readings point to “later,” and why stress sits in the solvency sum at all. A load that only works when both people are rested has no margin in it.\nTry: leave one evening this week with nothing planned. If it stays empty, enjoy it. If something goes wrong, you’ll have room to meet it.',
    u: '/about-in-depth.html#curriculum', l: 'Read where it came from' },

  undefinedowner: { t: 'Undefined ownership', m: ['undefined ownership', 'whoever notices first'],
    s: 'Work that belongs to everyone ends up belonging to no one.',
    f: 'It quietly lands on whoever notices first, every time. Then that person gets called controlling for noticing.',
    w: 'This is the problem the RACI Treaty (WP-03) was built for: one Responsible and one Accountable name per regular job. The amendment process matters as much as the table, so ownership changes on purpose and in writing, never by silent default.',
    x: 'The About page says fifteen years of audit work showed that most things that go wrong aren’t caused by incompetence. They’re caused by undefined ownership. Work that belongs to everyone lands on whoever notices first, and that person can end up called controlling for noticing.\nThe RACI Treaty (WP-03) gives each regular job one Responsible name (who does it) and one Accountable name (who notices if it didn’t happen). They can be the same person.\nAn everyday example: the smoke alarm chirps for a week. Both people hear it. Each assumes the other will deal with it. Finally the one who can’t stand it anymore changes the battery, feeling resentful. A single name in a row would have saved the whole week.\nChanges happen on purpose. Either person can call a review at the weekly closing, the change is written into the table, and both initial it. That way, work never shifts by silent default.\nTry: walk through one room together and list the jobs in it that don’t yet have a clear owner.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'Read the treaty' },

  /* ---------- Workpapers ---------- */

  field: { t: 'The Field Audit (WP-01)', m: ['Field Audit', 'Daily Balance Sheet', 'Who did what'],
    s: 'A one-week log of who actually did what at home.',
    f: 'The day, the task, who did it, rough minutes, and whether someone asked or it was simply noticed.',
    w: 'It captures the week before either person’s memory hardens into a story. Fill it in together, or separately and compare, but never about the other person. Its totals feed the Lemonade Stand and the workload balance in CALC-01. Part B, Neutral Refusals, sits in the same workpaper.',
    x: 'The log has five simple columns: the day, the task noticed or done, who did it, rough minutes, and whether it was asked for or just noticed and handled. That last column is where invisible work finally shows up.\nRough minutes are enough. The point isn’t precision; it’s having one shared page instead of two memories that have each quietly rounded in their own favor.\nAn everyday example: by Thursday, one person’s rows are mostly “noticed and handled,” and the other’s are mostly “asked for.” Neither is doing anything wrong. One is carrying the noticing, and now both of you can see it.\nAt the end of the week, the totals go into the Lemonade Stand, and over a month the logs feed the Deficit Audit (WP-04). The same workpaper holds Part B, the Neutral Refusals, for saying “not now” without a fight.\nTry: keep the log on the fridge or a shared note for just three days. Add a row each time you notice something, even if it only took a minute.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#log', l: 'Dive into the Field Audit' },

  auditrule: { t: 'The audit rule', m: ['Audit rule'],
    s: 'Log what happened, not what should have happened.',
    f: '“I noticed the trash needed to go out three times before doing it” is one honest row, not three rows of resentment.',
    w: 'It borrows an auditor’s discipline: write down plainly what happened before deciding what it means. That keeps the log a record of facts both people can read, not a case being built.',
    x: 'The rule keeps the log clean. Write what happened, in plain words, and leave what it means for later. Once a row carries a judgment, it stops being a record and starts being an argument.\nIt also stops one moment becoming many. If you noticed the trash three times before taking it out, that’s one row with an honest note, not three rows that each carry a little resentment.\nAn everyday example: one person is about to write “Did ALL the dinner again because nobody else would.” Instead they write “Cooked dinner, 45 min, noticed and handled.” The fact is the same. The second version can be read by both people without anyone bracing.\nThis comes from an auditor’s habit, which the How It Works page describes: write down what actually happened, in plain terms, before deciding what it means.\nTry: read back one row you wrote this week. If it contains “always,” “never” or a feeling about the other person, rewrite it as just the event and the minutes.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#audit-rule', l: 'Read the audit rule' },

  refusal: { t: 'A neutral refusal', m: ['Neutral Refusals', 'neutral refusal', 'kind ways to say no', 'Capacity Check', 'Delegation Pivot', 'Time Commitment'],
    s: 'A calm, kind way to say “not right now” that keeps the door open.',
    f: '“I know bedtime is a lot solo. I’m running on empty tonight. Can we split it: I’ll do bath, you do the rest?”',
    w: 'Three moves, in order: acknowledge the request is real, state your capacity honestly, and offer an alternative. The site groups them into three kinds: the Capacity Check, the Delegation Pivot and the Time Commitment. It isn’t a justification essay or a counter-list of everything you did. The other person can still respond.',
    x: 'A neutral refusal isn’t a no. It’s an honest “not like this, not right now,” with something offered in its place. The other person can still respond, push back, or suggest something else.\nIt includes three kinds. The Capacity Check is for when you’re already full. The Delegation Pivot is for a job someone else is better placed to do. The Time Commitment is for a job you’ll take on, just not on the timeline being assumed.\nAn everyday example of a Time Commitment: “I do want to plan the trip with you. I don’t want to commit to dates on the spot without checking my week. Let me look tonight and give you a real answer tomorrow.”\nWP-01 is honest that no fixed set of scripts can cover every situation. So it teaches the pattern underneath, and you build your own words from it.\nTry: think of one request that often catches you on a bad day. Write a neutral refusal for it now, while you’re calm, so it’s ready when you need it.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'See examples' },

  threemoves: { t: 'Acknowledge, state capacity, offer', m: ['State Capacity', 'Offer Alternative', 'three-part shape'],
    s: 'The three steps inside every neutral refusal.',
    f: 'Name why the request is fair. Say honestly what you have left. Offer something instead of a flat no.',
    w: 'Acknowledge means naming the actual need, not a generic “I understand.” Stating capacity is honest: not an excuse, and not overstating what you can’t do. Offer an alternative can be a different time, a different person, a partial version, or a plan to revisit. The goal: the other person is never left with nothing.',
    x: 'Every neutral refusal follows the same shape, in the same order. The order matters: being heard first makes the “not now” much easier to receive.\nIt includes: acknowledge (say something true and specific about why the request is reasonable); state capacity (say honestly what you have, whether that’s energy, time, skill or focus); and offer (give something concrete in place of a flat no).\nAn everyday example: “The car noise does need looking at. I genuinely don’t know enough about cars to handle this well. Can you take the lead, and I’ll cover something else off your plate in exchange?” Each sentence does one job.\nThe offer is what keeps it kind. WP-01 lists the options: a different time, a different person, a partial version, or a plan to revisit. The other person is never left holding nothing.\nTry: next time you want to say no, count your sentences. Aim for three: one acknowledge, one capacity, one offer. Then stop and listen.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#adapt', l: 'Build your own' },

  bandwidth: { t: 'Bandwidth', m: ['emotional bandwidth', 'bandwidth', 'at capacity'],
    s: 'How much you honestly have left to give right now.',
    f: 'Energy, attention, patience, skill or time. Some days there’s plenty; some days there isn’t.',
    w: 'Saying it plainly is the middle step of a neutral refusal. It’s information, not an excuse. If your bandwidth is low day after day, WP-02 helps you see how much you’re already carrying.',
    x: 'Bandwidth is a way of talking about capacity without making it sound like a character trait. It isn’t how much you care. It’s how much you have left today.\nIt covers more than time. WP-01 names bandwidth, energy, skill and timing. You might have time but no patience left, or energy but not the know-how.\nAn everyday example: a partner wants to talk through a hard day. The other says, “I can tell you need to talk this through. I don’t have the emotional bandwidth to be fully present right now. Can we talk after I’ve had a chance to reset, maybe in an hour?” That’s honest, and it keeps the door open.\nStating capacity isn’t an excuse, and it isn’t overstating what you can’t do. It’s just what’s true. If it’s low most days, the Battery Meter (WP-02) can help you see what else you’re carrying.\nTry: at the end of today, give your bandwidth one word, like full, half or empty, and share it with the person you live with.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#adapt', l: 'Read about stating capacity' },

  boundary: { t: 'A calm boundary', m: ['calm boundary', 'boundary statement'],
    s: 'One clear, kind statement of what you can and can’t do right now.',
    f: 'It’s a single sentence that keeps the door open, not a wall and not a speech.',
    w: 'WP-01 describes a neutral refusal as exactly this: a single, calm boundary statement that leaves room to renegotiate. It isn’t a refusal to ever revisit the request, and it isn’t a list of everything you already did.',
    x: 'A calm boundary is short on purpose. One sentence says what’s true for you right now and leaves space for the other person to answer. It isn’t a wall, and it isn’t a speech.\nWP-01 is clear about what it isn’t: a justification essay, a counter-list of everything you already did, or a refusal to ever talk about it again. Each of those shuts a door; a calm boundary keeps one open.\nAn everyday example: a relative asks for a big favor through your partner. The partner says, “I get why your mom’s request feels urgent to you. I don’t have room to take this on this week. Can you let her know it’ll have to wait, or handle this one yourself?” No blame, no lecture.\nIt works best when you’re settled enough to say it without an edge. If you can’t yet, the signal line from the Calm-Down Kit buys you time first.\nTry: write one boundary sentence you often need, and read it out loud once so it feels familiar when the moment comes.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#adapt', l: 'Read more' },

  battery: { t: 'Your battery score (WP-02)', m: ['Battery & Stress Meter', 'Battery &amp; Stress Meter', 'Autonomic Saturation Index', 'battery score', 'How full is your battery?'],
    s: 'A quick check, only about yourself, of how much you’re already carrying today.',
    f: 'Score five things from 0 to 4 for the last day or two, add them up and divide by 20.',
    w: 'Under 0.3: probably about the thing itself. 0.3 to 0.6: say “heads up, I’m carrying more than usual” before a hard talk. Over 0.6: put off anything that doesn’t need deciding in the next hour. It’s a postponement tool, not an exit: a high score means “later,” never “this doesn’t need to happen.” It isn’t a clinical tool.',
    x: 'The Battery Meter separates two questions that often get tangled: how much stress am I already carrying, and how upset am I about this specific thing? Each person fills it in about themselves, for the last day or two.\nThe score matters most for timing. A low score means whatever’s coming up is probably about the thing itself. A middle score is worth naming before a hard talk. A high score is a sign to postpone anything that doesn’t need deciding soon.\nAn everyday example: one person scores 0.7 after a night with a sick child and a deadline. They say, “My battery’s high today. Can we do the budget talk tomorrow evening instead?” That’s not dodging; it’s picking a time when they can actually think.\nIts limits are clear. Don’t fill it in about the other person, and don’t use a high score to end a talk you’d rather not have. It feeds the stress input in CALC-01 and is the scale the Calm-Down Kit reads before and after.\nTry: score yourself once this morning and once this evening, just to see how much a day can move it.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter-in-depth.html#reading', l: 'Dive into the Battery & Stress Meter' },

  fivefactors: { t: 'The five battery factors', m: ['5-Factor', 'five factors', 'Sleep debt', 'Workload elsewhere'],
    s: 'The five things you score to read your battery.',
    f: 'Each one is scored 0 (not at all) to 4 (very true), for how the last 24 to 48 hours actually went.',
    w: 'The five: sleep debt, workload elsewhere (job, school, caring), recent unresolved conflict, physical state (hungry, sick, in pain), and time pressure today. The Calm-Down Kit reuses the same five on purpose, so every reading can be compared with the others.',
    x: 'Each factor is something ordinary that quietly drains you. Scoring them one by one helps you see why today feels heavy, instead of just feeling that it does.\nIt includes: sleep debt (less rested than usual); workload elsewhere (job, school or caring for someone); recent unresolved conflict (from any source, not just home); physical state (hungry, sick or in pain); and time pressure today specifically.\nAn everyday example: someone feels “fine” but snaps at a small question. They score themselves: sleep 3, work 2, conflict 0, physical 3 (skipped lunch), time 2. That’s 10 out of 20, or 0.5. Eating something is the easiest first fix.\nThe same five are used everywhere they’re needed, in WP-02, the Calm-Down Kit and CALC-01. The Kit says this is on purpose: one measure for one thing means every reading can be compared with every other.\nTry: next time you feel short-tempered, run through just the five words in your head and see which one is loudest.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter-in-depth.html#checklist-h', l: 'See the checklist' },

  notclinical: { t: 'Not a clinical tool', m: ['not a clinical instrument', 'not a clinical or diagnostic instrument', 'not a clinical tool', 'not a validated clinical'],
    s: 'These tools help you reflect. They don’t diagnose or treat anyone.',
    f: 'Think of them as a structured gut-check, not a test with a right answer.',
    w: 'The numbers weren’t built from research data, and they predict nothing. They describe a situation, never a person’s worth. For panic, trauma responses or ongoing anxiety, a doctor or therapist is the better call.',
    x: 'The site says this plainly and often: the tools are a practical rule of thumb, not a clinical or diagnostic tool. They weren’t built from research data, and they predict nothing.\nThat’s not a weakness to hide. It’s what keeps them gentle. A structured gut-check can help two people start a calmer conversation. It can’t tell you what’s wrong with anyone, and it shouldn’t try.\nAn everyday example: someone notices their battery score is high most weeks, and they’ve started having racing-heart moments that come out of nowhere. The page has done its job by helping them notice. The next step isn’t a better worksheet; it’s a chat with a doctor.\nThe Calm-Down Kit says it directly: panic attacks, trauma responses and ongoing anxiety are not what these tools address. A doctor or therapist is the better call.\nTry: if a score keeps worrying you, write down what you’ve noticed and take the note to someone qualified to help.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'Read the honest note' },

  heuristic: { t: 'A heuristic', m: ['heuristic self-reflection tool', 'practical heuristic', 'heuristic'],
    s: 'A practical rule of thumb for thinking something through.',
    f: 'Like a quick sketch of a room: useful for deciding where the furniture goes, not a surveyor’s measurement.',
    w: 'The site calls its tools heuristics on purpose: a helpful starting point for a conversation, not a scientific instrument or a verdict. P(Solvency) is described this way, and so is the Carrier Wave Decoder. What makes them worth acting on is using them the same way, week after week.',
    x: 'A heuristic is a rule of thumb: a quick, practical way to think something through when a perfect answer isn’t available. It’s meant to be useful, not exact.\nThe site’s tools are heuristics on purpose. CALC-01’s weights are described as judgment calls, not numbers worked out from data. The word “probability” in P(Solvency) is only a label. None of it claims to predict anything.\nWhat makes a heuristic worth acting on is using it the same way over time. One entry is a mood. Twelve entries, asked the same way, week after week, start to show a pattern that memory alone can’t.\nAn everyday example: a couple uses the Battery Meter every Sunday. No single number means much. But after two months, they notice the scores always rise in the week before school breaks. That’s a pattern they can plan for.\nTry: pick one tool and use it at the same time each week for a month before deciding what it’s telling you.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'Read the honest note' },

  raci: { t: 'RACI: one owner per job (WP-03)', m: ['Domestic RACI Treaty', 'RACI Treaty', 'RACI', 'One owner per job'], cs: true,
    s: 'A shared list that gives every regular job a clear owner.',
    f: 'Groceries, dishes, bills, pet care, the family calendar: each gets two names written next to it.',
    w: 'Responsible does the job; Accountable notices if it didn’t happen and follows up (it can be the same person). It stops ownership being renegotiated every week. It changes through an amendment process, not by one person quietly doing more. RACI started in project management, so it fits teams too.',
    x: 'The treaty is a simple table. Each regular job gets a row, how often it happens, and two names: who is Responsible and who is Accountable. There’s a notes column, and room to add rows.\nThe starter list includes groceries, cooking, dishes, laundry, bills and scheduling, cleaning, pet care, car upkeep, the social and family calendar, and emotional check-ins. Every home will add its own.\nThe About page says it was borrowed almost directly from workplace “internal controls,” the checks that make sure every task has an owner. It fits there for the same reason it fits at home: work that belongs to everyone lands on whoever notices first.\nAn everyday example: a couple fills in the table and finds they both thought the other was handling birthday presents for nieces and nephews. One row, two names, and the next birthday is covered. It feeds ownership clarity in CALC-01, and it changes only through the amendment process, in writing.\nTry: start with just five rows for the jobs that cause the most friction, and fill in both names together.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#treaty', l: 'Dive into the RACI Treaty' },

  ra: { t: 'Responsible and Accountable', m: ['Responsible (R)', 'Accountable (A)', 'one Responsible'],
    s: 'Responsible does the job. Accountable notices if it didn’t happen.',
    f: 'For laundry, one person might do it (R) while the other keeps an eye on it (A). Or one person holds both.',
    w: 'A row only counts toward ownership clarity when both names are filled in. Accountable isn’t a boss; it’s the person who gently follows up so a job doesn’t silently become nobody’s.',
    x: 'Splitting the two roles lets a job be shared without becoming vague. One person can do it while the other keeps an eye on it, or one person can hold both.\nResponsible is the doer. Accountable is the one who notices if it didn’t happen and follows up. That second role isn’t a manager or a critic. It’s a quiet safety net, so a job doesn’t slide into nobody’s column.\nAn everyday example: for the weekly shop, one person writes the list and goes (R). The other checks on Friday that it’s planned, since Saturdays get busy (A). If the shop slips, there’s no argument about whose job it was.\nA row only counts toward the ownership clarity score when both names are written in. An empty Accountable box is often where a structural gap is hiding.\nTry: pick one shared job and decide together who does it and who keeps an eye on it. Write both names down.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'Read the treaty' },

  amendment: { t: 'The amendment process', m: ['amendment process'],
    s: 'How the job list changes when life changes: openly, and in writing.',
    f: 'A new job, a health issue, a new baby: that’s when the treaty gets reviewed, not silently rewritten.',
    w: 'Three steps: either person can call a review at the weekly closing; changes go into the table, not just agreed out loud; both people initial them. REPORT-01 flags a treaty that hasn’t changed in six weeks, since a living agreement that never changes probably isn’t being used.',
    x: 'The treaty is meant to change. Life changes: new jobs, health issues, a new baby, a move. What matters is how the treaty changes.\nIt includes three steps: either person can call for a review at the weekly closing; changes are written into the table, not just agreed out loud; and both people initial the change.\nThe written part protects both people. Without it, ownership slowly shifts to whoever cracks first, and the other person may not even know it happened.\nAn everyday example: one person starts a new job with early starts. At the Sunday closing, they ask to review mornings. School drop-off moves across, evening dishes move back, and both initial the new rows. Nobody had to earn it or apologize. REPORT-01 flags a treaty that hasn’t changed in six weeks, since a living agreement that never changes probably isn’t being used.\nTry: put “treaty check” on your next weekly closing, and ask one question: has anything in our lives changed that the table doesn’t know about yet?',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#amendment', l: 'Read about amending' },

  ratification: { t: 'Ratification', m: ['Ratification'],
    s: 'Both of you initial the list to say ownership is clear.',
    f: 'It doesn’t mean every job feels perfectly fair. It means you both know who owns what.',
    w: 'It’s the last part of WP-03. Clear first, fair next: once ownership is written down, fairness can be talked about calmly, using the Lemonade Stand and the Deficit Audit.',
    x: 'Ratification is the moment you both put your initials and a date on the current table. It’s small, but it turns a draft into an agreement.\nWhat you’re agreeing to is narrow and honest. You’re not saying every job feels perfectly fair. You’re saying you’ve both read the table and agree that ownership is clear as written.\nThat separation helps. Fairness is a real conversation, and the Lemonade Stand and the Deficit Audit are good places for it. But it goes much better once you both know who owns what.\nAn everyday example: a couple fills in the treaty on a Sunday afternoon. One job still feels lopsided to one of them. They initial anyway, add a note to revisit that row in a month, and move on. The table is clear today, and the fairness talk has a date.\nTry: after your next treaty change, both initial and date it, even if it feels a little formal. Clarity likes a paper trail.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#ratification', l: 'Read the treaty' },

  tone: { t: 'The Tone Filter (WP-09)', m: ['Tone Transducer', 'fact, feeling and ask', 'fact, feeling and a clear ask', 'Say it so it lands', 'transducer step'],
    s: 'A quick self-check before you answer something that stung.',
    f: 'Find the fact (one sentence, no adjectives), the feeling underneath, and the ask: what you’d like to happen next.',
    w: 'If the sentence is a verdict on the person (“you always,” “you’re careless”), stop: that’s criticism, not an ask. Swap it for one fact and one ask. The Filter half weighs what you just heard before you react. It never records or analyzes anyone’s voice; it’s a check on your own next message.',
    x: 'The Tone Filter starts with its transducer step. A transducer turns one kind of signal into another. Here, it turns a raw reaction into something the other person can actually receive. It’s a self-check on your own next message, done before you speak or send.\nIt includes three questions: the fact (one sentence, no adjectives); the feeling (a name for it, like frustrated, tired, unseen or rushed); and the ask (what you want to happen next, specifically).\nWP-09’s own example: the raw reaction is “You never think about anyone but yourself.” The fact is that the trash wasn’t taken out before pickup. The feeling is frustrated, and a little unseen. The ask is a fixed day and time for trash. Same issue, a frequency the other person can tune into.\nThen you can paste the ask into the Signal Translator and keep the fact in its facts box, so the translator scores your ask, not a verdict.\nTry: next time you draft a sharp text, stop and write the three lines first. Send only the ask, plus the fact if needed.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter-in-depth.html#transducer', l: 'Dive into the Tone Filter' },

  filter: { t: 'The filter step', m: ['filter step', 'Emotional Translation Filter'],
    s: 'Weighing what you just heard before you react to it.',
    f: 'Would I read this the same way if I weren’t already worn out? Is there a neutral meaning that fits too?',
    w: 'Four questions: is it about a specific task or event; would I read it the same way if I weren’t already worn out; is there a neutral reading; am I answering their words or an old pattern? If you’re running high, or a neutral meaning might still fit, pause or use a neutral refusal before going on.',
    x: 'The filter runs in the other direction from the transducer. Instead of shaping what you send, it weighs what you just received, before you react to it.\nIt includes four checks: is this about a specific task or event; would I read it the same way if I weren’t already worn out; is there a neutral meaning that also fits; and am I answering their words, or a pattern from an old conversation?\nAn everyday example: “Are you going to be on your phone all night?” lands like an attack. Running the filter: it is about something specific; I’m tired, so I may be reading it hotter; a neutral reading is “I’d like some time with you.” The answer changes from defensive to “Sorry, I’ll put it down. Want to watch something?”\nIf you’re running high, or a neutral meaning might still fit, WP-09 suggests using a neutral refusal or a pause script first.\nTry: pick one of the four questions to remember this week, and ask it silently once when something stings.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter-in-depth.html#filter', l: 'See the four questions' },

  factsbox: { t: 'The facts box', m: ['facts box'],
    s: 'A place to write the plain event, kept apart from the sentence you’ll say.',
    f: '“The dishes were still in the sink” is a fact. “You never help” is a criticism of the person.',
    w: 'In the Signal Translator the facts box holds the event and isn’t scored as the message. Keeping them apart means the listener hears the event instead of defending against a verdict.',
    x: 'The facts box is where the plain event goes: what happened, when, with no labels. The sentence you’ll actually say goes in a different box. Keeping them apart is the whole point.\nWhen a fact and a judgment are mixed, the listener can only answer the judgment. They defend their character, and the event never gets discussed. Separated, the event can be looked at calmly and given an owner.\nAn everyday example: the facts box says “Dishes still in the sink at 7am; the morning started behind.” The sentence says “Could we agree on a quick kitchen close by 9 at night?” The listener hears a request, not a charge.\nThe Check-ins page links this to WP-09. When you write your fact, feeling and ask, the fact goes in the facts box and the ask becomes the phrase. That way the Signal Translator reads the sentence, not the frustration behind it.\nTry: before a hard conversation, write the one-line fact on paper first. If it contains “always,” “never” or a label, trim it until it’s just the event.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#translator', l: 'Where the translator sits' },

  pll: { t: 'The 90-second check-in (WP-13)', m: ['Phase-Locked Loop', '90-second daily check-in', '90-second check-in', 'daily loop'],
    s: 'A 90-second daily check-in that keeps small things small.',
    f: 'One sentence each: your load, one thing you appreciated, one small friction, one ask for tomorrow.',
    w: 'One speaks, the other just listens. Nothing is debated or solved; anything bigger is flagged for the weekly resync. Its technical name, the Phase-Locked Loop, comes from a circuit that keeps two signals in step with tiny, constant corrections. One thing to watch for: using it to criticize, which makes people start avoiding it.',
    x: 'A phase-locked loop is a circuit that keeps two signals in step. It doesn’t wait for them to drift far apart and then yank them back. It makes tiny corrections all the time, so the drift never builds up.\nThe daily loop does the same for two people. WP-13 says a 90-second daily check-in prevents most of what would otherwise become a 45-minute weekend argument.\nPick a steady moment, like after dinner or before bed. Each person says four short things: their load, an appreciation, one small friction if there is one, and one ask if there is one. One speaks, the other listens. No debate, no solving, no rebuttal.\nAn everyday example: “Today I was at high capacity. I appreciated you taking the call from the school. One small thing: I missed a heads-up about dinner plans. Tomorrow it’d help to text me by four.” That’s it. The other person just says thanks.\nTry: do the loop tonight, even if it feels awkward. Keep it under two minutes and stop there.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#daily-loop', l: 'Dive into the daily loop' },

  fourparts: { t: 'Load, appreciation, friction, ask', m: ['load, appreciation, friction, ask', 'load, appreciation, friction'],
    s: 'The four things each person says in the daily check-in.',
    f: 'One sentence each, out loud, at a regular moment like after dinner.',
    w: 'Load: “Today I was at about low / medium / high capacity.” Appreciation: one specific thing you noticed. Friction: one small thing that didn’t feel great (optional). Ask: one thing that would help tomorrow (optional). Being specific beats being general, and small moments of noticing matter as much as fixing things.',
    x: 'Each part has a job. Together they make a quick status report, not a conversation.\nIt includes: load (“Today I was at about low, medium or high capacity”); appreciation (one specific thing you noticed, with the moment); friction (one small thing that didn’t feel great, optional); and ask (one thing that would help tomorrow, optional).\nSpecific beats general, especially for the appreciation. “Thanks for everything” is warm but vague. “Thanks for putting the kettle on when you saw I was on a call” shows you noticed. WP-13 points to research on everyday connection suggesting these small moments matter as much as fixing what went wrong.\nAn everyday example: “Medium today. I appreciated the note you left on the counter. No friction. Could you grab bread tomorrow?” Four lines, twenty seconds.\nTry: keep the four words on a sticky note where you’ll see them at your chosen moment, so the loop runs itself.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#daily-loop', l: 'See the daily loop' },

  drift: { t: 'Frequency drift', m: ['frequency drift'],
    s: 'Slowly slipping out of step, a little at a time.',
    f: 'Nobody notices a day’s drift. Weeks of it become a 45-minute weekend argument.',
    w: 'The daily loop exists to stop drift building up: tiny corrections every day instead of big ones every so often. Avoiding the loop is what lets drift build, which is why keeping it light matters.',
    x: 'Drift is the slow, quiet slide out of step. Nobody decides to drift. A missed thank-you here, a small irritation left unsaid there, and after a few weeks you’re further apart than either of you noticed.\nChapter I describes the squeal that happens when two people are out of tune. Drift is how you get there gradually, rather than all at once.\nAn everyday example: over a busy month, both people stop mentioning small frictions because there never seems to be time. Then one Saturday, a small comment about the car sets off a 45-minute argument about everything. Each small thing was minor. Together, they weren’t.\nThe daily loop (WP-13) is built to catch it early. Its main risk is being skipped, and the easiest way to get it skipped is to use it for criticism. Keeping it light is what keeps it going, and keeping it going is what keeps drift small.\nTry: if you realize you haven’t done a daily check-in for a while, restart tonight with only the load and one appreciation.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#why', l: 'Why small beats big' },

  resync: { t: 'The weekly resync', m: ['weekly resync'],
    s: 'Once a week, look back over the daily check-ins for anything that keeps coming up.',
    f: 'Anything that came up more than twice moves on to a bigger tool, instead of hanging around.',
    w: 'A repeated friction moves to the RACI Treaty (WP-03) if it’s about ownership, or the tone check (WP-09) if it’s about how things are said. The daily loop just notices; anything that needs weighing up waits for the resync. If it needs a real talk, that’s a check-in.',
    x: 'The resync is a short weekly look back over the daily check-ins. It happens alongside the weekly closing, so it doesn’t need its own evening.\nSkim the week’s loop notes for anything that came up more than twice. That repeat moves on: to the RACI Treaty (WP-03) if it’s about who owns a job, or to the Tone Filter (WP-09) if it’s about how things are said.\nThis keeps the daily loop safe. It stays a quick update, never a trial, because anything that needs weighing has a place to go.\nAn everyday example: “Friction: no heads-up about dinner plans” shows up three times in a week. At the resync, it becomes a small treaty row: one person owns the weekly meal plan, shared by Sunday night. The irritation stops coming back. If a repeat needs a real talk, the Check-ins page says not to stretch the 90 seconds. Use a check-in instead: a named time, a settled room, acknowledgement before any rebuttal.\nTry: this Sunday, read back the week’s frictions and circle any that appeared twice or more.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#weekly-resync', l: 'Read about the resync' },

  closing: { t: 'The weekly closing', m: ['weekly closing', 'closing the books', 'close the books'],
    s: 'A short weekly look at the week’s numbers together, like closing the books.',
    f: 'Tally the Lemonade Stand, glance at the treaty, and note anything that needs a real talk.',
    w: 'It’s the middle rhythm, between the daily loop and the monthly audit. Either person can call a treaty review here. Keeping it regular is what makes the monthly Deficit Audit accurate, and it’s the conversation the solvency number is meant to start.',
    x: 'Closing the books is the weekly habit that ties the rest together. It’s short, it’s regular, and it gives the week a clear end.\nIt includes: tallying the Lemonade Stand hours; glancing at the RACI Treaty; running the weekly resync over the daily loop notes; and noting anything that needs a proper talk, so it gets a check-in with a named time.\nChapter II says the solvency number’s only job is to give two people a shared, low-drama starting point for exactly this conversation. And WP-04 says the monthly audit is only as accurate as the weekly closings that feed it.\nAn everyday example: Sunday evening, with a cup of tea. Ten minutes adding up hours, a quick look at the table, one note: “Mornings are getting tight; talk Wednesday?” Then it’s done, and the week is closed.\nTry: choose a fixed time for your closing and keep the first one under fifteen minutes, so it feels easy to come back to.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'Read about the rhythms' },

  /* ---------- Settling the body ---------- */

  kit: { t: 'The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11)', m: ['Calm-Down Kit'],
    s: 'A plan you make while calm, for settling your body in a heated moment.',
    f: 'You decide now, so later you only have to do what you already chose.',
    w: 'Three parts: set your defaults (what starts it, your body’s first signal, two go-to tactics, a signal line); run them in the moment; then go back in only when your battery score says you’re ready. It’s for you, never something to hand the other person, and it isn’t treatment.',
    x: 'The Calm-Down Kit is built around one idea: in a heated moment you can’t weigh options well, so you choose them ahead of time. Part A is filled in on an ordinary day.\nIt includes three parts. Before you need it: what tends to start it, your body’s first signal, two default tactics, and a signal line. In the moment: read your battery, say the line, run your default. Re-entry: read your battery again, and go back when the number says so.\nAn everyday example: a talk about money starts to heat up. One person notices their jaw tightening, their first signal. They say their line, step outside, and walk slowly for five minutes. They come back at the time they named.\nIt’s used by one person, about themselves, never handed to the other. It leaves out anything that works by cold, pain or shock, and it isn’t treatment. For panic, trauma responses or ongoing anxiety, a doctor or therapist is the better call.\nTry: fill in Part A this week on a calm evening. It takes minutes, and your future self will thank you.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'Dive into the Calm-Down Kit' },

  firstsignal: { t: 'The body’s first signal', m: ['first signal', 'somatic signals'],
    s: 'The earliest sign in your body that you’re getting wound up.',
    f: 'A tight jaw, raised shoulders, shallow breath, sharper hearing, cold hands.',
    w: 'It arrives before the thought does, which is what makes it worth knowing. The Calm-Down Kit asks you to name yours on an ordinary day, so you can treat the first sign as the cue instead of waiting for the fifth.',
    x: 'Your body usually knows you’re getting wound up before your thinking does. The first signal is the earliest sign, and it’s often small.\nWP-11 lists common ones: a tight jaw, raised shoulders, breath going shallow, hearing getting sharper, hands going cold. Some people notice heat, a tight chest, or an urge to leave or to win the argument.\nThe Check-ins page makes the same point: the explosion comes late. The useful signs are earlier and ordinary. Treat the first one as the cue, not the fifth.\nAn everyday example: someone realizes that whenever a talk turns tense, they start gripping their mug. Now, when they notice the grip, they put the mug down, breathe out slowly, and say they need a moment. It happens before any sharp words.\nTry: next time you feel even slightly irritated, notice where it shows up in your body first. Write it into your kit.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'Name yours' },

  defaults: { t: 'Your two defaults', m: ['two defaults'],
    s: 'Two settling moves you pick ahead of time, so you don’t have to choose in the moment.',
    f: 'A first default, and a second in case the first isn’t possible.',
    w: 'The Kit’s list has six kinds of move: physiological (a long out-breath), orienting (naming the room), kinesthetic (weight and pressure), environmental (lower the input), movement (walking it out) and auditory (low, steady sound). None works by cold, pain or shock, and none claims to be treatment.',
    x: 'Defaults are two settling moves you choose in advance. When the moment comes, you don’t browse a list; you just do the first one, or the second if the first isn’t possible.\nIt includes six kinds to choose from: physiological (breathing out longer than in); orienting (naming what you can see, hear and touch); kinesthetic (feet pressed into the floor, a hand on your chest, something warm held); environmental (lower the lights, leave the noisy room); movement (a slow five-minute walk); and auditory (low, steady sound, if it helps you).\nAn everyday example: someone picks “walk it out” as their first default and “naming the room” as their second, for when they can’t leave, like in the car.\nEverything on the list works by slowing the body down, not startling it. None of it is treatment, and it makes no claims about frequencies or brainwaves.\nTry: test each of your two defaults once on a calm day, so your body already knows them.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'See the tactics' },

  asymbreath: { t: 'Asymmetric breathing', m: ['Asymmetric breathing', 'long exhale'],
    s: 'Breathing out for longer than you breathe in.',
    f: 'Four seconds in, six seconds out, for at least two minutes.',
    w: 'The longer out-breath is the part that helps; counting is what keeps you doing it. It’s one of the Calm-Down Kit’s tactics, and the Carrier Wave Decoder’s one-minute retune uses the same in-for-4, out-for-6 when you’re keyed up.',
    x: 'The idea is simple: breathe out for longer than you breathe in. WP-11 suggests four seconds in and six seconds out, for at least two minutes.\nThe longer out-breath is the part that does the work. Counting just keeps you doing it long enough, which is harder than it sounds when you’re upset.\nIt needs no equipment, no privacy and no explanation, which makes it a good first default for many people. You can do it sitting at a table, in a car or standing at the sink.\nAn everyday example: during a tense phone call about a bill, someone quietly counts four in and six out while the other person talks. By the time it’s their turn, their voice has come down a notch. The Carrier Wave Decoder’s one-minute retune uses the same four-in, six-out pattern when you’re keyed up. It’s an ordinary settling technique, not a treatment.\nTry: practice it for two minutes today while waiting for the kettle, so it’s familiar when you need it.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'See the tactics' },

  namingroom: { t: 'Naming the room', m: ['Naming the room', 'Five things you can see'],
    s: 'Bringing your attention back to where you actually are.',
    f: 'Five things you can see, four you can hear, three you can touch.',
    w: 'It moves attention from the argument in your head to the room you’re standing in. It’s the Kit’s orienting tactic, and a good pick if you tend to go flat or far away rather than revved up.',
    x: 'Naming the room is an orienting tactic. It brings your attention out of the argument in your head and back to the place you’re actually in.\nThe pattern is five things you can see, four you can hear, and three you can touch. Saying them quietly, or just thinking them, gives your mind something plain and present to hold.\nIt suits people who tend to go flat, far away or blank under stress, as well as those who get revved up. The Check-ins page notes that going blank is a kind of dysregulation too; it isn’t the same as calm.\nAn everyday example: a conversation turns heavy and someone feels themselves drifting off. They look around: the lamp, the blue mug, the window, a cushion, the clock. The fridge hum, a car outside, the other person’s voice. The chair arm, their sleeve, the table. They’re back in the room.\nTry: run through the five-four-three once today somewhere ordinary, like a bus stop or a kitchen, so it’s easy to reach for later.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'See the tactics' },

  arousal: { t: 'Autonomic arousal', m: ['autonomic arousal', 'acute arousal'],
    s: 'How revved up your body is right now.',
    f: 'Heart racing, voice rising, thoughts speeding. In that state even a careful sentence can land hard.',
    w: 'The Calm-Down Kit’s whole purpose is to bring it down far enough to have the conversation, or to put it off honestly. WP-03 and WP-09 both depend on it: neither works well while your body is revved up.',
    x: 'Arousal here just means how switched on your body is. It isn’t good or bad. High arousal helps you run for a bus. It doesn’t help you hear a careful sentence.\nOn the Nervous System Ladder, high arousal is the mobilized rung. Your pace speeds up, pauses feel like being ignored, and neutral comments can land at full volume.\nWP-11 says the whole point of the Calm-Down Kit is to lower arousal enough that an ordinary conversation, the treaty session (WP-03) or the Tone Filter (WP-09) becomes possible again. Both depend on it: a fact, feeling and ask written while you’re revved up all come out sharper than you meant.\nAn everyday example: someone finishes a stressful call and walks straight into a question about the weekend. Their answer comes out clipped. Nothing about the weekend upset them; their body was still running from the call.\nTry: after something stressful, give yourself five quiet minutes before starting any conversation that matters.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'Read the Kit’s purpose' },

  signalline: { t: 'Your signal line', m: ['signal line', 'return line', 'pause script'],
    s: 'One sentence you agree ahead of time to say when you step away.',
    f: '“I’m at capacity. I need ten minutes. I’ll be back at quarter past and we can pick this up.”',
    w: 'Its shape: name the state, name the length, name the return. The return time is the part that matters; without it the sentence is a departure, not a pause. Both people agree in advance that hearing it isn’t an insult or an escape, so using the kit isn’t mistaken for stonewalling.',
    x: 'Leaving the room mid-conflict without a word can look like walking out, however it’s meant. The signal line is one sentence you agree on ahead of time, so a pause is understood as a pause.\nIts shape is simple: name the state, name the length, name the return. The return time is the part that matters. Without it, the sentence is a departure, not a pause.\nBoth people agree in advance that hearing the line is not an insult and not an escape. It’s information, and the conversation picks up again at the stated time.\nAn everyday example: a disagreement about vacation plans is getting loud. One person says, “I’m at capacity. I need ten minutes. I’ll be back at half past and we can pick this up.” They go, settle, and come back at half past. The other person knew they would.\nTry: each write your own signal line tonight, read it to each other, and agree that either of you can use it.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'Make yours' },

  returntime: { t: 'A return time', m: ['return time'],
    s: 'A named time you’ll come back to the conversation.',
    f: '“Tomorrow after dinner” is a return time. “Later” isn’t.',
    w: 'It turns a pause into a promise. A flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes, and the return shouldn’t slip past the next day. When you come back, come back to the same topic, and don’t add a new one as interest on the delay.',
    x: 'A return time turns a pause into a promise. It tells the other person exactly when you’ll be back, so they don’t have to fill the silence with worry.\nThe Check-ins page, citing the Gottman Institute, says a flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again. It also says the return shouldn’t slip past the next day.\nWhen you come back, come back to the same topic. A regroup isn’t a rematch. Don’t open with what they did during the pause, and don’t add a new topic as “interest” on the delay.\nAn everyday example: “We are okay. This is a pause. I will come back at 8:40.” At 8:40: “I’m back. The topic is still the sink. I’m not against you.” If your body isn’t back yet, say so and set a new time, rather than coming back just because the time has come.\nTry: next time you need a pause, say an actual clock time instead of “in a bit.” Then set a reminder so you keep it.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regroup', l: 'Read about coming back' },

  reentry: { t: 'The re-entry threshold', m: ['re-entry threshold'],
    s: 'Going back into the talk when your battery score says you’re ready, not just when you feel better.',
    f: 'Score WP-02 again before going back in, and compare it with your score from before.',
    w: 'Under 0.50: go back at the time you said. 0.50 to 0.60: run another round. Still 0.60 or more after two rounds: postpone and name a new time. Then do one small, single-focus task first, like putting the dishes away, to check that your calm actually holds.',
    x: 'The re-entry threshold is a small rule that protects you from going back in too soon. “Feeling better” can be a guess; the battery number is steadier.\nBefore going back, you score the same five factors from WP-02 again and compare with your score from before. Under 0.50, you go back at the time you named. Between 0.50 and 0.60, you run another round of your default. Still 0.60 or more after two rounds means postpone, and name a specific new time.\nThen comes one small, single-focus task: put the dishes away, take the trash out, answer one message. It’s a quiet test that your calm actually holds before you go back into the harder thing.\nAn everyday example: after a walk, someone rescores at 0.55. Instead of heading straight back in, they breathe for another few minutes, rescore at 0.45, fold one basket of laundry, and then go back.\nTry: write the three bands on the back of your kit so you don’t have to remember them in the moment.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#reentry', l: 'Read about re-entry' },

  postpone: { t: 'Postponing, not avoiding', m: ['postponement', 'avoidance'],
    s: 'Putting a talk off to a named time is postponing. Leaving it open-ended is avoiding.',
    f: '“Tomorrow after dinner” is a postponement. “Later” is an avoidance.',
    w: 'The program leans hard on this difference. A high battery score, an unready room or a failed re-entry are all good reasons to postpone. But a kit or a score used to make hard conversations never happen has become the problem it was meant to solve. WP-13 exists partly to catch the difference.',
    x: 'Postponing and avoiding can look the same from the outside. The difference is a named time. “Tomorrow after dinner” is a postponement. “Later” is an avoidance.\nThe program gives plenty of good reasons to postpone: a high battery score, a room that isn’t ready, a re-entry number that hasn’t come down, someone about to leave for work. Postponing with a return time is care, not escape.\nBut the same tools can be misused. WP-02 says a high score is a postponement tool, not an exit. WP-11 says a kit used to make hard conversations never happen has become the problem it was meant to solve.\nAn everyday example: a talk about in-laws keeps getting pushed to “sometime.” One person finally says, “Can we set Thursday at seven for this? I don’t want it hanging over us.” Naming the time takes the dread out of it for both.\nTry: look for one conversation you’ve been putting off “until later,” and offer the other person a specific time for it this week.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#reentry', l: 'Read about re-entry' },

  box: { t: 'Box breathing', m: ['box breathing', 'Box breathing'],
    s: 'Breathe in, hold, breathe out, hold: four counts each.',
    f: 'It slows you down to a few breaths a minute, which is one of the most reliable ways to settle your body.',
    w: 'Nurses, athletes and first responders use it to stay steady. In the Night Garden you can breathe along with a glowing light. It’s a calm practice, not a treatment.',
    x: 'Box breathing has four equal sides: breathe in for four, hold for four, breathe out for four, hold for four. Then repeat.\nThe Night Garden explains why it helps: it slows your breath to a few breaths a minute, and slow, steady breathing like this is one of the most reliable ways to settle the body. In the garden, a glowing light keeps the count for you, and a flower blooms with every round.\nIt’s different from asymmetric breathing in the Calm-Down Kit, where the out-breath is longer. Both are ordinary settling practices. Some people prefer the steady square; others prefer the long exhale. Use whichever feels easier for you.\nAn everyday example: lying awake with a busy mind, someone does four slow rounds of box breathing with their eyes closed, tracing a square in their head. The thoughts are still there, but quieter. The garden isn’t treatment, and it doesn’t replace talking to someone.\nTry: do three rounds of box breathing before your next meal, just to learn the rhythm.',
    u: '/night-garden.html', l: 'Breathe in the Night Garden' },

  regulatefirst: { t: 'Regulate first, negotiate second', m: ['regulate first, negotiate second', 'regulate first', 'autonomic regulation'],
    s: 'Settle your body before you try to settle the disagreement.',
    f: 'A calm body can hear a hard sentence. A revved-up one hears an attack even in a careful one.',
    w: 'It’s a rule that runs through the whole system: check the ladder, use the Calm-Down Kit if you need it, and only then open the topic. It’s why check-ins start with a state check, and why the Kit comes before the treaty session.',
    x: 'This rule came from the founder’s own experience, described on the About page. Thinking tools worked well until the body was past its limit, and then they did nothing at all. So this practice starts with the body instead of the argument.\nSo the order matters. Settle first. Then talk. A calm body can hear a hard sentence. A revved-up body can hear an attack in even a careful one.\nIt shows up across the program: check-ins start with each person naming their state on the ladder; the Calm-Down Kit comes before the treaty session and the Tone Filter; and CALC-01 asks you to check your state before reading any number.\nAn everyday example: someone comes home wanting to sort out a problem right now. Instead, they change clothes, eat, and sit for ten minutes. The same conversation, an hour later, takes half the time and ends kindly.\nTry: before your next important talk, each name your rung on the Nervous System Ladder. If either of you isn’t on safe and social, settle first.',
    u: '/about-in-depth.html#curriculum', l: 'Read where it came from' },

  /* ---------- The nervous system ---------- */

  nervous: { t: 'Your nervous system', m: ['nervous system'],
    s: 'The part of you that speeds up for danger and slows down for rest.',
    f: 'When it’s revved up, small things feel big. Slow breathing and a few calm minutes help it settle, and your thinking follows.',
    w: 'The program treats its states as information, not character: shutting down isn’t a choice, and gearing up isn’t aggression. The Nervous System Ladder and Today’s Weather give you a quick way to name where yours is before a hard moment.',
    x: 'Your nervous system is always quietly asking one question: am I safe right now? The answer shapes how fast you talk, how you hear a comment, and whether you can think clearly.\nThe About page says learning this ended years of treating these reactions as character defects. It also explains why the same sentence can land three different ways depending only on the state of the body hearing it that day.\nAn everyday example: a friend asks, “Are you free Saturday?” On a rested morning, it sounds like an invitation. After a bad night and a stressful call, it can sound like one more demand. The words didn’t change. The body hearing them did.\nThat’s why so many tools here start with a quick state check, and why the program keeps saying: settle first, then talk. It’s information about a moment, not a verdict on a person.\nTry: set a reminder for mid-afternoon and, when it goes off, simply notice: clear, gusty or fogged in?',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#today', l: 'Check in with yours' },

  ladder: { t: 'The Nervous System Ladder', m: ['Nervous System Ladder', 'the ladder'],
    s: 'A thirty-second check of which of three states your body is in right now.',
    f: 'Calm and connected, revved up, or shut down. You name your own rung, never the other person’s.',
    w: 'The three rungs: ventral (safe and social), sympathetic (mobilized, braced) and dorsal (shut down, far away). If either person is mobilized or shut down, it isn’t the moment for a hard talk. Today’s Weather uses the same three as Clear, Gusty and Fogged in.',
    x: 'The ladder has three rungs, and you name your own. It takes about thirty seconds. It isn’t a grade. It just tells you what this moment is good for.\nIt includes: ventral, or safe and social (calm, connected, able to listen); sympathetic, or mobilized (heart up, thoughts fast, on guard); and dorsal, or shut down (flat, far away, words hard to find).\nYou meet it all over the site. CALC-01 asks you to check it before reading a number. Check-ins ask each person to name their rung before the topic. Today’s Weather uses the same three as Clear, Gusty and Fogged in, so you never need the technical words.\nAn everyday example: before a talk about the holidays, one person says, “I’m gusty.” The other says, “I’m clear.” They agree to take a short walk first. The talk goes better for it.\nTry: at dinner tonight, each name your rung in one word. No explanation needed.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#ladder', l: 'Check your rung' },

  ventral: { t: 'Ventral: safe and social', m: ['ventral vagal', 'Safe and social'],
    s: 'The calm, connected state where you can listen and think.',
    f: 'Breath is easy, shoulders aren’t braced, and you can hear something hard without defending yourself.',
    w: 'It’s the top rung of the Nervous System Ladder, Clear in Today’s Weather. It’s the state hard conversations need. You don’t need the term; it just names what calm feels like from the inside.',
    x: 'Ventral is the state where you feel safe enough to be social. It’s where curiosity lives, where jokes land, and where you can hear something hard without bracing.\nToday’s Weather describes it simply: breath is easy, shoulders aren’t braced, and you can hear something hard without defending yourself.\nNobody lives here all the time, and that’s fine. The point isn’t to stay on the top rung. It’s to notice when you’re there, because that’s the best time for the conversations that matter.\nAn everyday example: on a slow Sunday morning, two people are both relaxed and rested. One says, “Can we talk about the summer plans?” The other says, “Sure, now’s good.” That’s the ventral window, and it’s worth using.\nTry: next time you notice you both feel calm and easy, bring up one small thing you’ve been meaning to discuss, rather than waiting for a crisis to force it.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#ladder', l: 'Check your rung' },

  sympathetic: { t: 'Sympathetic: mobilized', m: ['sympathetic activation', 'sympathetic', 'mobilized'],
    s: 'The revved-up, on-guard state: heart up, thoughts fast.',
    f: 'You talk faster, interrupt sooner, and small things start to feel urgent or personal.',
    w: 'It’s the middle rung of the Nervous System Ladder, Gusty in Today’s Weather. It isn’t aggression; it’s a body doing its job. In this state, logic tends to make things harder. A long out-breath, slowing down or a pause helps before any real talk.',
    x: 'The sympathetic state is your body getting ready to act. It’s useful for a deadline or an emergency. It’s harder in a conversation, because it makes everything feel urgent.\nMood Arbitrage lists signs you might see: a tight jaw, rapid speech, narrow eyes, rigid posture, words getting louder or sharper. Part of the person feels unsafe, and arguing with logic tends to make it worse right now.\nChapter I adds that in this state people talk faster and read neutral pauses as stonewalling. That’s pace shifting without anyone meaning it to.\nAn everyday example: someone running late is asked where the car keys went. Their answer comes out sharp. They aren’t angry at the question; their body is already sprinting. A calm “No rush, I’ll look too” helps more than pointing out the tone.\nTry: when you notice your own speech speeding up, slow your next sentence on purpose and breathe out longer than you breathe in.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#ladder', l: 'Check your rung' },

  dorsal: { t: 'Dorsal: shut down', m: ['dorsal vagal', 'shut down', 'shutdown'],
    s: 'The flat, far-away state when your body has run out of steam.',
    f: 'Words are hard to find, answers get short, and you may want to disappear.',
    w: 'It’s the bottom rung of the Nervous System Ladder, Fogged in in Today’s Weather. Shutdown is not calm, and it isn’t a choice. Fixing things won’t help yet; quiet, steady company and a later time will. During a shutdown, stop asking questions.',
    x: 'Dorsal is the state where the body has run out of steam and pulled back to rest. It can look calm from outside. Inside, it often feels heavy, blank or far away.\nMood Arbitrage lists signs: a blank face, slow or no speech, looking away or down, the body feeling heavy, withdrawal. The Check-ins page is clear that shutdown is dysregulation too, not calm.\nQuestions and problem-solving tend to feel like pressure here. What helps is calm, steady company and time.\nAn everyday example from Mood Arbitrage: a person facing a messy kitchen says only, “I don’t know. Whatever.” The other doesn’t push. They say, “The dishes aren’t going anywhere. Neither am I. Let’s just sit for a minute. I’m not mad. I’m just here.”\nTry: agree on a quiet phrase each of you can use when you’re going flat, like “I’m fogged in,” so the other knows to slow down rather than ask more.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#ladder', l: 'Check your rung' },

  vagalbrake: { t: 'The vagal brake', m: ['vagal brake'],
    s: 'Your body’s natural calming brake, which can engage when things feel safe.',
    f: 'After about thirty seconds of steady, calm presence, it can kick in and connection becomes possible again.',
    w: 'Mood Arbitrage leans on this: a lower voice, slower pace and steady company signal safety. That’s why its last step is to hold the new, calmer frequency rather than rushing to the point.',
    x: 'The vagal brake is the body’s own way of easing off the accelerator. When things feel safe, it can slow the heart and settle the system, and connection becomes possible again.\nMood Arbitrage says our bodies pick up whether we feel safe long before we make sense of the words. That’s why a calm voice, a slower pace and steady presence matter more in a tense moment than a clever argument.\nIts four-step sequence ends with holding the new, calmer feeling. After about thirty seconds of steady presence, the brake can kick in. Rushing to your point too soon can undo it.\nAn everyday example: after a tense exchange, one person sits down beside the other, breathes slowly, and says nothing for half a minute. The shoulders beside them drop a little. Only then do they ask, “Want to talk, or want some space first?”\nTry: next time a moment starts to soften, stay quiet and steady for a slow count of thirty before you say anything else.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage' },

  dysregulation: { t: 'Dysregulation', m: ['emotional dysregulation', 'dysregulation'],
    s: 'When your feelings or body get ahead of you and are hard to steady.',
    f: 'A voice getting faster or louder, “always” and “never” arriving, or going blank and wanting to disappear.',
    w: 'Shutdown is dysregulation too; it isn’t calm. The useful signs are early and ordinary. Treat the first as your cue, name your own rather than diagnosing theirs, and regulate before the next sentence: “I’m leaving the conversation. My voice is up.”',
    x: 'Dysregulation means your body and feelings have gotten ahead of you. It happens to everyone. The skill isn’t never getting there; it’s noticing early.\nIt includes signs like these from the Check-ins page: a voice faster, sharper or louder than the topic deserves; the same sentence said again, as if volume will help; “always,” “never” or a label arriving where a fact was; a second topic brought in to win the first; jaw, heat, a tight chest, the urge to leave or to win; going blank or short; and reaching for old messages or a list of evidence.\nWhen you notice one, name your own, not theirs. Diagnosing it in the other person as a way to win only adds heat.\nAn everyday example: mid-talk, someone hears themselves say “you always” for the second time. They stop and say, “I’m leaving the conversation. My voice is up. Back in twenty.”\nTry: choose the one sign from the list you recognize most in yourself, and write it at the top of your Calm-Down Kit.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#signs', l: 'Notice it early' },

  flooded: { t: 'Flooded', m: ['emotional flooding', 'flooded'],
    s: 'So overwhelmed that you can’t really hear or think clearly.',
    f: 'A single deep breath isn’t a plan when you’re flooded. A pause is.',
    w: 'A flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again, and the return shouldn’t slip past the next day. Use the pause to come back to yourself, not to prepare a better case. Say one line (“We’re okay. This is a pause. I’ll be back at 8:40.”), then actually come back.',
    x: 'Flooding is what happens when there’s more coming in than your body can handle. Thinking narrows, hearing gets worse, and even kind words can sound like attacks.\nThe Check-ins page, citing the Gottman Institute, says a flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again. A single deep breath won’t do it. A real pause will.\nWhat you do in the pause matters. It’s for coming back to yourself, not for building a better case. The Family Institute puts it plainly: the timeout is for connection, not for lawyering up.\nAn everyday example: a talk about money floods one person. They say, “We’re okay. This is a pause. I’ll come back at 8:40.” They walk, drink water, and use their kit. The other person doesn’t follow or send a stack of texts. At 8:40, they sit down again.\nTry: agree now, while calm, on a pause of at least twenty minutes whenever either of you says you’re flooded.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regulate', l: 'Read about the pause' },

  coregulate: { t: 'Co-regulating', m: ['co-regulate'],
    s: 'Helping each other settle by being calm together.',
    f: 'Slow breathing side by side, a steady voice, quiet company.',
    w: 'Wired Differently suggests it first when someone is activated, especially for trauma-wired people, before trying to talk. It’s offered, never forced, and it doesn’t replace each person’s own settling (the Calm-Down Kit).',
    x: 'Co-regulating means settling alongside someone. Calm is catching: a steady voice, a slow breath and quiet company can help another body feel safe enough to ease off.\nWired Differently suggests it first when someone is activated, especially for trauma-wired people, whose nervous system may stay on watch. Slow breathing together, or the Calm-Down Kit, comes before talking.\nIt’s always offered, never forced. Some people want closeness when they’re upset; others need space first. Both are fine. Asking is part of it.\nAn everyday example: after a loud noise sets one person on edge, the other sits nearby and breathes slowly without saying much. After a few minutes, the tension eases, and they can talk about the evening. It doesn’t replace each person settling themselves. It helps alongside it.\nTry: ask the person you live with, on a calm day, “When you’re upset, is it better if I stay close or give you space?” Write down the answer.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#trauma', l: 'Read more' },

  /* ---------- Hard conversations: Check-ins ---------- */

  checkins: { t: 'Check-ins', m: ['Check-ins'],
    s: 'A short, agreed talk about one hard topic, at a moment that can hold it.',
    f: 'Twenty to forty minutes, one topic, settled bodies, and a named time to stop and come back.',
    w: 'It follows an order: name the container (time, place, one topic), check states, speaker gives one fact and one impact, listener mirrors before any rebuttal, switch, then a solution that works for both, with an owner and a review. The daily 90-second loop is the small version; this is for the topic that’s been waiting.',
    x: 'A check-in is not a fight with a time on the calendar. It’s a short, agreed talk in a room that can hold it: bodies settled enough to listen, outside noise turned down, and a speaking order that puts acknowledgement before any rebuttal.\nIt includes, in order: name the container (time, place, one topic, the stop rule, the return); regulate; speaker gives one fact and one impact; listener acknowledges all the way through; roles reverse; a rebuttal only if something was inaccurate; a solution that works for both; one repeatable positive; and a close with one specific appreciation.\nAn everyday example: “Can we use twenty minutes at 8, after dinner, for the kitchen thing?” That’s a check-in. “While you’re walking out the door” is an ambush.\nIt can’t replace a clinician or a mediator, and it scores no one.\nTry: pick one small topic that’s been waiting, and suggest a twenty-minute check-in at a named time this week.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read about check-ins' },

  groundrules: { t: 'The ground rules', m: ['check-in ground rules', 'ground rules'],
    s: 'A hard topic is allowed. Overload, blame and criticism of the person are not.',
    f: 'Say it once, before the content: “I am not against you. I don’t think you meant harm.”',
    w: 'The rules: one topic (a second one gets parked); no blame and no criticism of the person; no assumed bad intent on either side; prepare so the room isn’t doing the sorting; notice dysregulation early and pause with a return time. If it heats up, stop and come back to the same topic.',
    x: 'The ground rules keep a hard topic from turning into a trial. They’re short enough to say out loud before you begin, so the room isn’t left guessing.\nIt includes: don’t overload the conversation (one topic); don’t deviate (a second topic gets parked with a date); no blame and no criticism of the person; no negative intent assumed, on either side; prepare, so the room isn’t doing the sorting; and notice dysregulation early, then regulate before the next sentence.\nThe speaker says, once: “I am not against you. I am not saying you are the problem. I do not think you meant harm.” The listener says the other half: “I’m not taking this as an attack until I have mirrored it. If I start defending, stop me.”\nAn everyday example: a talk about a missed pickup starts to drift into last year’s vacation. One person says, “That’s real. It isn’t this meeting. Can we write it down for Sunday?” They return to the pickup.\nTry: read the ground rules together once on a calm day, so they feel familiar before you need them.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read the ground rules' },

  onetopic: { t: 'One topic', m: ['One topic'],
    s: 'Talk about one thing at a time.',
    f: 'If you have five, you have a list, not a meeting.',
    w: 'Before you sit down, sort everything into three piles: today (one item), a system (a job with no owner, which goes to WP-03 or the weekly resync), and can wait (with a named date). Pick the one that, if settled, would make the others smaller.',
    x: 'One topic is the simplest ground rule and the hardest to keep. A check-in that tries to clear the whole relationship in half an hour overloads both bodies, and an overloaded body hears criticism even in a careful sentence.\nSorting helps. Today: what will still be wrong tomorrow if untouched, or what needs a bond signal so nobody is left afraid. A system: a job with no owner or a recurring miss, which goes to WP-03 or the weekly resync. Can wait: real, but not this room, and named with a date.\nThe Check-ins page adds that the urgency you feel is often fear of leaving something unfinished, not proof that every item is equal.\nAn everyday example: someone has five things they want to raise. They write all five, then notice that if bedtime routines were settled, two of the others would shrink. Bedtime becomes the one topic.\nTry: before your next hard talk, write your list, then circle only one item. Put a date beside the rest.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read the ground rules' },

  notagainst: { t: '“I am not against you”', m: ['not against you', 'not against them'],
    s: 'Saying out loud, once, that you’re on the same side.',
    f: '“I am not against you. I am not saying you are the problem. I want this one thing sorted.”',
    w: 'Leaving it unsaid lets the nervous system write a worse story than you meant. The listener can say the other half: “I’m not taking this as an attack until I’ve mirrored it.” Once is enough; repeating it after every sentence starts to sound like a script.',
    x: 'This line does something simple and important: it tells the other person’s nervous system that the conversation is not an attack, before the hard part starts.\nThe Check-ins page gives the full version: “I am not against you. I am not saying you are the problem. I do not think you meant harm. I want this one thing sorted, and I can hear how it landed.” WP-09 and Mood Arbitrage both point back to it.\nOnce is enough. Saying it after every sentence starts to sound like a script. Leaving it out lets the other person fill the silence with a worse story than the one you meant.\nAn everyday example: before raising the phone bill, one person says, “I’m not against you, and I don’t think you meant anything by it. I just want to sort out how we share the plan.” The other’s shoulders drop. The talk that follows is about the bill, not about them.\nTry: write the line in your own words, so it sounds like you when you say it.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read the ground rules' },

  parkit: { t: 'Parking a topic', m: ['Park it', 'park the rest', 'parked'],
    s: 'Writing a second topic down for later, so this talk stays on one.',
    f: '“That’s real. It isn’t this meeting. I’m writing it down for Sunday. We’re still on the sink.”',
    w: 'The person who raised it isn’t wrong, just early. Don’t explain why their point matters less. And a parked item needs a date: one with no date is a dismissal wearing good manners.',
    x: 'Parking is how a check-in stays on one topic without dismissing anything. The second topic is written down, given a date, and left for its own time.\nThe Check-ins page says a second topic usually shows up just as the first one starts to land. The person who raises it isn’t wrong to have it. They’re early.\nThe date is what makes it honest. A parked item with no date is a dismissal wearing good manners. And don’t explain why their point is less important; just say it isn’t this meeting.\nAn everyday example: halfway through a talk about the dishes, one person says, “And you never plan anything for weekends.” The other says, “That’s real. It isn’t this meeting. I’m writing it down for Sunday. We’re still on the sink.” On Sunday, the weekend topic gets its own twenty minutes.\nTry: keep a small “parked” list on your phone or the fridge, with a date next to each item, and look at it at your weekly closing.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read about parking' },

  complaint: { t: 'Criticism or complaint', m: ['criticism of the person', 'complain, don\'t blame', 'complain, don’t blame'],
    s: 'A complaint is about the event. Criticism is about the person.',
    f: 'Criticism: “You never help. You’re careless.” A complaint: “The sink was full at 7. I need it cleared by 9.”',
    w: 'A complaint can be owned and fixed tonight; a verdict on someone’s character can’t. Blame in a soft voice is still blame (“I’m not angry, but you always…”). If a sentence needs a label, it isn’t ready: take it through WP-09, then the Signal Translator.',
    x: 'The difference is simple but powerful. A complaint describes an event and a need. Criticism describes a person’s character. You can fix an event tonight. You can’t fix a character by morning.\nThe Check-ins page uses Gottman’s softened start as the line: complain, don’t blame. Describe what happened, don’t judge the person. Start from what you need, not from what they are.\nTone doesn’t turn one into the other. “I’m not angry, but you always…” is criticism wearing a calm voice.\nAn everyday example: “You never think about anyone but yourself” is criticism. “The trash wasn’t taken out before pickup, and I felt a bit unseen since I’d mentioned it twice. Can we pick a fixed day for it?” is a complaint. It comes straight from WP-09’s worked example.\nTry: next time you feel a “you always” coming, swap it for “yesterday, at…” and one specific event.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read the ground rules' },

  softstart: { t: 'A softened start', m: ['softened start', 'Soften the start', 'soft start'],
    s: 'Opening a hard topic gently, from what you need rather than what they are.',
    f: '“I need the kitchen clear in the morning” instead of “you always leave a mess.”',
    w: 'It’s the first of Gottman’s six skills that Check-ins draws on: complain without blaming, describe without judging, speak from “I.” For a highly sensitive listener, a soft start and a quiet room matter even more.',
    x: 'How a hard conversation starts often decides how it goes. A soft start makes it easier for the other person to stay open; a harsh one puts them on guard from the first word.\nIt includes three habits from Gottman’s work, as the Check-ins page lists them: complain without blaming; describe without judging; and speak from “I,” not “you always.”\nWired Differently notes it matters even more for some listeners. For a highly sensitive or trauma-wired person, a raised voice or sharp edge can land before the words do. A soft start and a quiet room help the words get through.\nAn everyday example: instead of “Why is there never any milk?”, one person says, “I noticed we ran out of milk again. I’d love for us to have a spare. Could we add it to the list?”\nTry: before bringing up something that bothers you, write your first sentence starting with “I noticed” or “I need,” and read it once out loud.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Read the order' },

  intentimpact: { t: 'Intent and impact', m: ['Intent does not cancel impact', 'impact from intent'],
    s: 'What you meant and how it landed can both be true.',
    f: '“I didn’t mean harm. I can see it landed as harm. I’m not asking you to cancel that.”',
    w: '“I didn’t mean it that way” isn’t a request for them to stop feeling it. Say the no-harm line before the fact, not after they flinch, and save the defense of your intent until they’ve been heard. Otherwise it’s the rebuttal arriving early.',
    x: 'Intent is what you meant. Impact is how it landed. Both are real, and neither cancels the other. Most static between differently wired people is a missed signal, not a plot.\nThe Check-ins page suggests a three-part line: “I did not mean harm. I can see it landed as harm. I am not asking you to cancel that.” Then stop, and let them mirror it.\nTiming matters. Say the no-harm line before the fact, not after they flinch. Defending your intent before they’ve been heard is a rebuttal arriving early.\nAn everyday example: someone jokes about their partner’s cooking in front of friends. It was meant lightly, and it stung. Later, instead of “It was just a joke,” they say, “I didn’t mean to embarrass you. I can see it did. I’m sorry.” The hurt is allowed to be real.\nTry: next time someone says something you did hurt, say “I can see it landed that way” before you explain anything.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Read the ground rules' },

  saferoom: { t: 'A room that can hold it', m: ['A safe time', 'A safe environment', 'A stable connection', 'The room has to be safe'],
    s: 'A hard conversation needs the right conditions before the right words.',
    f: 'Not at 10:50pm when you’re both nearly empty. The topic was fine; the room was wrong.',
    w: 'Four things at once: a safe time (named ahead, not in a spike); a safe environment (private, phones down, no children listening); outside factors handled (hunger, pain, alcohol, deadlines); and a stable connection (a short signal the bond is okay). If one is missing, postpone with a return time.',
    x: 'The Check-ins page says hard conversations fail less from the words than from the conditions around them. If one condition is missing, postpone. Postponing with a return time isn’t avoidance.\nIt includes four conditions: a safe time (named ahead, not in or just after a spike, not when someone is about to leave); a safe environment (private, no children listening, phones face down, both seated or both standing); outside factors handled (hunger, thirst, pain, alcohol, a deadline); and a stable connection (a short signal that the bond is okay, if welcome).\nThe page is clear about substances: if either person has been drinking or is otherwise altered, the check-in waits.\nAn everyday example: a talk planned for after dinner is moved from the cluttered kitchen to the sofa, phones in another room, after both have eaten. Same topic, very different room.\nTry: before your next planned talk, check the four conditions out loud and fix the easiest missing one first.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#room', l: 'Read about the room' },

  bondsignal: { t: 'A bond signal', m: ['bond signal', 'bond line', 'the bond is ok'],
    s: 'A short sign that the relationship is safe, before the hard part.',
    f: '“We are okay. This is one topic, not a verdict on us. I’m not leaving this.”',
    w: 'Some people can’t hear a hard fact until they know the bond is still there; for them silence feels like being left. Others need a little space first. Both can be true in one pair. A hug or a hand is optional, never owed, and off if anyone doesn’t want it.',
    x: 'A bond signal is a short reassurance that the relationship isn’t on trial. It goes before the topic, if it’s welcome.\nSome people can’t hear a hard fact until they know the bond is still there. For them, silence can feel like the start of being left. Others can’t think until they have a little space. Both can be true in the same pair.\nThe Check-ins page is careful: a hug or a hand is optional, never owed, and off if anyone doesn’t want it, or if you’re not in private. For an anxious listener, say the bond line once and mean it; repeating it after every sentence starts to sound like a script.\nAn everyday example: before a talk about money, one person says, “We’re okay. This is one topic, not a verdict on us. I’m not leaving this.” The other nods, and they begin.\nTry: ask each other, on a calm day, “Before a hard talk, what helps you feel we’re okay?” and remember the answer.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#room', l: 'Read about the room' },

  speakerlistener: { t: 'Speaker-Listener', m: ['Speaker-Listener'],
    s: 'Taking turns: one person speaks in short pieces, the other repeats back before replying.',
    f: 'Pieces of about fifteen to thirty seconds, then a check: “Did I get that?”',
    w: 'It’s one of three published methods Check-ins puts in one order, with Imago dialogue and Gottman’s six skills. The listener isn’t preparing a rebuttal while the other is still talking. Practice it once on a small topic before using it on a loaded one.',
    x: 'Speaker-Listener slows a hard talk down on purpose, so each person is heard before anyone answers. The Check-ins page credits the Family Institute at Northwestern, describing the technique in Markman, Stanley and Blumberg.\nThe speaker talks in short pieces. The listener paraphrases before adding anything, asks whether they got it, and only then do the roles reverse. The listener is not preparing a rebuttal while the other person is still talking.\nShort pieces are the point. About fifteen to thirty seconds, then a check, so neither person stockpiles a case.\nAn everyday example: “I felt rushed this morning because the lunches weren’t made.” “What I heard is that the morning felt rushed because lunches weren’t ready. Did I get that?” “Yes.” Only then does the listener share their side. The Family Institute notes the order is easier in a tense talk if you’ve already used it when nothing is at stake.\nTry: practice one round tonight on something tiny, like what to have for dinner, just to learn the rhythm.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Read the order' },

  gottman: { t: 'Gottman’s six skills', m: ['Gottman\'s six skills', 'Gottman’s six skills'],
    s: 'Six research-based habits for handling conflict well.',
    f: 'Soften the start, accept influence, repair if it spikes, soothe yourself, soothe them if welcome, and compromise.',
    w: 'They come from the Gottman Institute and are one of the three methods Check-ins puts in order. Turning Toward draws on the same research for everyday closeness: bids, love maps, and fondness and admiration.',
    x: 'These six skills come from the Gottman Institute and are one of three published methods the Check-ins page puts in one order.\nIt includes: soften the start (complain without blaming, speak from “I”); accept influence (let the other person’s view change your plan); repair if it spikes; soothe yourself; soothe the other person, if welcome; and compromise.\nThe Check-ins page draws on them throughout. Gottman is also the source for the note that a flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again.\nAn everyday example: a talk about vacation plans heats up. One person repairs: “Sorry, that came out sharp. Let me try again.” They breathe, soften the start, and ask what matters most to the other. The plan that follows includes a bit of both. Turning Toward uses the same research for everyday closeness: bids, love maps, and fondness and admiration. The site notes the studies mostly involve couples, and applies the ideas more widely.\nTry: choose one of the six to practice this week, and tell the other person which one you picked.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Read the order' },

  acknowledgement: { t: 'Acknowledgement', m: ['acknowledgement before any rebuttal', 'proof of receipt', 'acknowledgement', 'Mirror first', 'mirrored'],
    s: 'Showing you heard them, before you answer.',
    f: '“What I heard is: the dishes were still there this morning, and it felt like the evening was left on you. Did I get that?”',
    w: 'It shows the message arrived; it isn’t agreement. “I hear you, but” cancels out everything before it; use “and.” If they correct you, mirror the correction. Stay there until they say you have it; two or three short turns is normal.',
    x: 'Acknowledgement shows the message arrived. It isn’t agreement, and it isn’t a therapy voice. It simply shows the other person that what they said arrived.\nThe Check-ins page is precise about it. “I hear you, but” cancels out everything before it; use “and.” If they correct you, mirror the correction without defending the first version. Stay until they say you have it; two or three short turns is normal.\nIt works differently across wirings. For an autistic person, a literal repeat is respect, not coldness. For ADHD, writing the mirror in one line can help. For a trauma-wired person, slow down and ask “Was that close?” rather than performing perfect recall.\nAn everyday example: “Got it. Not the dishes in general. Last night’s, still in the sink at 7. Did I get that now?” “Yes.” That short exchange changes the whole talk.\nTry: in your next disagreement, say “What I heard is…” before you say anything about your own view.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Read about acknowledgement' },

  validation: { t: 'Validation', m: ['validation', 'validating before arguing'],
    s: 'Saying that their reaction can make sense, even if you see it differently.',
    f: '“That can make sense. If I came down to a job I thought was done, I’d be frustrated too.”',
    w: 'It comes after the mirror, not instead of it. It doesn’t mean you intended the harm, and it doesn’t mean theirs is the only reading. “You’re too sensitive” and “that isn’t what happened” do the opposite: they shut the listening down.',
    x: 'Validation is the next small step after the mirror. It says: your reaction can make sense, given what happened. It doesn’t say you’re the only one who’s right.\nThe Check-ins page warns that it’s easy to fake. “That can make sense” means their reading is possible from the facts. It doesn’t mean you intended harm, and it doesn’t mean their reading is the only one.\nEmpathy goes one step further, and it has a limit. Don’t assign a feeling they didn’t name. For someone with alexithymia (who finds it hard to name their own feelings), being handed a feeling-word can feel like a correction. “You said it landed as being left with it” is safer than “You seem hurt.”\nAn everyday example: “If I came down to a job I thought was done, I’d be frustrated too. You don’t have to be wrong for me to have a different picture. I’m not answering yet.”\nTry: next time you disagree, find one honest way their reaction makes sense, and say only that before giving your view.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Read about acknowledgement' },

  rebuttal: { t: 'A proper rebuttal', m: ['proper rebuttal', 'a rebuttal'],
    s: 'Adding your side without erasing what you just heard.',
    f: '“I got the part about last night’s dishes. My part: I said I’d do them and didn’t. One fact I want added: I did the lunch boxes.”',
    w: 'Four moves, in order: restate their fact in one line; own your part without a discount; add one fact they may not have (one event, no “always”); separate impact from intent, then stop. If it needs a label like “lazy” or “too much,” it’s a verdict, not a rebuttal.',
    x: 'A rebuttal is allowed. Accuracy matters. What isn’t allowed is a rebuttal that pretends the acknowledgement never happened, or treats the other person’s feeling as the thing to disprove.\nIt includes four moves, in this order: restate their fact in one line, so they hear it survived; own the part that’s yours without a discount; add one fact they may not have (one event, a time, no “always”); and separate impact from intent, then stop.\nThe Check-ins page shows the difference. “I hear you, but you never notice what I did do” erases the mirror. “I got the part about last night’s dishes. My part: I said I’d do them and didn’t. I did do the lunch boxes. I’m not asking you to feel differently about the sink” keeps it.\nAfter a rebuttal, be quiet. Their turn is another acknowledgement, not an instant verdict.\nTry: write your next rebuttal as four short lines before you say it, and cross out any line that contains a label.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#rebuttal', l: 'Read about rebuttals' },

  acceptinfluence: { t: 'Accepting influence', m: ['Accept influence', 'accepting influence'],
    s: 'Letting the other person’s view actually change your plan.',
    f: 'Their picture is useful information, not an attack to push back.',
    w: 'It’s one of Gottman’s skills and the heart of a solution that works for both: write both needs down first, then find one move that meets the minimum of each, not the maximum of the louder one.',
    x: 'Accepting influence means letting the other person’s view actually shape the outcome, not just listening politely and then doing what you planned. Gottman names it as a skill, alongside compromise.\nThe Check-ins page says the other person’s picture is useful information, not an attack to fight off. A solution that meets only the faster or louder person’s need will be called peace and felt as a loss.\nThe practical step is to write both needs down before either solution. One person may need the bond confirmed; the other may need a written plan, quiet or space first.\nAn everyday example: one person wants to repaint the living room this weekend. The other says weekends are their only rest. Instead of pushing, the first person asks what rest looks like, and they agree to paint one wall on Saturday morning and leave Sunday free.\nTry: in your next decision together, ask “What would make this work for you?” and change one thing in your plan because of the answer.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#both', l: 'Read about solutions' },

  bothsides: { t: 'A solution for both', m: ['both-sides solution', 'A solution that works for both'],
    s: 'An answer that meets both people’s needs, not just the louder one’s.',
    f: '“You need the kitchen not to be a surprise. I need the evening close not to be a test. Move: a note on the fridge. Owner: me. We look again Sunday.”',
    w: 'Five parts, or it’s just a wish: need A, mirrored; need B, mirrored; one action covering the minimum of each; a named owner and a clock time; a review. Some topics take more than one sitting; a seven-day experiment with a date isn’t failure. If it’s really about ownership, move it to WP-03.',
    x: 'Understanding isn’t the close. A check-in that ends on “I feel heard” with no next step tends to have the same conversation next week.\nIt includes five parts: need A, in their words, mirrored; need B, in their words, mirrored; one action covering the minimum of each; a named owner and a clock time; and a review, like Sunday or the next weekly closing. If one is missing, the Check-ins page says, you have a wish.\nSome topics won’t settle in one sitting. A temporary experiment with a date, “for seven days, not forever,” is not failure. Forcing a permanent answer because one person can’t stand leaving it open can produce a promise the other can’t keep.\nAn everyday example: one needs the bond confirmed after work; the other needs twenty quiet minutes first. The move: a quick hello and hug at the door, then twenty minutes apart, then dinner together. They review it on Sunday.\nTry: after your next agreement, check it has all five parts, and add whichever is missing.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#both', l: 'Read about solutions' },

  negpos: { t: 'Turning a negative into a positive', m: ['negative into a positive', 'Negatives to positives'],
    s: 'Using what went wrong to build one small habit that works.',
    f: 'The sink with no owner after 9 becomes a ten-minute close together, or a note on the fridge.',
    w: 'It isn’t looking on the bright side. Keep the cost in plain words; ask what the miss was pointing at; turn it into one repeatable action; and name one thing the other person already does that counts. If you leave with only the sting, the cost was wasted. If you leave with only a compliment, the problem comes back.',
    x: 'This is about making a miss useful. The Check-ins page says it plainly: a miss is information about the system. Used well, the last minutes of a check-in turn it into one repeatable action.\nIt includes: keep the cost in plain words; ask what the miss was pointing at (a need, a missing owner, a tired time of day, a hint that should have been an ask); turn it into one positive action you can repeat; name one specific thing the other person already does that counts; and put the action where the program can see it, like the weekly resync or the treaty.\nAn everyday example: “The negative was a sink with no owner after 9. The positive we’re keeping is a ten-minute close, or a note. Thank you for saying it as a fact instead of as a verdict on me.”\nThe positive should be something you can do, not a mood. “A written close of the kitchen” is a positive. “Try to be more considerate” isn’t.\nTry: after the next thing that goes wrong, ask together, “What’s one small habit this is pointing us toward?”',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#leverage', l: 'Read how' },

  toxicpos: { t: 'Not toxic positivity', m: ['toxic positivity'],
    s: 'Shifting the mood isn’t pretending everything is fine.',
    f: 'A pile of dishes doesn’t become a gift because you rename it.',
    w: 'Mood Arbitrage and Check-ins both say this plainly: the negative stays visible. What changes is the carrier, the feeling in the room, so the real point can land. Test your sentence in the Signal Translator so the shift doesn’t sand the fact down into a compliment.',
    x: 'Toxic positivity is pretending a problem isn’t there, or hurrying past it to a nice feeling. The program asks for something different: keep the problem in view, and change the mood around it so it can be talked about.\nMood Arbitrage calls this changing the carrier. You calm your own body on purpose, so the feeling between you settles, and what you want to say can land safely. The negative stays visible.\nThe Check-ins page adds a warning in the other direction: don’t sand the fact down into “a little miscommunication.” If you leave with only a compliment, you’ve hidden the cost, and it will come back.\nAn everyday example: “It’s fine, don’t worry about it!” said through gritted teeth is toxic positivity. “That was hard, and I’m glad we’re talking about it calmly. What would help next time?” is the real thing.\nTry: next time you want to smooth something over, name the problem in one plain sentence first, then add something warm.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#leverage', l: 'Read more' },

  regroup: { t: 'Regrouping', m: ['regroup'],
    s: 'Coming back after a pause to the same topic, calmly.',
    f: '“I’m back. The topic is still the sink. I’m not against you. My state is ___.”',
    w: 'A regroup is not a rematch. Don’t open with what they did during the pause, or with a new topic you thought of while you were gone. If your body isn’t back yet, say so and set a new time; the clock arriving isn’t the same as being ready.',
    x: 'A regroup is how a pause ends. It’s the moment you come back to the same topic, in a calmer body, and start again from the fact.\nThe Check-ins page gives a return line: “I’m back. The topic is still the sink. I am not against you. My state is ___. If I’m not settled enough, we set a new time instead of pretending the clock won.”\nSome things don’t belong in a regroup: what they did during the pause, a new topic you thought of while away, or a better-prepared case. The pause is over when you’re back on the original topic, not when someone has won the argument about the pause.\nAn everyday example: after twenty minutes apart, one person comes back, sits down and says, “I’m back. Still on the car booking. I’m clear now. Can you tell me again what you needed?” They pick up exactly where they left off.\nTry: write your own return line on your Calm-Down Kit, next to your signal line.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regroup', l: 'Read about regrouping' },

  repair: { t: 'Repair', m: ['repair'],
    s: 'Coming back after a miss, and putting things right in a specific way.',
    f: 'Not “let’s communicate better,” but the sentence you wish you’d used, and one change for next time.',
    w: 'Repair is one of Gottman’s six skills, and one of the situations you can pick in the Signal Translator. The apex score in CALC-01 counts it, because a home can share work evenly and still not be repairing. After a talk squeals anyway, the Carrier Wave Decoder helps with the moment it slipped.',
    x: 'Repair is what happens after something goes wrong: the conversation squealed, a word landed badly, a promise slipped. It’s how two people come back to each other.\nThe program asks for repair that’s specific. Not “let’s communicate better,” but the sentence you wish you’d used, and one change for next time. The Carrier Wave Decoder helps with the moment it slipped; the Signal Translator helps with the better sentence.\nIt matters enough to count. CALC-01’s apex score includes how often you retuned after friction, because a home can share work evenly and still not be repairing. Solvency high and apex low means friction is being swallowed, not talked through.\nAn everyday example: the morning after a sharp exchange, one person says, “Last night I said you never listen. What I meant was I felt rushed. Next time I’ll ask for five minutes instead.”\nTry: think of one recent moment that went sideways, and offer one specific sentence of repair today.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#translator', l: 'Read about repair' },

  talkingnotes: { t: 'Talking notes', m: ['talking notes'],
    s: 'The few sentences you might say, written down before anyone is waiting.',
    f: 'You build them in the Signal Translator and save one page to take into the room.',
    w: 'One sentence is light; two can fit if both are small; four is a list, not a conversation. You don’t owe the whole list. Start with one, stop, let them say what they heard. Leave any scores at home, and never read a score out loud.',
    x: 'Talking notes are your possible sentences, written calmly before a hard talk. They live in the Signal Translator, and you save them as one page to bring into the room.\nBuilding them follows a few steps from the Check-ins page. Write everything you’re tempted to raise; sort it into today, a system, and can wait; set up both people and the room in the translator; translate each sentence you might say; read the load note; then save the PDF.\nThe load note keeps it kind. One sentence is light. Two can fit if both are small and you’re both settled. Three is getting heavy. Four is a list, not a conversation. You don’t owe the whole list.\nAn everyday example: someone brings a page with two sentences into a check-in. They say the first, stop, and listen. The second can wait for another day.\nTry: before your next check-in, write just one sentence you want to say, and test it in the translator first.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#notes', l: 'How to build them' },

  stonewalling: { t: 'Stonewalling', m: ['stonewalling'],
    s: 'Shutting a conversation down by going silent or walking away with no word.',
    f: 'Leaving mid-conflict without saying why reads as walking out, however it’s meant.',
    w: 'The program’s answer is the signal line: name the state, the length and the return, so a pause isn’t mistaken for stonewalling. Chapter I also notes that a revved-up person can read an ordinary pause as stonewalling when it isn’t.',
    x: 'Stonewalling means going silent or leaving without a word, so the other person is left talking to a wall. It often isn’t meant as punishment. Many people do it when they’re overwhelmed and can’t find words.\nThe trouble is how it looks from the other side. WP-11 says leaving mid-conflict without saying why reads as walking out, however it’s meant. For someone who fears being left, silence can feel like the start of it.\nThe fix is not to force yourself to keep talking. It’s to leave with a sentence: name the state, the length and the return. That turns a wall into a door. The other side matters too. Chapter I notes that when someone is revved up, they can read an ordinary pause as stonewalling. Not every silence is a shutdown.\nAn everyday example: “I’m going quiet because I’m overwhelmed, not because I don’t care. I’ll be back in twenty minutes.” The other person can breathe instead of chasing.\nTry: agree on your signal lines together, so a pause from either of you is never a mystery.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'Read about the signal line' },

  defensiveness: { t: 'Defensiveness', m: ['defensiveness'],
    s: 'Jumping to protect yourself before you’ve really heard the other person.',
    f: '“I know, I know, it was a terrible day and I just couldn’t…” said before anything is even asked.',
    w: 'It’s usually a body on guard, not a character flaw. The Check-ins order helps: mirror first, rebut later. As a listener you can even say “If I start defending, stop me.” Mood Arbitrage shows how to soften the room so there’s less to defend against.',
    x: 'Defensiveness is the reflex to protect yourself before you’ve fully heard what’s being said. It usually comes from a body on guard, not from a bad character.\nMood Arbitrage gives a familiar picture: tight shoulders, a sharp voice, and “I know, I know, it was a terrible day and I just couldn’t…” said before anyone has asked anything. The body is bracing for criticism.\nThe Check-ins order helps by taking the pressure off. The listener mirrors first and answers later. You can even ask for help: “If I start defending, stop me.” And if your answer is already crowding your listening, you can say so and take a minute.\nAn everyday example: when a partner mentions the overflowing trash can, the other feels the urge to list everything they did today. Instead they say, “Give me a second, I want to hear this properly,” then mirror what was said.\nTry: notice the moment you start preparing your answer while the other person is still talking, and bring your attention back to their words.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Read about acknowledgement' },

  contempt: { t: 'Contempt', m: ['contempt'],
    s: 'Treating someone as beneath you, with mockery, sneering or eye-rolling.',
    f: 'Gottman’s research singles it out as the most damaging of the conflict patterns he studied.',
    w: 'Turning Toward’s guard against it is remembering, on purpose, what you like and admire about someone. One caution from Wired Differently: an eye roll or grunt can be a tic, not contempt, so respond to the words.',
    x: 'Contempt goes beyond anger. It’s a message of “I’m above you,” carried by mockery, sneering, sarcasm or rolling eyes. Turning Toward notes that Gottman’s research singles it out as the most damaging of the conflict patterns he studied.\nThe guard against it is fondness and admiration: deliberately remembering what you like, respect and admire about someone. In a hard stretch, attention narrows to what’s going wrong. Remembering the good widens it again.\nWired Differently adds an important caution. An eye roll or a grunt during a serious talk can be a tic, especially with Tourette syndrome, and stress can make tics more frequent. Respond to the words, not the tic. If someone tells you a gesture was a tic, believe them.\nAn everyday example: in a stressful month, one person catches themselves sighing loudly at everything their partner says. They stop and write down one thing they admire about them, then say it out loud.\nTry: once this week, name one quality you admire in someone close to you, and one moment when you saw it.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#fondness', l: 'Read about fondness' },

  carrier: { t: 'The carrier wave', m: ['Carrier Wave Decoder', 'carrier wave', 'carrier from the payload'],
    s: 'The tone that carries a message, as opposed to the words themselves.',
    f: '“You never help” can be carrying “I’m exhausted and invisible.”',
    w: 'Chapter I separates the carrier (the tone) from the payload (what’s being said). Most household conflict isn’t about content; it’s a carrier the other person can’t receive. The Decoder is a guided tool for the moment a talk starts to squeal: decode, check your state, retune, and try again.',
    x: 'The Program Overview describes Chapter I’s idea like this: every message has a payload (the thing being said) and a carrier (the tone carrying it). Most household conflict isn’t really about content. It’s one person hearing static where a message was meant.\nThe Decoder walks you through one moment that squealed, step by step. It includes: check your state; a one-minute retune (a long exhale, in for 4 and out for 6, or “look around” if you’ve gone flat); decode the moment across pace, register and urgency; a tone check on your draft, built from fact, feeling and ask; and, if something keeps coming back, a finding with a plan.\nAn everyday example: “You never help” arrives. Decoding it, the carrier may be saying “I’m exhausted and invisible.” Answering that, rather than the words, changes the next line.\nIt’s a reflection aid, not a clinical tool, and it measures nothing about either person. Its notes stay in your browser, so anyone else using that browser could see them.\nTry: pick one recent moment that turned sharp and run just the “what was out of sync?” step for it.',
    u: '/carrier-wave-decoder.html', l: 'Open the Decoder' },

  findings: { t: 'Findings and action plans', m: ['corrective action plans', 'corrective action plan', 'Material weakness', 'Significant deficiency'],
    s: 'A calm way to log a problem that keeps coming back, and a plan to fix it.',
    f: 'A finding describes the arrangement, never a person, and a plan doesn’t start until you’ve both had a say.',
    w: 'Four levels, borrowed from audit: an observation (a one-off, just noted), a deficiency (a quick fix with an owner), a significant deficiency (a full plan with a date, reviewed weekly) and a material weakness (your usual repairs aren’t catching it; reviewed monthly, and it may be worth seeing a counselor). Each is written as condition, criteria, cause and effect.',
    x: 'Findings borrow an auditor’s habit for a problem that keeps coming back: write it down plainly, agree on it, and give it a plan. The finding always describes the arrangement, never a person.\nIt includes four levels: an observation (a one-off, often already fixed, just noted); a deficiency (could recur, small effect, a quick fix with a named owner); a significant deficiency (recurring or clearly felt, a full plan with a date and a test, reviewed at the weekly close); and a material weakness (your usual repairs aren’t catching it, reviewed monthly with WP-04, and it may be worth seeing a counselor).\nFrom deficiency up, each is written as condition (what happened), criteria (what was expected), cause (what in the arrangement allowed it) and effect (what it cost). No character words allowed.\nThe other person responds first, in their own words: agree, partly agree or disagree. A plan closes only after it has held through one monthly review, and closed findings become strengths.\nTry: take one small repeat problem and write just its condition and criteria, in one line each, with no adjectives.',
    u: '/carrier-wave-decoder.html', l: 'Open the Decoder' },

  translator: { t: 'The Signal Translator', m: ['Signal Translator'],
    s: 'A tool for testing how a sentence may land before you say it.',
    f: 'Pick how each of you is wired, type your sentence, and see a version you’re both more likely to hear the same way.',
    w: 'It also takes the relationship, the channel, each person’s state, the situation and the room. It’s a pre-flight check for your own next line, never a score to read out at someone. Every reading says “may,” because people vary. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'The Signal Translator is a preparation tool. It works best before a conversation, while you can still choose the room. The page itself says: if it’s already a fight, don’t open it in their face.\nIt walks through a few steps: who’s talking to whom (each person’s wiring); the relationship, channel and the listener’s state; the situation and the room, plus what each person needs; then the sentence you plan to say. You can add an optional plain-facts note and one reply they’ve already given.\nIt looks for patterns in the words, like hints, sarcasm, vague timing and labels, that often cause static for particular wirings. It can’t hear tone, see faces or know your history, and it can’t diagnose anyone. It scores the wording, never the person.\nAn everyday example: someone types “We need to talk later.” For an anxious listener, the translator notes this may land as something being wrong, and suggests leading with “nothing’s wrong” and a real time.\nTry: test one ordinary text you’re about to send today, and read what it says about how it may land.',
    u: '/signal-translator.html', l: 'Try the Signal Translator' },

  reader: { t: 'The Conversation Reader', m: ['Conversation Reader'],
    s: 'Paste a text thread and see where it turned.',
    f: 'It shows what each of you may be hearing, and a calmer way to answer.',
    w: 'It watches for things like good news met with a flat reply, one of the most common ways people drift apart without meaning to. It runs in your browser and nothing you paste is saved.',
    x: 'The Reader takes a pasted thread, like a text chat, a chat export or an email exchange, and shows where it warmed up, what each person may be hearing, and a calmer way to answer. You can also type a remembered talk as “Me:” and “Them:” lines.\nIt looks for patterns in the words: “always” and “never,” sentences about who someone is, dismissals, sarcasm markers, capitals, apologies and other repair attempts, clear asks, questions that may not have been answered, and the past being brought in. It can also check a reply as you draft it.\nIt can’t hear tone, and it doesn’t know your in-jokes or what either of you meant. The same words can be affectionate between two people and cutting between two others. Use it to slow down, never as proof of who was right.\nTry: paste a recent thread that left you uneasy, and look only at the first place it marks as turning.',
    u: '/conversation-reader.html', l: 'Open the Reader' },

  freq: { t: 'Frequency Calibration', m: ['Frequency Calibration'],
    s: 'Maps how often each of you naturally needs to check in on five parts of life.',
    f: 'Money, recovery after stress, decisions, talking, and values. Where you’re out of step shows up plainly.',
    w: 'Mismatched rhythms are information, not failure, and it isn’t a compatibility test. The fix is usually a coherence mechanism: an agreed rhythm, like a weekly money check or a set return time, that lets two different speeds work together.',
    x: 'Frequency Calibration asks each person to write how often they naturally want to check in on five parts of shared life, in plain terms like “every two days” or “weekly.” Then it shows the gaps side by side.\nFor each gap, it suggests a coherence mechanism and a tool to support it. It suggests starting with gaps in stress recovery (autonomic), then communication, then decisions, money and values. It also suggests re-running the audit each quarter, since jobs, health and seasons change people’s rhythms.\nIt is clear about what it isn’t: not a diagnostic tool, not a compatibility test, and not a prediction about the relationship. Large gaps are normal in every kind of relationship.\nAn everyday example: one person likes to look at spending weekly; the other, monthly. Instead of arguing each time, they agree on a short money check on the first Sunday of each month, with a quick glance in between if something big comes up.\nTry: each write down, separately, how often you’d like to talk about money. Compare, and pick one shared rhythm to try for a month.',
    u: '/tools/frequency-calibration.html', l: 'Open Frequency Calibration' },

  framework: { t: 'The Frequency Framework', m: ['Frequency Framework'],
    s: 'Why two reasonable people can end up in a fight neither of them started.',
    f: 'You want to decide about the vacation now; they want a few days. Neither is stalling or rushing.',
    w: 'It treats a relationship as a set of rhythms running at five different speeds: financial, autonomic, decisional, communicational and philosophical. The friction lives in the mismatch, the beat frequency, and the fix is agreed rhythms, not changing who you are.',
    x: 'The Frequency Framework takes Chapter I’s radio picture and applies it to everyday rhythms. Every relationship is a set of rhythms running side by side: money, stress recovery, decisions, talking and values.\nWhen two rhythms don’t match, the gap shows up as friction and feeling out of sync. The Framework calls that the beat frequency. It stresses that the mismatch isn’t a failure of compatibility. It’s information.\nThe fix is coherence mechanisms: clear, spoken agreements about when, how and how often you’ll sync up. You don’t need to change your natural speed. You need a shared rhythm both speeds can lock onto.\nA small scene from the page: Saturday morning. One person wants to decide about the vacation now, while it’s in front of them. The other wants a few days. Neither is stalling or rushing. They agree to decide on Wednesday evening, and the pressure drops for both. The page is also honest that these are pictures, not claims about physics or physiology.\nTry: think of one decision you often disagree about the timing of, and agree on a set day to decide it.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#five', l: 'Read the Framework' },

  fivefreq: { t: 'The five frequencies', m: ['five frequencies', 'Financial Frequency', 'Autonomic Frequency', 'Decisional Frequency', 'Communicational Frequency', 'Philosophical Frequency'],
    s: 'Five parts of shared life that each run on their own natural rhythm.',
    f: 'You might like to check the budget weekly and they monthly. That’s a rhythm gap, not a character flaw.',
    w: 'The five: financial (bills, pay, spending), autonomic (how long your body takes to reset after stress), decisional (how long you need before committing), communicational (how often you need to check in), and philosophical (how often you revisit values). Frequency Calibration measures yours.',
    x: 'Each of the five is a different part of life with its own natural rhythm. Seeing them separately helps you spot where the friction really lives.\nIt includes: financial (how often bills, pay and spending are checked or talked about); autonomic (how long your body takes to reset after stress, minutes for some and hours for others); decisional (how long you need before committing); communicational (how often you need to check in to feel secure); and philosophical (how often you want to revisit whether your values still line up).\nThe Framework gives an example for each. One for autonomic: after a work crisis, one person wants to talk straight away, while the other needs an hour of quiet. One feels abandoned; the other feels pressured. Neither is wrong.\nAn everyday example for communicational: one person likes a quick check-in every morning; their parent calls once a week. Neither is too much or too little. They just differ.\nTry: rate yourself on each of the five as “fast” or “slow,” and ask someone close to you to do the same. Look for the biggest gap.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#five', l: 'See all five' },

  beat: { t: 'Beat frequency', m: ['beat frequency', 'beat frequencies'],
    s: 'The friction that appears when two rhythms don’t match.',
    f: 'Your 90-minute reset collides with their need to talk every ten minutes, and the “why won’t you talk to me?” fight is the beat frequency.',
    w: 'The Framework’s point: the real friction isn’t in either rhythm, it’s in the gap between them. Knowing that removes the shame (you’re not incompatible), makes solutions obvious, and lets you predict friction before it happens.',
    x: 'In sound, a beat frequency is the wobbling interference you hear when two tones are close but not the same. The Framework uses it for the friction between two rhythms: it isn’t in either person, it’s in the gap.\nAnother example from the page: one person decides immediately; the other needs three weeks to think. The beat frequency is the resentment you both carry about feeling rushed or delayed.\nThe Framework gives three reasons knowing this matters. It removes the shame, because you’re not incompatible. It makes solutions clearer, because a mismatch in reset times needs a reset agreement, not “communicate better.” And it lets you predict friction before it happens.\nAn everyday example: every Friday evening, one person wants to plan the weekend and the other wants to switch off. Seeing it as a beat frequency, they move planning to Thursday lunchtime, when both are fresh.\nTry: name one argument that keeps coming back, and ask: which two rhythms are clashing here?',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#beat', l: 'Read about beat frequency' },

  coherence: { t: 'Coherence mechanisms', m: ['coherence mechanisms', 'coherence mechanism'],
    s: 'Simple agreements about when and how often you’ll sync up.',
    f: 'A weekly money check, a set time to decide, a daily 90-second loop.',
    w: 'You don’t have to change your natural rhythm. You build shared rhythms, locked cycles and planned check-ins so two different speeds stop producing friction. Frequency Calibration suggests them for each of the five frequencies.',
    x: 'A coherence mechanism is an agreement about timing. It gives two different rhythms a shared beat to meet on, so neither person has to change who they are.\nFrequency Calibration lists a few kinds: shared rhythms, locked cycles and planned check-ins. Examples from around the site include a weekly money check, a set day to decide, a named return time after a pause, and the daily 90-second loop (WP-13).\nThe trick is to say it out loud and agree on it. Unspoken rhythms drift. Frequency Calibration puts it plainly: scheduled alignment holds.\nAn everyday example: one person needs to talk things through as they happen; the other needs time alone after work. Their mechanism: a text at lunchtime, twenty quiet minutes after getting home, then ten minutes of catch-up before dinner. Both needs have a slot.\nTry: pick one repeated timing clash and write a one-line agreement for it, with a day or time in it. Try it for two weeks, then review.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#beat', l: 'Read more' },

  mood: { t: 'Mood Arbitrage', m: ['Mood Arbitrage'],
    s: 'Gently helping a tense moment soften by shifting the feeling in the room.',
    f: 'Notice how someone’s body is doing before you decode their words.',
    w: 'Four steps: notice their state (calming yourself first), choose the move that fits, try it, then hold the calmer frequency for about thirty seconds. It isn’t toxic positivity: the real point still needs to be said, just once the room can hear it.',
    x: 'Mood Arbitrage is about noticing how someone’s body is doing before you decode their words, and gently helping a tense moment soften. The page says our bodies pick up whether we feel safe long before we make sense of the words.\nIt starts with you. The first step is calming yourself on purpose, so the mood between you can settle and what you want to say can land safely.\nIt isn’t toxic positivity. The real point still gets said. The Check-ins page describes the same move inside a planned talk: keep the cost visible, then turn it into one repeatable action.\nAn everyday example from the page: you come home to a messy kitchen after your roommate’s stressful day. Instead of a lecture, you notice their tight shoulders, lighten the moment warmly, and then ask, “Want company while you handle this? Or do you need space first?” The free page shows the principle and one full example; a fuller version is in the membership.\nTry: next time someone close to you is tense, take three slow breaths before you say anything at all.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage' },

  threemoods: { t: 'The three mood frequencies', m: ['Three Mood Frequencies', 'Helicopter Brain', 'Structural Containment', 'Priority Anchor'],
    s: 'Three states a tense person is usually in: on guard, shut down, or racing.',
    f: 'Each needs a different kind of help; logic rarely reaches any of them.',
    w: 'Sympathetic (wound up, on guard): help them feel safe; the free move is the Absurdity Pivot. Dorsal (shut down): offer steady, calm company, called Structural Containment. Anxious (helicopter brain, racing thoughts): clear one thread, called the Priority Anchor.',
    x: 'A tense person usually shows one of three states. Reading which one comes before choosing what to do, because each needs a different kind of help.\nIt includes: sympathetic, wound up and on guard (tight jaw, rapid speech, louder words), where the goal is helping them feel safe; dorsal, shut down (blank face, slow or no speech, looking away), where the goal is a steady sense of safety; and anxious, the “helicopter brain” (darting eyes, many worries spilling out, fidgeting), where the goal is clearing one thread.\nThe matching moves are the Absurdity Pivot for on guard, Structural Containment for shut down, and the Priority Anchor for racing. The free page teaches the first one in full.\nAn everyday example for racing: someone lists five worries about a trip in one breath. Instead of answering all five, the other asks, “Which one is loudest right now?” and they deal with just that.\nTry: next time someone is upset, silently name which of the three it looks like before you respond.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage' },

  fourstep: { t: 'The four-step sequence', m: ['Four-Step Sequence'],
    s: 'The four steps of every gentle mood shift.',
    f: 'Notice, choose, try, hold.',
    w: 'Notice the current frequency (calm yourself first, then read their body). Choose the move that matches their state. Try it with your words, your body and your presence. Hold the new frequency: stay steady for about thirty seconds while their body’s natural calming brake kicks in.',
    x: 'Every gentle mood shift in Mood Arbitrage follows the same four steps. They’re simple, and they work best in order.\nIt includes: notice (calm yourself first with three deep breaths, then notice which state they’re in, without judgment); choose (pick the move that matches their state, since logic doesn’t soothe someone on guard); try (use specific words, your body and your presence, because the feeling you bring matters more than what you say); and hold (stay calm and steady, since after about thirty seconds their body’s natural calming brake can kick in).\nThe last step is the one most people skip. Rushing to the point as soon as the tension eases can bring it straight back.\nAn everyday example: a partner is quiet and heavy after a hard day. The other notices, sits nearby, and says, “I’m not going anywhere.” Then they wait, not filling the silence, until the partner speaks.\nTry: next time a tense moment starts to ease, count a slow thirty in your head before raising anything new.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage' },

  absurdity: { t: 'The Absurdity Pivot', m: ['Absurdity Pivot'],
    s: 'Breaking tension with warm, unexpected lightness.',
    f: '“I have a theory. These lonely forks are staging a rebellion.”',
    w: 'Not sarcasm, not mockery: real, warm absurdity that signals “I’m on your side.” It’s for someone on guard, not someone shut down, where it could feel like teasing. If it lands as dismissal, stop being silly, say so, and listen properly.',
    x: 'The Absurdity Pivot breaks tension with warm, unexpected lightness. Your tone signals “I’m on your side,” and the other person’s alarm system gets a chance to settle.\nThe example on the page: a messy kitchen and a stressed roommate who starts, “I know, I know, it was a terrible day.” Instead of a lecture: “Okay, I have a theory. These lonely forks? They’re staging a rebellion. Tiny ceramic boats, just left adrift on the counter.”\nWhat happens next varies. Sometimes they laugh. Sometimes they roll their eyes and smile. Either way, you’re no longer two people bracing against each other. Then you can help properly: “Want company while you handle this, or space first?”\nIt has limits. It’s never sarcasm or mockery. It’s for someone on guard, not someone shut down, where it could feel like teasing. And if it lands badly, drop the joke, say so kindly, and listen.\nTry: think of one silly, kind line that fits a small, repeated friction in your home, and keep it for a tense-but-safe moment.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'See the example' },

  lemonade: { t: 'The Lemonade Stand', m: ['Lemonade Stand Heuristic', 'Lemonade Stand'],
    s: 'A simple tally of the week’s household jobs and each person’s hours.',
    f: 'You both fill in your own hours, then look at the split together as a plain fact, not a verdict.',
    w: 'The idea: one person makes the lemonade and one sells it; both matter, but if only one keeps the books, the other can never know if it’s fair. Its totals feed the workload balance in CALC-01. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'The Lemonade Stand is a simple weekly tally. List the jobs it took to keep the household running, and each person enters their own hours. The split appears as two bars and two percentages.\nThe idea behind it, from PROG-01: one person makes the lemonade, one sells it. Both are needed. But if only one keeps the books, the other can never know whether it’s fair.\nIt works best fed by a week of the “who did what” log (WP-01), so the hours come from notes rather than memory. The totals feed the workload balance in CALC-01.\nAn everyday example: a couple adds up the week and sees a 70/30 split. That isn’t a verdict. It’s a starting point: one person picked up extra because the other had a deadline. They agree to swap it back next week. Nothing you type is saved or sent; it stays in the browser tab.\nTry: fill it in together this Sunday with rough hours, and say one thing each that surprised you.',
    u: '/lemonade-stand.html', l: 'Open the Lemonade Stand' },

  weather: { t: 'Today’s Weather', m: ['Today’s Weather', 'Today\'s Weather'],
    s: 'A one-minute reading of your own inner weather today.',
    f: 'Clear (calm), Gusty (revved up) or Fogged in (running on empty), plus pressure, body, sleep and people load.',
    w: 'It gives you a forecast, a talk window, what today is good for and what to leave. It isn’t a grade or a diagnosis; weather passes. Sleep is the single strongest signal in the check. The three skies match the three rungs of the Nervous System Ladder.',
    x: 'Today’s Weather is a one-minute reading of your own conditions. Only the first step is required: pick the sky that’s closest to true right now. Everything else sharpens the forecast.\nIt includes seven steps: the sky (clear, gusty or fogged in); the pressure (how much you were already carrying); ground conditions (hunger, thirst, pain, noise, caffeine, screens); last night’s sleep; people load; what’s blowing in (work, money, caring, health, family, the house, one conversation); and what would help in the next hour.\nThe forecast then shows what today is good for, what to leave until the front passes, sixty seconds that would help, and, if you need to say something now, a sentence you can copy.\nAn everyday example: someone picks Gusty, Heavy and Short night. The forecast suggests leaving the budget talk for another day and taking a short walk first. The talk happens on Thursday instead, and goes much better. It isn’t a diagnosis or a judgment; conditions pass.\nTry: do it once today, taking only step one if that’s all you have time for.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#today', l: 'Check today’s weather' },

  talkwindow: { t: 'The talk window', m: ['talk window'],
    s: 'Whether today is a good time for a hard conversation: open, wait, or closed.',
    f: 'A closed window doesn’t mean never. It means pick a better time.',
    w: 'Today’s Weather gives it as part of your forecast, along with what today is good for. It pairs with the Check-ins idea that the room has to be safe before the topic is.',
    x: 'The talk window is part of your Today’s Weather forecast. It gives a plain answer to one question: is today a good day for a hard conversation?\nOpen means your conditions look steady enough. Wait means maybe later, once something has changed, like eating or resting. Closed means today isn’t the day. Closed never means never. It means choose a better time.\nIt lines up with the Check-ins idea that the room has to be safe before the topic is. A good sentence in a bad moment still tends to go badly.\nAn everyday example: someone wants to talk about a relative’s visit. Their forecast says the window is closed: poor sleep, heavy pressure. They send a short message instead: “Can we talk about the visit tomorrow after dinner?” Tomorrow, the window is open.\nTry: before bringing up something that matters, check your window first. If it isn’t open, suggest a specific time instead.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#forecast', l: 'See your forecast' },

  peopleload: { t: 'People load', m: ['people load', 'peopling'],
    s: 'How much being around people has already asked of you today.',
    f: 'A day of meetings, calls and small talk can empty you, even if nothing went wrong.',
    w: 'It’s one of the steps in Today’s Weather, from “barely any” to “nonstop, no gaps.” A heavy reading is information about a day, not a failing in a person.',
    x: 'People load is how much being with others has already cost you today. It isn’t about liking people. Even good company takes energy, and some days ask for a lot of it.\nToday’s Weather asks it as one step, from “barely any” to “a normal amount,” “a lot,” or “nonstop, no gaps.” It sits alongside sleep and pressure, because it quietly changes how much you have left for home.\nWired Differently notes that some wirings spend more on each interaction, for example people who are highly sensitive or who work hard to mask. For them, a heavy people load can be the main reason an evening feels hard.\nAn everyday example: a teacher comes home after a day of nonstop talking. Their partner asks a simple question and gets a one-word answer. It’s not rudeness. The tank is empty. Twenty quiet minutes later, they’re ready to chat.\nTry: on busy days, tell the people at home your people load in one word when you walk in, so quiet isn’t mistaken for distance.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#today', l: 'Check today’s weather' },

  almanac: { t: 'Your almanac', m: ['almanac'],
    s: 'A private record of your daily weather, kept only on your device.',
    f: 'Over time it shows which fronts keep coming back, and how much of it is really sleep.',
    w: 'One entry is a mood; twelve entries are a pattern. Nothing is uploaded, no one else can see it, and you can erase it any time.',
    x: 'The almanac is where your daily weather readings are kept, if you choose to log them. It lives only on your device. Nothing is uploaded, and no one else can see it. You can erase it at any time.\nIts value comes from repetition. The How It Works page puts it well: one entry is a mood; twelve entries are a pattern. Memory alone can’t show you that.\nOver a few weeks, the almanac can show which fronts keep coming back, like Monday money worries or Friday exhaustion, and how often the heavy days follow a short night.\nAn everyday example: after a month of logging, someone notices nearly every Fogged in day came after less than six hours of sleep. They start protecting bedtime on weeknights. Their almanac gets lighter.\nTry: log today’s weather every day for two weeks, then look back and see if one factor shows up most on your hardest days.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#almanac', l: 'See your almanac' },

  wired: { t: 'Wired differently', m: ['Wired Differently', 'neurotypes', 'neurotype'],
    s: 'The same words can land very differently depending on how each brain takes them in.',
    f: '“Fine.” One person meant it literally. The other heard a door closing.',
    w: 'The guide covers fourteen kinds of wiring, from neurotypical to autistic, ADHD, dyslexic, anxious and highly sensitive, with how each receives words and how to speak to it. It describes tendencies, not any one person, and it can’t diagnose anyone. A mix-up usually runs both ways, so compare notes instead of looking for blame.',
    x: 'Wired Differently adds a second layer to Chapter I’s radio picture. Even when two people are on the same station, the receivers are built differently. None is broken. The static comes from not knowing what the other person runs on.\nIt includes wirings that are neurodevelopmental (autism, ADHD, dyslexia, dyspraxia, auditory processing differences, developmental language disorder, Tourette), traits that run across groups (alexithymia, high sensitivity), and conditions that change how words land (anxiety, trauma, OCD), plus neurotypical wiring and autistic and ADHD together.\nThe page leans on the double empathy problem: when two very different people misread each other, the breakdown is mutual, not a deficit inside one person.\nAn everyday example: one person texts “Fine.” and means it literally. The other reads the period as a slammed door. Neither is wrong; they compare notes and agree what “Fine.” means between them. Everything describes tendencies, not any one person, and it can’t diagnose anyone. The best source on how someone receives words is that person.\nTry: ask someone close to you, “Is there a word or text habit of mine that lands differently than I mean it?”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#double-empathy', l: 'Read Wired Differently' },

  card: { t: 'The Wiring Card', m: ['Wiring Card'],
    s: 'A short card about how you take in words, to swap on a calm day.',
    f: 'How you receive things best, what your silence means, what to avoid, and how a request should be phrased.',
    w: 'It isn’t a diagnosis, a label or a contract, and it changes with your state: when you’re depleted, every line gets stricter. Make it before a hard conversation, not during one. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'The Wiring Card is a short card about how you take in words, filled in by you and handed over on an ordinary day. It covers questions like how you receive things best, whether you take words literally, how much time you need to answer, what your quiet means, what short texts from you mean, what you need when upset, what to avoid, and how a request lands best.\nIt also has a line for “when I’m depleted, tighten this,” because every line gets stricter when you’re running low.\nThe page is clear about what it is: a receipt, not a label, and not a license to criticize or proof of bad intent. It tells someone how to send a request so it arrives. It doesn’t oblige them to manage you, and it doesn’t oblige you to be available in every state.\nAn everyday example: a card says, “If I go quiet, it means I’m thinking, not upset. Please write down anything with more than two steps.” A week later, a tricky plan arrives as a short list, and it works.\nTry: fill in just three lines of your card this week, and swap them with someone you live or work with.',
    u: '/wiring-card.html', l: 'Make your card' },

  report: { t: 'REPORT-01: The Full Read', m: ['REPORT-01', 'The Full Read', 'compilation layer'], cs: true,
    s: 'One place to record what your worksheets showed each week, read side by side.',
    f: 'A minute a week. It shows where the figures agree, where they contradict, and which difference matters.',
    w: 'Each worksheet sees one thing well and is blind to the rest; this is where they meet. Every finding is a stated rule you can check. It never judges either person or the relationship’s future. Your ledger is a file you save and reload; nothing is stored in the browser.',
    x: 'Each worksheet sees one thing well and is blind to the rest. WP-01 can’t see whether the load is owned. WP-03 can’t see whether it’s shared. REPORT-01 is where the figures sit side by side.\nEach week, you type in what the worksheets produced: minutes from WP-01, battery sums from WP-02, tasks owned and listed from WP-03, friction and retunes from WP-09, loops run from WP-13, kit use, and treaty changes. Each month, you add the Deficit Audit figures.\nThe report then writes where things stand and which contradictions matter. Every finding is a stated rule, listed on the page, so it can be checked rather than trusted.\nIt never contains a judgment about either person, a comparison between you, or advice about the relationship’s future. Your ledger is a small file you save and reload, so it isn’t left in a shared browser. An everyday example: two months in, the report notes stress rising while the chore split stays flat. The couple realizes work, not home, is the driver.\nTry: after your next weekly closing, spend one minute adding that week’s figures, then save the file somewhere private.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html', l: 'Dive into REPORT-01' },

  reportfindings: { t: 'The Full Read’s findings', m: ['Clarity without parity', 'Fixes aren’t holding', 'Fixes aren\'t holding', 'Treaty gone quiet', 'Invisible work surfacing'],
    s: 'Plain rules that notice when two worksheets tell different stories.',
    f: '“Clarity without parity”: the treaty is clear, but the split still isn’t even.',
    w: 'Others include: repair carrying the structure; load isn’t the driver (stress rising while the split stays flat); fixes aren’t holding (gaps coming back); treaty gone quiet (no amendments in six weeks); the loop isn’t feeding forward; kit doing its job; and invisible work surfacing, where a falling clarity score is actually good news because hidden work got named.',
    x: 'Each finding comes from two or more worksheets read together, which is why no single worksheet can produce it. Every rule is written out on the page.\nIt includes, for example: clarity without parity (ownership clear, split still uneven); structure holding, repair not (solvency well above apex); repair carrying the structure (apex well above solvency); load isn’t the driver (stress rising for three or more weeks while the split stays flat); fixes aren’t holding; treaty gone quiet (six weeks with no amendments); the loop isn’t feeding forward (friction logged for weeks, but nothing moved on to a fix); kit doing its job; and invisible work surfacing.\nThat last one is worth knowing. When the task list grows and the clarity score falls, it usually means hidden work got named, which is the score behaving correctly.\nAn everyday example: a couple sees “the loop isn’t feeding forward.” They realize the same friction has come up for weeks in their daily check-in. At the resync, they finally give it a treaty row.\nTry: read the list of rules once, and pick the one you think most likely to show up for you.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html#method', l: 'See every rule' },

  recurrence: { t: 'Recurrence rate', m: ['recurrence rate'],
    s: 'How many of the gaps you fixed last month came back.',
    f: 'A falling gap count means less if the gaps you fixed keep coming back.',
    w: 'REPORT-01 records it from the monthly Deficit Audit. Above 50 percent across the recorded months means the same gaps are being re-fixed rather than closed, so the structure, not the effort, needs another look.',
    x: 'Recurrence rate asks a simple question about last month’s fixes: did they hold? REPORT-01 records it from the monthly Deficit Audit, alongside how many structural gaps were found.\nIt matters because the gap count alone can mislead. A falling count looks like progress. But if many of the gaps you fixed keep coming back, you’re re-fixing rather than closing.\nREPORT-01’s rule is “fixes aren’t holding”: a recurrence rate above 50 percent across the recorded months. That points at the structure, not the effort. Maybe the owner is wrong, the timing doesn’t fit, or the job needs splitting.\nAn everyday example: “the recycling” was given an owner in March, April and May. It keeps coming back. Instead of a fourth reassignment, the couple looks at why: collection day falls on the owner’s longest workday. They move the job to the evening before.\nTry: at your next monthly audit, mark any gap you’ve seen before with a small star, and count the stars.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'Read about the monthly record' },

  prog: { t: 'PROG-01: the 6-week program', m: ['PROG-01', '6-Week Program', 'six gentle weeks'],
    s: 'A gentle six-week path through the chapters and worksheets, one at a time.',
    f: 'You don’t need week one to be perfect before starting week two.',
    w: 'Roughly: week 1 foundation and self-audit; week 2 ownership; week 3 the numbers and your battery; week 4 communication and tone; week 5 frequency and the daily loop; week 6 long-term rhythm. The other person joining is optional but helpful. It ends with a second read you can compare with the first.',
    x: 'PROG-01 is a guided, week-by-week path through the program, for anyone who’d rather be walked through it than choose their own order. It suits any two people who share time, space, work or care.\nIt includes: week 1, foundation and self-audit (the Preface, Chapter I, the ladder, and a week of WP-01); week 2, ownership (Chapters II and III, WP-03 and a first solvency read); week 3, the numbers and your battery (Chapter IV and WP-02); week 4, communication and tone (WP-09); week 5, frequency and the daily loop (WP-13); and week 6, long-term rhythm.\nYou don’t need week one to be perfect before starting week two. The other person joining is optional but recommended; you can still do it on your own.\nAn everyday example: someone starts alone, logging a week of WP-01. In week two, their partner reads the Lemonade Stand idea and decides to join for the treaty.\nTry: read just the week 1 page and pick one small task from it to finish this week.',
    u: '/prog-01-in-depth.html', l: 'See the six weeks' },

  extraction: { t: 'The extraction cycle', m: ['extraction cycle'],
    s: 'How unseen work, taken for granted over time, slowly turns into resentment.',
    f: 'One person keeps giving without it being seen, until small things start to sting.',
    w: 'PROG-01’s week 3 looks for it in your logs and battery readings: who’s carrying more, and when stress spikes. The remedy is making the work visible and owned, not a bigger argument.',
    x: 'The extraction cycle is how unseen work slowly turns into resentment. It starts small: one person gives without it being noticed. Over time, the giving stops feeling like a gift and starts feeling taken.\nPROG-01’s week 3 looks for it in your own records. With a few weeks of WP-01 logs and a week of battery readings, patterns start to show: who is carrying more, and when stress spikes.\nIt connects to the Preface’s unbilled debt. The work is invisible, so it can’t be thanked, so it can’t be shared. Nobody has to be unkind for the cycle to run.\nAn everyday example: for months, one person quietly plans every family birthday. At first they enjoy it. Then one year, a forgotten thank-you stings more than it should. That sting is the cycle showing itself. The remedy isn’t a bigger argument. It’s making the work visible and owned, and thanking the specific thing.\nTry: name one piece of work someone does for you that you may have stopped noticing, and thank them for it today.',
    u: '/prog-01-in-depth.html#week-3', l: 'Read week 3' },

  externalstatic: { t: 'External static', m: ['external static', 'environmental statics'],
    s: 'Stress from outside the relationship that shows up inside it.',
    f: 'Broken sleep, money worries or burnout can look like relationship trouble when they aren’t.',
    w: 'Separating the two stops blame landing on the wrong thing. The planned Static Dampening Log (WP-14) is designed for this; until then, WP-02 and the stress-reducing conversation on Turning Toward help.',
    x: 'External static is strain from outside the relationship that shows up inside it. Money, work, illness and broken sleep don’t start at home, but home is often where they come out.\nThe Program Overview says the goal is to stop outside strain being blamed on the other person by default. PROG-01 puts it plainly: not all relational stress is relational.\nAn everyday example: during a month of night shifts, one person becomes short with everyone. The other starts to wonder if something is wrong between them. Seen as external static, it becomes a plan for a hard season: fewer asks, simpler meals, a check-in after the night shifts end.\nThe planned Static Dampening Log (WP-14) is designed to separate the two, but no text for it exists yet. Until then, the Battery Meter (WP-02) shows how much you’re already carrying, and Turning Toward’s stress-reducing conversation helps you talk about outside stress together.\nTry: at your next weekly closing, each name one outside pressure from the week, and ask whether it’s been landing on home.',
    u: '/program-overview-in-depth.html#signal', l: 'Read about the Signal Layer' },

  designed: { t: 'Designed, not built yet', m: ['Designed, Not Built', 'designed-not-built', 'Static Dampening Log', 'Carrier Frequency Audit', 'Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment'],
    s: 'Some parts of the program are planned but not written yet, and the site says so.',
    f: 'If it isn’t listed as live on the Suite Index, it isn’t live yet.',
    w: 'Designed so far: the Carrier Frequency Audit (WP-12, pacing mismatch), the Static Dampening Log (WP-14, outside pressure versus friction between you) and Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment (WP-15, staying in step over years). Chapters VI to XII are also still being shaped.',
    x: 'The site keeps an honest list of what exists and what doesn’t. Each item is marked either built or designed-not-built. Designed items describe intended structure only; no text exists for them yet.\nIt includes: the Carrier Frequency Audit (WP-12, mapping pacing mismatch, a difference in tempo rather than willingness); the Static Dampening Log (WP-14, separating outside pressure from friction between you); and Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment (WP-15, staying in step across years). Chapters VI to XII, some workpaper numbers and some calculator slots are also not final yet.\nWhere the Program Overview and the Suite Index disagree, the Suite Index is correct. Release order and timing are estimates, not promises.\nAn everyday example: someone reads about WP-14 in PROG-01’s week 6 and goes looking for it. The Suite Index tells them it isn’t live yet, and the overview suggests what to use in the meantime.\nTry: if a tool you’ve read about seems missing, check the Suite Index first before hunting for it.',
    u: '/program-overview-in-depth.html#designed', l: 'See what’s planned' },

  suite: { t: 'The Workpaper Suite', m: ['Workpaper Suite'],
    s: 'Pick your road, and your worksheets line up in the order that helps most.',
    f: 'Just you, a kind of relationship, or the 6-week program.',
    w: 'Fill them in on the page or in one fillable PDF, and get a report grouped the way your road goes. Each person fills in their own side.',
    x: 'The Workpaper Suite lines up your worksheets in the order that helps most for your road. Roads include just you, a particular kind of relationship, or the six-week program.\nIt includes four steps: choose your road; see your workpapers in order, by step or week by week; bring in anything you’ve already filled in, like drafts, a suite file or a filled-in PDF; and make your PDF, either a fillable one you can type into or print, or a report grouped the way your road goes.\nWhat you type stays on your device. Nothing is sent or stored in the browser. To pick up later, save a suite file or bring back your PDF. If you’re on a shared computer, there’s a button to clear the page when you’re done.\nAn everyday example: two roommates choose their road, each fill in their own sheets on their phones, drop both files in, and print one report for their monthly chat.\nTry: choose a road and just look at the order it gives you, without filling anything in yet.',
    u: '/workpapers/fill/suite.html', l: 'Build your suite' },

  workpapers: { t: 'Workpapers', m: ['workpapers', 'workpaper'],
    s: 'The program’s worksheets: short, practical sheets you fill in.',
    f: 'An auditor’s word for a working paper. Each one answers one question well.',
    w: 'The core set: WP-01 (who did what, and kind refusals), WP-02 (your battery), WP-03 (one owner per job), WP-04 (the monthly Deficit Audit), WP-09 (fact, feeling and ask), WP-11 (the Calm-Down Kit) and WP-13 (the daily check-in). Four produce numbers for CALC-01; three deliberately don’t.',
    x: 'In audit work, a working paper is where the evidence gets written down. The site borrows the word for its worksheets: short, practical sheets that each answer one question well.\nThe Suite Index suggests an order: WP-01, WP-02, WP-03, WP-09, WP-13. Read CALC-01 once the first three have produced numbers, and pick up WP-04 monthly after that. Using them all at once turns them into paperwork, which is the most common way a household gives up in week two.\nIt also gives quick starting points. New? Start with one week of WP-01. Something on fire right now? WP-02, then WP-09. The same argument keeps coming back? That’s a WP-03 problem, not a communication problem.\nFour produce numbers for CALC-01; three don’t, on purpose, because not everything worth doing is worth scoring. The numbering has gaps, and the site says those gaps are honest, not accidental.\nTry: pick the one workpaper that fits where you are this week, and leave the rest for later.',
    u: '/suite-index-in-depth.html', l: 'See the Suite Index' },

  selfdiscovery: { t: 'Self-discovery', m: ['self-discovery'],
    s: 'Understanding yourself first: your load, your wiring and your patterns.',
    f: 'You snap at 4pm and can’t work out why. A quick check shows you’ve been running near empty since morning.',
    w: 'It’s the first half of the program; relationship tools come second. It starts with Today’s Weather, WP-02 and WP-11, then Wired Differently and the Wiring Card. Self-discovery isn’t self-criticism: the aim is to understand yourself as kindly as you’d want to be understood.',
    x: 'Self-discovery is the first half of the program. Before any tool for two people, it helps each person understand their own load, wiring and patterns.\nThe How It Works page names three things to look at: your life situation (what you’re carrying right now), your wiring (how your nervous system handles stress, noise, change and conversation), and what you discover when you write things down for a few weeks.\nPatterns tend to appear with time: the hour you get short-tempered, the job that always slips, the conversation that always goes sideways. Seen from outside, they stop looking like character flaws and start looking like problems you can solve.\nAn everyday example: after two weeks of Today’s Weather, someone notices they’re always Gusty on days with back-to-back calls. They start leaving ten minutes between calls, and evenings at home get easier. It should feel curious, not clinical. Closer to a puzzle about yourself than a test.\nTry: for one week, note the time of day you first feel irritable, and look for a pattern on Sunday.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#outside-lens', l: 'Read How it works' },

  outsidelens: { t: 'An outside lens', m: ['outside lens', 'honest auditor'],
    s: 'Looking at your own life calmly, as if from the outside.',
    f: 'Write down what actually happened, in plain terms, before deciding what it means.',
    w: 'It looks at three things that shape almost every reaction: your life situation (what you’re carrying right now), your neurological wiring (how your brain handles stress, noise and talk), and what you discover when you write things down for a few weeks. No verdict on your worth, just what’s true.',
    x: 'Most of us only see our lives from the inside, through our mood, our history and the way our brain is wired. The outside lens is a calm way to look at yourself as an honest auditor would, with no agenda except what’s true.\nIt borrows an auditor’s discipline: write down what happened, in plain terms, before deciding what it means. Then it turns that gently inward.\nThe How It Works page says it should feel curious, not clinical, with nothing to defend. No one is graded and no one is diagnosed. Every number describes a situation, never a person’s worth.\nAn everyday example: someone believes they’re “just bad at mornings.” Written down for two weeks, the picture changes: mornings go wrong on days after late screens and no breakfast. It isn’t a flaw. It’s a pattern with a fix. This is a practical rule of thumb, not a clinical tool, and your mood comes along when you fill it in. Repetition is what makes it trustworthy.\nTry: write one plain sentence about something that happened today, then a second sentence about what you think it meant. Notice the difference between the two.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#outside-lens', l: 'Read How it works' },

  /* ---------- Wiring and neurotypes ---------- */

  neurotypical: { t: 'Neurotypical', m: ['neurotypical'],
    s: 'The kind of wiring most social customs were designed around.',
    f: 'Reads between the lines, and trusts the tone over the words when they disagree.',
    w: 'Neurotypical listeners tend to expect some softening and indirectness, and can read hidden meaning into messages that had none: a bare “No,” an “ok.” with a period, a long pause. Speaking to this wiring: add the social layer a blunt message is missing (“it’s not about you”).',
    x: 'Neurotypical listeners decode a message on several channels at once: the words, the tone, the face, the timing, and how the relationship has been lately. When those disagree, they tend to trust the tone.\nThey also expect some softening. Wired Differently notes that people choose less direct wording when they expect disagreement, to avoid friction. The flip side is that neurotypical receivers can find hidden meaning that was never there.\nSome common misreads from the page: a long pause read as reluctance; no eye contact read as not listening; “That’s not how you load a dishwasher” heard as “you’re incompetent.” When they’re the one talking, a hint is often a request, and “maybe” can be a soft no.\nAn everyday example: a partner replies “No.” to “Want to go out tonight?” and simply means no. The neurotypical partner hears coldness. Next time, the reply is “No, not tonight. I’m wiped out, it’s not about you.” Same answer, and it lands softly.\nTry: if your texts are short by habit, say so once, in advance, so they stop being decoded for hidden meaning.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#neurotypical', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  neurodivergent: { t: 'Neurodivergent', m: ['neurodivergent', 'neurodivergence'],
    s: 'Wiring that differs in a meaningful way from what most customs were built around.',
    f: 'Estimates suggest 15 to 20 percent of people worldwide, and probably more, since many are never assessed.',
    w: 'It covers neurodevelopmental wirings like autism, ADHD, dyslexia and dyspraxia. It isn’t a flaw to hide; it’s information to understand and explain. Two neurodivergent people can differ as much as any two people, so ask, don’t assume.',
    x: 'Neurodivergent means a brain that takes in, sorts or responds to information differently from the design most social customs assume. Wired Differently gives an estimate of 15 to 20 percent of people worldwide, likely low because many people are never assessed.\nIt isn’t one thing. Autism, ADHD, dyslexia and dyspraxia are all different, and many people carry more than one wiring at once. Two autistic people can be as different from each other as any two neurotypical people.\nThe How It Works page puts the program’s view simply: neurotypical or neurodivergent, your wiring isn’t a flaw to hide. It’s information to understand, and then to explain to the people you live and work with.\nAn everyday example: someone realizes that noisy family dinners always leave them drained and short-tempered. Instead of forcing through, they explain it and agree on a ten-minute quiet break halfway through. The evening gets easier for everyone. Wired Differently can’t diagnose anyone. If you recognize yourself and want to know more, a qualified clinician is the right next step.\nTry: ask the person you’re closest to, “Is there anything about how your brain works that you wish I understood better?”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#words', l: 'Read the words, used carefully' },

  neurodev: { t: 'Neurodevelopmental', m: ['neurodevelopmental'],
    s: 'Wiring that’s been there from early life.',
    f: 'Autism, ADHD, dyslexia, dyspraxia, auditory processing differences, developmental language disorder and Tourette.',
    w: 'Wired Differently sorts wirings into three groups: neurodevelopmental ones; traits found across every group (alexithymia, high sensitivity); and conditions that change how words land (anxiety, trauma, OCD). They often travel together.',
    x: 'Neurodevelopmental means present from early life. These wirings aren’t something that happened to a person later; they’re how the brain has always been built.\nIt includes the seven Wired Differently covers in this group: autism, ADHD, dyslexia, dyspraxia, auditory processing differences, developmental language disorder and Tourette syndrome.\nThe page sets these beside two other groups. Traits run across every group: alexithymia and high sensitivity. And some conditions change how words land without being neurotypes in the strict sense: anxiety, trauma and OCD. They’re included because they reshape the receiver just as much, and because they often travel together.\nAn everyday example: one person has dyslexia and ADHD, and also carries some anxiety. A long, careful email about weekend plans costs reading effort, loses the key ask in paragraph four, and leaves them worrying. A short list with the ask first works far better.\nTry: when you learn someone’s wiring, ask which one shapes how they receive messages most, rather than assuming from the label.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#words', l: 'Read the words, used carefully' },

  doubleempathy: { t: 'The double empathy problem', m: ['double empathy'],
    s: 'When two differently wired people misunderstand each other, the gap runs both ways.',
    f: 'It isn’t a deficit sitting inside one person. Each is using a decoder built for their own kind of signal.',
    w: 'Named by Dr. Damian Milton. The research is still being tested, and the honest reading is that mixed pairs can pass facts along fine, but the talk tends to feel harder to both people. The lesson for every pairing: don’t open a case against either person. Assume two decoders, both partly right, and compare notes.',
    x: 'The usual assumption is that when a neurodivergent person and a neurotypical person misunderstand each other, the neurodivergent person “has trouble communicating.” The double empathy problem says the breakdown is mutual. Both people are using a decoder built for their own kind of signal.\nWired Differently is careful with the evidence. An early experiment passed a story along chains of people and found that mixed chains, with autistic and non-autistic people together, lost details faster. A much larger repeat of the study didn’t find that extra loss, but did find the people got on less easily. The honest reading: facts get through, but the conversation tends to feel harder to both.\nOther studies on the page found that non-autistic observers formed less favorable first impressions of autistic people from audio and video, but not from transcripts alone. The judgment was about delivery, not content.\nAn everyday example: one person thinks the other is being cold; the other thinks the first is being vague. Instead of deciding who’s at fault, they agree that the words, not the tone, are the official message between them.\nTry: after a mix-up, each describe what you meant and what you heard, and look for where the two decoders differed.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#double-empathy', l: 'Read about double empathy' },

  autistic: { t: 'Autistic', m: ['autistic', 'autism'],
    s: 'A wiring that often takes words literally first, with tone and hints taking extra effort.',
    f: '“It’d be nice if someone took the trash out” may be heard as a true statement about trash, not a request.',
    w: 'The difference is often cost, not capacity. Helps: say the request as a request, define the finish line, flag sarcasm, give processing time, offer writing for complex topics, and stop asking questions during a shutdown. Directness is usually efficiency, not coldness, and silence often means “I’m processing.”',
    x: 'Many autistic people take words at their stated meaning first. Tone, facial expressions, hints, idioms and sarcasm take extra effort to decode, especially when tired or focused elsewhere.\nWired Differently notes that this is often about cost, not capacity. In one study, autistic and non-autistic students read hints equally well from context. But decoding hints, tone and faces all at once takes deliberate effort, and many autistic adults spend so much energy masking that they miss the conversation itself.\nSome useful guidance from the page: say the request as a request; define the finish line (what, where, how much, by when); flag sarcasm or leave it out; give processing time before repeating a question; and offer writing for complex topics.\nAn everyday example: “Clean up a bit before my parents come” creates anxiety, because “a bit” has no end point. “Could you clear the living room table and vacuum by 5?” is kind, clear and doable.\nTry: next time you ask an autistic person for something, include the what and the by-when in one sentence.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#autistic', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  masking: { t: 'Masking', m: ['masking', 'camouflaging', 'drop the mask'],
    s: 'Hiding your natural ways of being to fit in or keep others comfortable.',
    f: 'Watching your eye contact, face and turn-taking so closely that you miss the conversation itself.',
    w: 'It’s common among autistic adults, and exhaustion is its most consistent cost. The site’s founder writes about dropping the mask as a turning point. A Wiring Card is one way to explain your needs openly instead of performing around them.',
    x: 'Masking, sometimes called camouflaging, means hiding your natural ways of being to fit in: watching your eye contact, copying expressions, rehearsing small talk, holding in movements or sounds.\nWired Differently cites a study in which most autistic adults reported camouflaging, and exhaustion was its most consistent cost. It can use so much energy that the conversation itself gets missed.\nThe site’s founder writes on the About page about years of masking to make everyone else comfortable, and the profound exhaustion that followed. Dropping the mask, and building an environment where his true wiring could thrive, became the turning point that led to this program.\nAn everyday example: someone comes home from a sociable day and needs an hour alone before they can talk. It isn’t rudeness. They’ve spent the day performing. Knowing this, the people at home can give them that hour without taking it personally.\nTry: fill in the Wiring Card line “If I go quiet, it means…” and share it, so you can explain your needs openly rather than perform around them.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#autistic', l: 'Read more' },

  adhd: { t: 'ADHD', m: ['ADHD'], cs: true,
    s: 'A wiring where attention comes in bursts, and details can slip during brief lapses.',
    f: '“Grab milk, drop off the package, and call your mom back” can become just “grab milk” by the parking lot.',
    w: 'Working memory is often stretched, “later” can quietly evaporate, and emotion can run hot. Helps: one request at a time or in writing, a real time instead of “later,” the ask first, and criticizing the system rather than the person. A forgotten task is rarely a message that you don’t matter.',
    x: 'ADHD attention tends to come in bursts. During brief lapses, parts of a message can drop out. Working memory is often stretched, so a list of spoken jobs is hard to hold, and “later” can quietly disappear without a time or cue attached.\nWired Differently adds that emotion can run hot. Criticism can hit harder than intended, especially if it echoes years of similar comments. An unanswered text can feel like rejection.\nHelpful habits from the page: one request at a time, or the list in writing; a real time instead of “later”; the ask first, then the reasons; separating the behavior from the person; and an “echo,” where the listener repeats back what they heard.\nAn everyday example: “The bill slipped. Can we set a reminder together?” works far better than “You forgot again.” Same problem, but one invites a fix and the other invites shame. When they’re the one talking: interrupting is often fear of losing the thought, and a tangent is often how they connect ideas.\nTry: swap one “later” this week for a real time or cue, like “after dinner” or “by 6.”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#adhd', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  rsd: { t: 'Rejection sensitivity', m: ['rejection sensitivity', 'rejection sensitive dysphoria'],
    s: 'Reading neutral or unclear moments as rejection, and feeling it very strongly.',
    f: 'An unanswered text for three hours can feel like “they’re upset with me.”',
    w: 'It isn’t a formal diagnosis, but many people with ADHD describe it. What helps: close loops (“busy until 5, all good”), separate the behavior from the person, and remember criticism can land with far more force than you intended.',
    x: 'Rejection sensitivity describes reading unclear or neutral moments as rejection, and feeling it with real intensity. Wired Differently notes it isn’t a formal diagnosis, but many people with ADHD describe it.\nAmbiguity is where it bites. Silence, a short reply, or a delay leaves a gap, and rejection sensitivity fills that gap with “they’re upset with me.” The page mentions a student who stayed up until 4am checking their phone after an unanswered text.\nHelpful habits: close loops (“Busy until 5, all good”); describe the behavior, not the person; and remember that criticism can land with far more force than you meant.\nAn everyday example: before a long meeting, someone sends their partner a quick “In a meeting till 3, nothing’s wrong, talk tonight.” It takes five seconds. It saves the other person three hours of worry. When they’re the one feeling it: naming it can help. “My brain is reading your silence as upset. Is it?” is a fair question to ask.\nTry: agree on a short “all good, just busy” message either of you can send when you’ll be slow to reply.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#adhd', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  audhd: { t: 'Autistic and ADHD together', m: ['AuDHD', 'Autistic and ADHD together'], cs: true,
    s: 'Autism and ADHD often occur together, sometimes called AuDHD.',
    f: 'You can need blunt, literal clarity and still lose the second half of a long explanation.',
    w: 'There can be a pull between ADHD’s need for novelty and autism’s need for routine. Helps: direct, short and written plans, changes given early with the reason and the new plan together, and criticizing the process, not the person.',
    x: 'Autism and ADHD commonly occur together. Wired Differently notes shared traits, such as trouble following conversations, sensory differences and rejection sensitivity, and a real pull between ADHD’s need for novelty and autism’s need for routine.\nFor communication, that can mean needing blunt, literal clarity and still losing the second half of a long explanation. Wanting a predictable plan and still forgetting it if it isn’t written down. Taking words literally while reacting strongly to criticism.\nSome phrases hit two sensitivities at once. “Let’s play it by ear this weekend” is vague, unstructured and has nothing to remember. “Why didn’t you do what I asked?” is a literal question to answer and an accusation to feel.\nAn everyday example: instead of “We’ll figure out Saturday later,” a partner writes: “Saturday: groceries at 10, free after 1.” Clear, short and in writing. There’s even room for a spontaneous plan in the free afternoon. When they’re the one talking, an abrupt topic jump can be both a tangent and a deep dive. Both are engagement.\nTry: when plans change, give the reason and the new plan together, as early as you can.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#audhd', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  dyslexic: { t: 'Dyslexic', m: ['dyslexic', 'dyslexia'],
    s: 'A wiring where spoken words come easily and written words cost effort.',
    f: 'A five-paragraph text about a disagreement can be exhausting before it’s even started.',
    w: 'It has nothing to do with intelligence. Helps: short lines, one idea per line, the important part first, a voice note or call for anything emotional, and never commenting on spelling in a personal conversation. A short reply may just mean typing costs effort.',
    x: 'Dyslexia affects the processing of written language. Many dyslexic people understand spoken words easily but read slowly, reread to understand, and lean on memory, context or listening. Wired Differently is clear that it has nothing to do with intelligence.\nThat makes the channel matter. A phone call and a wall-of-text email can carry the same message at very different costs. Emotional weight and reading effort stack on top of each other.\nHelpful habits from the page: short lines, one idea per line; the important part in the first line; a voice note or call for anything emotional or complex; and never commenting on spelling in a personal conversation.\nAn everyday example from the page’s mixed pairs: a careful long-message writer and a dyslexic reader. Paragraphs meant as respect arrive as a wall. The fix: the headline in the first line, the rest in three short lines, and an offer to talk it through on a call. When they’re the one writing, a short, blunt text may simply be short because typing costs effort. A slow reply may be a reread, not a cold shoulder.\nTry: next time you send something that matters, put the one key line first and keep the rest to three lines.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dyslexic', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  dyspraxic: { t: 'Dyspraxic', m: ['dyspraxic', 'dyspraxia'],
    s: 'A wiring that mainly affects movement and coordination, and can slow a reply in fast talk.',
    f: 'Rapid-fire questions can lead to pressure, then a freeze.',
    w: 'Drop “just,” “easy” and “obviously.” Give steps in order, show rather than describe, and let the pause happen. A long pause is them building the answer, not a brush-off.',
    x: 'Dyspraxia, also called developmental coordination disorder (DCD), mainly affects movement and coordination. Wired Differently notes it also shows up in conversation: keeping up with fast talk can be hard, and organizing a reply can take longer.\nMany dyspraxic people also find organization, attention and memory harder. Handwriting is often tricky, so typing is usually easier.\nSome words make things worse. “It’s easy, just do it like this” can sound like “something is wrong with you if you can’t,” because “just” and “easy” shrink a task that really does cost more.\nAn everyday example: showing someone a new way to fold fitted sheets, a partner slows down, shows each step in order, and waits. The dyspraxic person gets it on the second try, with no pressure and no “obviously.” When they’re the one talking, a long pause is them building the answer. Being late or disorganized isn’t a statement about how much they care.\nTry: when asking a dyspraxic person something, ask one question and then let the silence sit for a few seconds longer than feels natural.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dyspraxic', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  apd: { t: 'Auditory processing differences', m: ['auditory processing'],
    s: 'The ears work, but speech gets scrambled in noise or when it’s fast.',
    f: 'A request called from the next room with the TV on can arrive as noise with a few words in it.',
    w: 'Helps: face to face, quiet, rephrasing rather than repeating louder, and following up important plans in text. “What?” is a real question, and a joke told deadpan can easily be taken as serious.',
    x: 'With auditory processing differences, hearing is usually normal, but the brain has trouble making sense of speech, especially in noise, when someone talks fast, with strong accents, or with similar-sounding words.\nWired Differently adds other signs: longer response times, frequent requests to repeat, difficulty with complex spoken directions, and missing the tone shifts that signal a joke or sarcasm.\nHelpful habits: talk face to face and don’t cover your mouth; turn the TV off or move somewhere quieter for anything that matters; rephrase rather than repeat louder; and follow up important plans in text.\nAn everyday example: “I already told you that” lands as blame, but it was said from another room with the dishwasher running. It didn’t arrive intact. Next time, the plan is said at the table and sent as a quick text. When they’re the one talking, an answer that seems off-topic may be an answer to what they thought they heard.\nTry: for anything important this week, find a quiet moment and say it face to face, then send a one-line text confirming it.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#apd', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  dld: { t: 'Developmental language disorder', m: ['Developmental language disorder', 'developmental language disorder', 'DLD'], cs: true,
    s: 'A difference in understanding and using language that can last into adulthood.',
    f: '“The ball’s in your court” can be confusing, and a long sentence can get lost after its first part.',
    w: 'It’s common and often missed. Helps: short sentences, one idea each, concrete words, and kindly checking understanding. A roundabout answer may be a word-finding problem, not evasion.',
    x: 'Developmental language disorder affects learning, understanding and using language, and its effects can continue into adulthood. Wired Differently notes it is common and often missed.\nAdults with DLD may use fewer complex sentences, find it hard to reach for the right word, or struggle with figurative language. A sentence with two “unless” clauses can be lost after the first part, and idioms have to be learned one by one.\nHelpful habits from the page: short sentences, one idea each; concrete words over abstract ones; and checking understanding kindly, like “Tell me what you’re taking from this so I know I said it well.”\nAn everyday example: “We’ll cross that bridge when we come to it” gets a puzzled look. The speaker switches to “Let’s decide that next week, when we know more.” The plan lands straight away. When they’re the one talking, a roundabout answer may be a word-finding problem, not evasion. Give them time to find the word before offering one.\nTry: notice how many idioms you use in one conversation today, and swap one for plain words.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dld', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  tourette: { t: 'Tourette syndrome', m: ['Tourette'],
    s: 'Sudden, repeated movements or sounds (tics) that aren’t chosen.',
    f: 'An eye roll or grunt during a serious talk can be a tic, not contempt.',
    w: 'In communication the static usually sits with the listener, who mistakes a tic for a reaction. Respond to the words, not the tic, and if they tell you something was a tic, believe them. “Can’t you just stop?” adds stress and often more tics.',
    x: 'Tourette syndrome involves sudden, repeated movements or sounds, called tics, that aren’t chosen. Tics can be held back briefly, at a cost, and talking about them can bring them on, which leads people to wrongly assume they’re deliberate.\nWired Differently notes that in communication, the static usually sits with the listener. People mistake a tic for a reaction to what was just said. Stress can make tics more frequent, which is exactly when serious talks happen.\nHelpful habits: respond to the words, not the tic; don’t comment on tics mid-conversation unless asked; and if a tic lands at an awkward moment, let it pass without meaning. If they tell you something was a tic, believe them.\nAn everyday example: during a serious talk, one person grunts and rolls their eyes. The other feels a flash of hurt, then remembers. They carry on with the sentence, and the talk stays on the topic. The page also notes that more than half of people with Tourette also have OCD, ADHD or both, so other wirings may be in the room too.\nTry: if someone close to you has tics, ask them once, on a calm day, how they’d like you to respond when one happens during a talk.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#tourette', l: 'Read about this wiring' },

  alexithymia: { t: 'Alexithymia', m: ['alexithymia'],
    s: 'Finding it hard to identify and name your own feelings.',
    f: '“How does that make you feel?” can feel like an exam question with no answer.',
    w: 'It’s a trait found in any wiring, especially common with autism. The feelings are there; the label isn’t. Helps: ask about the body or actions (“Is your chest tight? Space or company?”), offer options, and translate feelings into actions. “I’m fine” may be the honest answer they have right now.',
    x: 'Alexithymia means finding it hard to identify and describe your own feelings. It’s a trait that can appear in any wiring, and Wired Differently notes it’s especially common with autism.\nIt can make someone seem unfeeling when they feel plenty. The feeling is there; the label isn’t. Their body may react strongly while their words stay flat, and distress may show up as “I’m tired” or a headache.\nHelpful habits from the page: ask about the body or actions (“Is your chest tight? Do you want space or company?”); offer options rather than open questions (“More like tired, or more like annoyed?”); and turn feelings into actions (“Supportive, for me, means asking how the appointment went”).\nAn everyday example: “I just need you to be more supportive” gives nothing to act on. “Could you text me after my scan on Tuesday?” does. The care was always there; now it has instructions. The Check-ins page adds: don’t hand someone a feeling-word they didn’t use. It can feel like a correction.\nTry: next time you ask how someone feels, offer two options instead of an open question.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#alexithymia', l: 'Read about this trait' },

  hsp: { t: 'Highly sensitive', m: ['highly sensitive', 'sensory processing sensitivity'],
    s: 'A temperament where tone, mood and surroundings are taken in very deeply.',
    f: 'A slightly clipped “Sure” can be noticed instantly and felt for hours.',
    w: 'It’s found in roughly 20 percent of people. The problem isn’t too little getting through; it’s too much. Helps: a soft start, a quiet setting, one topic, and naming your tone if it’s off for other reasons. Lower the volume of the house before you lower the volume of the talk.',
    x: 'High sensitivity, or sensory processing sensitivity, is a temperament trait. Wired Differently describes it as found in roughly 20 percent of people. Highly sensitive people often pick up tone and mood that others miss.\nThe page puts it neatly: the problem isn’t that too little gets through. Too much does. A slightly clipped “Sure” can be noticed instantly and felt for hours, and a hard talk in a loud room can be overwhelming before a word is said.\nHelpful habits: a soft start, a quiet setting, one topic; naming your tone if it’s off for other reasons (“I’m short because of work, not you”); and time to process before a decision.\nAn everyday example: a partner’s voice gets loud with excitement about a plan. The sensitive partner says, “Your voice got loud. I know you’re not angry, but my body doesn’t.” The volume comes down, and the plan gets talked through. When they’re the one talking, “something felt off” is often accurate about mood, even if the cause is misread.\nTry: before a serious talk, lower the house first: TV off, lights softer, phones away.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#sensitive', l: 'Read about this trait' },

  overload: { t: 'Sensory overload', m: ['sensory overload', 'sensory differences'],
    s: 'When light, noise or busyness is simply too much to take in.',
    f: 'A hard talk in a loud restaurant can feel overwhelming before a word is said.',
    w: 'Highly sensitive people take in more than most, and sensory differences are common with autism and ADHD. Check-ins asks for light, noise and temperature low enough that a sensitive nervous system isn’t already spending its budget on the room. The Calm-Down Kit’s environmental move is to lower the input.',
    x: 'Sensory overload is when the input around you is more than you can take in: bright light, noise, crowds, strong smells, lots happening at once. It can make even a simple talk feel impossible.\nWired Differently notes that highly sensitive people take in more than most, and sensory differences are common with autism, ADHD and auditory processing differences. Chapter IV treats light, noise and scent as real inputs to a conversation, not background.\nThe fixes are practical. The Check-ins page asks for light, noise and temperature low enough that a sensitive nervous system isn’t already spending its budget on the room. The Calm-Down Kit’s environmental move is to lower the lights, leave the noisy room, or step outside.\nAn everyday example: a family argument keeps flaring at a busy dinner table with the TV on. Moving the talk to a quiet room afterward, just two people, changes the tone completely. When someone needs to leave a loud place, it isn’t rejecting the company.\nTry: notice one place in your home that feels loudest or busiest, and agree not to have hard conversations there.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#sensitive', l: 'Read more' },

  anxiety: { t: 'Anxiety and how words land', m: ['anxiety'],
    s: 'An anxious mind fills gaps with the worst possible reading.',
    f: '“We need to talk” can mean hours of bracing for disaster.',
    w: 'The more a message leaves unsaid, the more room the worst reading has. Helps: lead with the reassurance, then the topic (“Nothing’s wrong. Can we talk about the weekend tonight?”), give the reason for a call, and close loops. “Are you mad at me?” is a real question; answer it plainly.',
    x: 'Anxious minds tend to fill uncertainty with the most threatening meaning. Wired Differently describes an experiment where socially anxious people chose the threatening meaning of unclear sentences about twice as often as others.\nSo the fewer details a message gives, the more room the worst reading has. “We need to talk” can mean hours of bracing. “Call me when you can” can mean “something is wrong.” A short “k.” can read as anger.\nHelpful habits: lead with the reassurance, then the topic; give the reason for a call up front; and close loops with a quick “Got it, thanks!” rather than silence.\nAn everyday example from the page’s mixed pairs: one person is anxious, the other a slow texter. They agree: “No reply means busy, never mad. If I’m upset, I’ll say so in words.” The worry has somewhere to rest. When they’re the one talking, over-explaining is often a way to avoid being misunderstood, and “Are you mad at me?” deserves a plain answer.\nTry: rewrite one “We need to talk” into “Nothing’s wrong. Can we talk about ___ tonight?”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#anxiety', l: 'Read about this' },

  hypervigilance: { t: 'Hypervigilance', m: ['hypervigilance', 'trauma-wired'],
    s: 'A nervous system that stays on watch for danger after trauma.',
    f: 'A raised voice, even an excited one, can register as danger before the words do.',
    w: 'Content only gets processed once the body decides it’s safe. Helps: a steady volume, the purpose first, no serious talks sprung late at night, and settling together before talking. A startle or sharp reply may be the alarm, not their opinion of you.',
    x: 'After trauma, the nervous system can stay on watch. Wired Differently describes hypervigilance as reading neutral or unclear signals as threatening, and scanning for danger. For communication, volume, sudden movement and a hard edge in a voice can register before any words do.\nThe page gives examples. A raised voice, even an excited one, can be heard as danger. “Come here. Now.” can echo past threat. “Calm down” can sound like “your reaction is the problem,” and alarms get louder when ignored.\nHelpful habits: a steady volume, the purpose first (“Can you come look at this? It’s a good surprise”), no approaching from behind, no serious talks late at night, and settling together before talking.\nAn everyday example: a partner flinches when a door bangs mid-conversation. Instead of “What’s wrong with you?”, the other pauses, lowers their voice, and says, “Sorry, that was loud. Take a second.” When they’re the one talking, needing to leave a heated conversation is often self-protection. Agree on a time to come back. This page can’t diagnose anyone; a clinician can help.\nTry: give the reason first whenever you call someone’s name urgently.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#trauma', l: 'Read about this' },

  ocd: { t: 'OCD and certainty', m: ['OCD', 'Reassurance seeking'], cs: true,
    s: 'OCD makes uncertainty sticky, so doubt can loop.',
    f: '“I think I locked it” leaves the doubt open. A clear “I locked it” closes it.',
    w: 'Helps: answer the first time clearly, without hedging, and agree in a calm moment how you’ll both respond to repeat questions, ideally with a clinician’s input. A repeated question isn’t distrust of you; it’s the doubt talking.',
    x: 'OCD makes uncertainty sticky. Reassurance seeking, asking the same question again and again to quiet a doubt, is common. Wired Differently notes that family members often give reassurance because they don’t know what else to do, even though it can keep the cycle going.\nSome answers keep the doubt open. “I think I locked it” leaves room. “Whatever, it’s probably fine” hands the doubt the exact word it attaches to. “Stop asking me that” denies the answer without addressing the loop.\nHelpful habits from the page: answer the first time clearly and without hedging; for repeat questions, use a script you agreed on together; speak to the person, not the doubt; and keep your tone warm.\nAn everyday example: “Did you turn off the oven?” “Yes, I turned it off.” Asked again: “I answered that one. I’m here with you while the doubt passes.” Said warmly, and agreed in advance, it helps both. The page suggests agreeing on that script in a calm moment, ideally with a clinician’s input. It isn’t a treatment plan.\nTry: practice giving one clear, unhedged answer today, without “I think” or “probably.”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#ocd', l: 'Read about this' },

  subtext: { t: 'Subtext and hints', m: ['subtext', 'indirectness'],
    s: 'The meaning carried between the lines rather than in the words.',
    f: '“It’s cold in here” can mean “please close the window.”',
    w: 'People often soften a request to avoid friction. That works for some wirings and gets lost for others. The first of Wired Differently’s ten rules: say the request as a request. If you can’t tell, it’s fair to ask, “Is that a request, or just an observation?”',
    x: 'Subtext is meaning carried between the lines: in tone, in hints, in what’s left unsaid. It’s a normal part of how many people talk. Wired Differently notes people often choose indirect wording when they expect disagreement, to avoid friction and leave room to retreat.\nThat works well for some listeners and gets lost for others. An autistic person may hear “It’d be nice if someone took the trash out” as a true statement about trash. Someone with ADHD may catch the hint and then lose it with no time attached.\nThe relationship changes the cost too. A hint between friends risks awkwardness. The same hint between co-parents can sound like a record being built.\nAn everyday example: one person sighs, “The trash is full again.” Nothing happens. Next week they say, “Could you take the trash out before 9 tonight?” and it’s done. Nobody was ignoring anyone; the request was simply hidden.\nTry: next time you catch yourself hinting, turn it into a question with a time, like “Could you ___ by ___?”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#neurotypical', l: 'Read more' },

  tenrules: { t: 'Ten rules for every wiring', m: ['Ten rules'],
    s: 'Ten habits that help a sentence arrive well, whoever is listening.',
    f: 'Say the request as a request. Put a real time on it. One thing at a time.',
    w: 'The rest: lead with the headline; say the feeling in words; leave sarcasm out of anything serious; choose the channel on purpose; allow silence; check what arrived; describe the behavior, never the person. They’re about how a sentence arrives, not permission to pile topics or blame.',
    x: 'These ten habits come at the end of Wired Differently. They’re about how a sentence arrives, and they help with almost any listener.\nIt includes: say the request as a request; put a real time on it; one thing at a time; lead with the headline; say the feeling in words; leave sarcasm out of anything serious; choose the channel on purpose (writing gives processing time, voice carries warmth); allow silence; check what arrived (“What did you take from that?”); and describe the behavior, never the person.\nThe page is clear that they aren’t permission to pile on topics, blame a person, or treat a miss as proof of bad intent. The ground rules still apply.\nAn everyday example: “Can you clean up a bit when you get a chance?” becomes “Could you clear the counter and run the dishwasher before 8 tonight?” It uses three rules at once: a request, a time, and a clear finish.\nTry: pick the one rule you break most often, and practice it for a week.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#rules', l: 'Read the ten rules' },

  dynamics: { t: 'The seven relationship dynamics', m: ['seven dynamics'],
    s: 'The seven kinds of relationship the program is built around.',
    f: 'The same sentence can be a loose plan between friends and a broken agreement between partners.',
    w: 'The seven: partners, family, co-parents, friends, roommates, coworkers and caregivers. Wiring is how a brain takes words in; the relationship is the arrangement under the words. The Signal Translator lets you set both.',
    x: 'Wiring is how a brain takes words in. The relationship is the arrangement under the words: what you share, what you didn’t choose, and what it would cost to be direct. Wired Differently treats them as separate layers.\nIt includes seven dynamics: partners (sharing a life); family (shared history, old roles still in the room); co-parents (raising a child, in one home or two); friends (chosen, no job description); roommates (a home, not a life); coworkers (shared work, sometimes uneven power); and caregivers (sharing care for someone who needs it).\nThe same words can land very differently in each. “Whenever” is a loose plan between friends. Between partners, it can sound like the shared life isn’t a priority. Between co-parents, it can sound like “this will be late, and I’ll be blamed.”\nAn everyday example: siblings caring for a parent. One says “help more,” and the other hears that rest is never allowed. Naming the dynamic helps them talk about the load, not each other.\nTry: set the relationship in the Signal Translator before testing your next important sentence.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dynamics', l: 'Read about the dynamics' },

  conflictneeds: { t: 'Two conflict needs', m: ['two conflict needs'],
    s: 'In a hard moment, one person may need closeness while the other needs space.',
    f: 'Silence can feel like being left to one; being pushed to talk can feel like pressure to the other.',
    w: 'Neither need is the childish one. The care is to do the small thing first (a signal the bond is okay), then the pause, then the return: “I’m not leaving this. I need twenty minutes. We’re okay.” Offer touch, never take it.',
    x: 'A hard moment often holds two different needs. One person may need the bond confirmed straight away, because silence feels like losing you. The other may need space before their body can think.\nWired Differently says neither need is the childish one. Forcing the talk before the body settles makes a second spike. Taking space with no signal makes the pause feel like an ending.\nThe care is an order: the small thing first (a signal that you’re okay together), then the pause, then the return. The page offers a bridge: “I am not leaving this. I need 20 minutes, until 8:15, because I cannot think clearly yet. We are ok. This is a pause, not an ending.” The other can say, “I can wait if I know you’re coming back.”\nAn everyday example: one partner wants a hug before a pause; the other wants to walk alone. They hug briefly, name 8:15, and the walk happens. At 8:15, they sit down again. Offer touch, never take it. “Hug, hand, or words?” A no is complete.\nTry: ask each other which need you usually have in a hard moment, closeness or space, and write down both answers.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#situation', l: 'Read about caring for both' },

  /* ---------- Turning toward ---------- */

  bids: { t: 'Bids for connection', m: ['bids', 'a bid', 'bid for', 'Turning away', 'turning against'],
    s: 'Small reaches for attention: “look at this,” a sigh, a joke, a hand on the shoulder.',
    f: 'You can turn toward it (look up, answer), turn away (miss it) or turn against it (“can’t you see I’m busy?”).',
    w: 'Research on newlyweds found couples who stayed together turned toward most of these small moments. Turning away is rarely cruel; it’s usually tiredness. Nobody catches every bid; the overall pattern counts. Can’t right now? “I want to hear this. Give me ten minutes?” then come back.',
    x: 'A bid is any small reach for attention, interest or support: “look at this,” a sigh, a joke, a question about your day, a hand on the shoulder, a link sent with no comment. Most of our days are made of these tiny moments, not big talks.\nTurning Toward describes three ways people answer. Turning toward (looking up, answering) tends to say “you’re here with me.” Turning away (not noticing, staying on the phone) tends to say “I don’t matter much right now.” Turning against (“can’t you see I’m busy?”) tends to say “reaching for you costs me.”\nThe page describes researchers who followed newlywed couples for six years. The couples still together had turned toward these small moments most of the time; those who split had done so only about a third of the time.\nA small scene from the page: a Tuesday, nothing wrong. Someone on the sofa laughs and holds up their phone: “Look at this.” You could say “mm” without looking up, or lean over and laugh with them. It feels too small to matter. It isn’t.\nTry: for one evening, notice every small bid that comes your way, and turn toward just one more than you usually would.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#bids', l: 'Read about bids' },

  turning: { t: 'Turning toward', m: ['turning toward'],
    s: 'Answering someone’s small reach for connection with warmth.',
    f: 'Looking up, answering, asking one question, a small “oh nice.”',
    w: 'The page has seven everyday practices: notice bids, say thank you specifically, celebrate good news, the stress-reducing conversation, small rituals, knowing their world, and remembering what you like about them. Warmth doesn’t make an unfair split acceptable, and a fair split doesn’t make up for coldness.',
    x: 'Turning toward is the everyday side of the program. Most tools here are for when something has gone wrong. This page is about what makes those hard talks easier when they come: small moments, answered warmly, many times over.\nIt includes seven practices: notice bids and turn toward them; say thank you, specifically; celebrate good news; the stress-reducing conversation; small rituals of connection; know their world; and remember what you like about them.\nThe page is clear that none of this replaces fairness. Warmth doesn’t make an unfair split of work acceptable, and a fair split doesn’t make up for coldness. The two support each other.\nIt also works across relationships. For partners, start with bids and good news. For co-parents, keep warmth about the children and the effort. For roommates and coworkers, a short weekly “what went well.” For caregivers, thanks in both directions. An everyday example: “I want to hear this. Give me ten minutes?” then coming back, still counts as turning toward.\nTry: pick one of the seven practices that feels easiest, and do it once a day for a week.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#bids', l: 'Read Turning Toward' },

  appreciation: { t: 'Appreciation', m: ['appreciation', 'specific thanks'],
    s: 'Noticing and thanking a specific thing someone did.',
    f: 'Not “thanks for everything,” but “thank you for sorting the school forms. I know that took your lunch break.”',
    w: 'Thanks is how invisible work becomes seen. Specific thanks lands better because it proves you noticed. It’s the second line of the daily check-in, and one way a check-in closes. Thanking someone doesn’t cancel a fairness conversation; it makes it easier.',
    x: 'Appreciation is how invisible work becomes seen. The Preface starts with unseen work that never gets a thank-you; specific thanks is one of the simplest ways to change that.\nTurning Toward mentions the psychologist Sara Algoe, who describes gratitude in close relationships as working three ways: it helps you find people who are good for you, reminds you of them, and binds you closer.\nSpecific beats general, because it proves you noticed. You can thank the thing, the effort, or the quality behind it: “You’re always the one who remembers birthdays.”\nAn everyday example: instead of “thanks for everything,” someone says, “Thank you for sorting the school forms. I know that took your whole lunch break.” The other person feels seen, not just thanked. It appears across the program. It’s the second line of the daily check-in (WP-13), and a check-in closes with one specific appreciation. Thanking someone for their share of the work doesn’t cancel a fairness talk. It makes that talk easier.\nTry: tonight, thank someone for one specific thing they did today, and say what it made easier for you.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#thanks', l: 'Read about thanks' },

  goodnews: { t: 'Celebrating good news', m: ['Active and warm'],
    s: 'Meeting someone’s good news with real interest.',
    f: '“That’s amazing! What did they say? When do you start?”',
    w: 'Of four common responses, only active and warm was linked to closer relationships. Quiet and warm (“oh, nice”), active and deflating (“isn’t the commute awful?”) and quiet and deflating (changing the subject) are common and usually meant kindly. Save the practical questions for tomorrow.',
    x: 'How you respond to good news may matter as much as how you respond to bad news. Turning Toward describes four common responses, from research by Shelly Gable and colleagues.\nIt includes, for “I got the job!”: active and warm (“That’s amazing! What did they say? When do you start?”); quiet and warm (“Oh, nice,” then back to your screen); active and deflating (“Does it pay more? Isn’t the commute awful?”); and quiet and deflating (“Did you remember the milk?”).\nOnly the first was linked to closer, more satisfying relationships. The others are common and usually meant kindly, especially the practical worry. The page’s advice: save the practical questions for tomorrow. Tonight, ask one question that lets them tell the story again.\nAn everyday example: a friend texts that their sister had a baby. Instead of a thumbs-up, the reply is: “Oh wow! What’s the name? How’s everyone doing?” The friend gets to share the joy twice. The Conversation Reader watches for good news met with a flat reply, because it’s one of the most common ways people drift apart without meaning to.\nTry: the next time someone shares good news, ask one more question than you normally would.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#good-news', l: 'Read about good news' },

  stresstalk: { t: 'The stress-reducing conversation', m: ['stress-reducing conversation'],
    s: 'A short, regular talk about stress from outside the relationship.',
    f: 'Each person gets about ten to fifteen minutes. The listener is on the speaker’s side.',
    w: 'It’s not about each other. Don’t problem-solve unless asked (“ideas, or just listen?”), and show you understand first. Outside stress nobody talks about tends to leak into the relationship as irritability; this gives it somewhere to go.',
    x: 'The stress-reducing conversation is a short daily or near-daily talk about stress from outside the relationship: work, family, health, the world. Turning Toward credits it to Gottman. It is not about each other.\nIt includes a few simple rules: take turns, with about ten to fifteen minutes each; the listener is on the speaker’s side (“That sounds exhausting”), not defending the boss; don’t problem-solve unless asked (“Do you want ideas, or do you want me to just listen?”); and show you understand before anything else.\nThe reason it matters: stress from outside that nobody talks about tends to leak into the relationship as irritability. This gives it somewhere to go.\nAn everyday example: after dinner, one person spends ten minutes on a difficult manager. The other just listens and says, “No wonder you’re fed up.” Then they swap. Nobody fixes anything, and both feel lighter. It pairs naturally with the daily check-in (WP-13) and Today’s Weather. For caregivers, the page suggests it in both directions.\nTry: tonight, ask someone close to you, “Want to tell me about your day, and I’ll just listen?”',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#stress-talk', l: 'Read how it works' },

  rituals: { t: 'Rituals of connection', m: ['rituals of connection', 'small rituals'],
    s: 'Small things you do together on purpose, the same way each time.',
    f: 'A real hello and goodbye, a weekly walk, “what was the best part of your day?” at dinner.',
    w: 'They don’t depend on anyone’s mood, which is the point. Keep them small enough to survive a bad week: a ritual that only happens when things are good isn’t a ritual yet.',
    x: 'A ritual is a small thing you do together on purpose, the same way each time. Because it doesn’t depend on anyone’s mood, it keeps working through busy or tired weeks.\nTurning Toward gives examples for different relationships: how you say hello and goodbye (looking up, a real greeting, a hug that lasts a breath longer); a weekly walk, coffee or call with nothing to sort out; a question at dinner like “What was the best part of your day?”; a five-minute Friday “what went well” for roommates or coworkers; and a weekly meal where devices go in a basket for families.\nThe key is size. A ritual that only happens when things are good isn’t a ritual yet. Keep it small enough to survive a bad week.\nAn everyday example: two friends in different cities keep a standing Sunday call, just twenty minutes. Some weeks it’s light, some weeks it’s heavy. It’s there either way.\nTry: choose one tiny ritual, like a proper hello when someone comes home, and do it every day this week.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#rituals', l: 'Read about rituals' },

  lovemap: { t: 'A love map', m: ['love map'],
    s: 'A detailed picture of someone’s inner world, kept up to date.',
    f: '“What’s on your mind this week that I don’t know about?”',
    w: 'It’s Gottman’s term: what they’re worried about, looking forward to, and who matters to them right now. People change, so the map needs updating, and the listening matters more than the question.',
    x: 'A love map is Gottman’s term, described on Turning Toward, for a detailed picture of someone’s inner world: what they’re worried about, what they’re looking forward to, and who matters to them right now.\nPeople change, so the map needs updating. What someone cared about last year may not be what’s on their mind this month. The page says the listening matters more than the question.\nIt offers a list of questions to try every so often, such as: What’s on your mind this week that I don’t know about? Who at work or school do you enjoy right now? Who’s hard? What are you looking forward to this month? What’s a small thing I do that you like? What’s one thing you’re proud of lately?\nAn everyday example: over tea, one person asks, “What’s been stressing you that you haven’t mentioned?” The answer is a worry about a parent’s health they hadn’t shared. Now it can be carried together.\nTry: pick one question from the list and ask it this week, then listen without planning your reply.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#their-world', l: 'See the questions' },

  fondness: { t: 'Fondness and admiration', m: ['fondness and admiration'],
    s: 'Remembering, on purpose, what you like and respect about someone.',
    f: 'Once a week, name one quality you admire, and one moment you saw it.',
    w: 'In a hard stretch, attention narrows to what’s going wrong. This widens it again, and it guards against contempt, the most damaging conflict pattern in Gottman’s research.',
    x: 'Fondness and admiration means deliberately remembering what you like, respect and admire about someone. It’s the seventh practice on Turning Toward.\nIn a hard stretch, attention narrows to what’s going wrong. Remembering the good widens it again. It also guards against contempt, which Gottman’s research singles out as the most damaging of the conflict patterns he studied.\nIt includes three small practices from the page: once a week, name one quality you admire in them and one moment when you saw it; tell someone else something good about them, and let it get back to them; and remember how you met, or a hard time you got through together.\nAn everyday example: during a tense month, someone tells a mutual friend how patient their partner was with a tricky neighbor. The friend mentions it later. The partner hears it, and something softens.\nTry: write down one quality you admire in someone close to you, and one recent moment you saw it. Then tell them.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#fondness', l: 'Read more' },

  /* ---------- Stories and calm places ---------- */

  stories: { t: 'Stories from Philosophy', m: ['Stories from Philosophy', 'Second Arrow', 'Ship of Theseus', 'Blind Men and the Elephant', 'Empty Boat', 'Ring of Gyges'],
    s: 'Twelve old stories, retold simply, that act as honest mirrors.',
    f: 'From the Buddha and Plato to Zhuangzi and Buber, each tied to part of the program.',
    w: 'They follow the program’s path: knowing yourself, seeing the other person, living together, and the ethics of keeping a ledger at all. Among them: the Second Arrow (the story we add to what happened), the Ship of Theseus (agreements made for an older life), the Blind Men and the Elephant (each true view is only part), the Empty Boat (the intent we imagine) and the Ring of Gyges (using a ledger honestly). Ideas to think with, not proofs.',
    x: 'Stories from Philosophy retells twelve old stories and ideas from Greek, Buddhist, Chinese, Stoic, German and Jewish thought, in plain language. Each one comes with what it shows, where it lives in the program, and one small thing to try.\nIt includes four parts: knowing yourself (the Second Arrow, the Ship of Theseus, What Is Up to Us); seeing the other person (the Cave, the Blind Men and the Elephant, the Joy of Fish, the Empty Boat); living together (the Porcupines, Three Kinds of Friendship, the Circles of Hierocles); and the ethics of keeping a ledger (the Ring of Gyges, and Buber’s I and Thou).\nThat last one is a gentle guardrail for the whole site. Every tool here is a table or a score, and that’s useful. But the moment the numbers become the person, as in “you’re a 0.4,” the ledger has failed.\nAn everyday example, from the Second Arrow: something stings. Two lines get written: “What happened” and “What I’m telling myself it means.” Only the first belongs in the ledger. They’re retellings in the site’s own words, with sources listed, and ideas to think with, not proofs.\nTry: read one story tonight and do its small “Try this” step tomorrow.',
    u: '/learn/index-in-depth.html', l: 'Read the stories' },

  garden: { t: 'The Night Garden', m: ['Night Garden'],
    s: 'A quiet place to breathe, play and let your mind settle.',
    f: 'Box breathing with a glowing light, fireflies and a lily-pond puzzle.',
    w: 'No timers and no way to lose. It isn’t treatment, just a calm place to be.',
    x: 'The Night Garden is a calm corner of the site with three gentle things to do. Breathe: box breathing with a glowing light, in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4, with a flower blooming each round. Fireflies: connect stars into pictures that sing. Lily pond: a slow puzzle where lily pads bloom.\nThe page explains why these can help: slow, steady breathing settles the body; relaxed, casual play has been linked with better mood and lower stress; fitting shapes together uses the same mental room that worry loops use; and gently moving light, water and leaves lets tired attention rest.\nThe garden grows as you visit. Flowers you bloom stay, the moon follows the real one, and the seasons change with the calendar. Missing a day costs nothing, and everything is kept only on your device.\nAn everyday example: before a planned check-in, someone spends five minutes breathing in the garden. They arrive at the table calmer, and the first sentence comes out softer. It isn’t treatment and doesn’t replace talking to someone.\nTry: open the garden tonight for three rounds of breathing before bed.',
    u: '/night-garden.html', l: 'Step into the garden' },

  petals: { t: 'Pause & Play levels', m: ['every few levels', 'Pause & Play'],
    s: 'The calm games count every level you finish.',
    f: 'Every few levels, something new and calm appears in the living garden behind every page.',
    w: 'It’s a gentle nudge to take calm breaks, not a score to chase. There are no timers and no way to lose.',
    x: 'The calm games in Pause & Play, like the Night Garden, Word Bloom and the Quiet Crossword, each count the levels you finish.\nEvery few levels, and never quite the same number, something new arrives in the garden behind every page: a swing, koi, a little bridge, a blossom tree, fairy lights and more. Each one joins in with the animals already there.\nThe point isn’t to collect as much as possible. It’s to make a pause feel like something worth coming back to.\nAn everyday example: after a hard afternoon, someone plays one short Word Bloom level before starting dinner. It’s a tiny thing, and it marks a real pause in a busy day. Pause & Play suggests a few minutes of play as a lovely way to settle before a hard conversation, or to wind down at night.\nTry: next time you feel wound up, play one calm level before you reply to anything.',
    u: '/pause-and-play.html', l: 'Open Pause & Play' },

  soundscapes: { t: 'The Soundscape Catalog', m: ['Soundscape Catalog'],
    s: 'Background audio made for settling down and focusing.',
    f: 'Optional, always: no workpaper requires audio.',
    w: 'It grew from the program’s rule to regulate first and negotiate second. A practice that becomes another obligation has already stopped working, so use it only if it helps you.',
    x: 'The Soundscape Catalog holds ambient audio made for the program. It adds one real, finished piece at a time, and nothing listed is a placeholder. The first was The Breath Beneath, a slow, low-register piece just under three minutes long. One Breath to Anchor You, Nothing Needs to Change and The Road We Made have joined it since.\nThere are also live soundscapes made in your browser as you listen, so they never repeat or end: Deep, Ocean, Soft rain and Singing bowls. The page suggests headphones, since the deep tones and gentle left-to-right movement are much of what makes them calming.\nThe page is honest about what they are. They’re ambient pieces, not a validated therapeutic or clinical tool. Some people find this kind of audio calming as a personal listening habit; that’s taste, not an established treatment.\nAn everyday example: someone plays Soft rain while filling in their weekly log. It makes the task feel a little more like a quiet ritual and a little less like paperwork.\nTry: play one live soundscape for five minutes today and notice whether it helps. If it doesn’t, skip it, guilt-free.',
    u: '/soundscapes.html', l: 'Open the catalog' }
};
